//@version=5
// =====================================================================================
//  VSISA SETUPS - the three-stage pattern, drawn on the chart
//
//  Zee, 2026-09-19: "let's create a drawing on a tradingview indicator of our setups..
//  what if we make the heavy volume as the heaviest of the last"
//
//  Counted on 28 sessions of OANDA M5, the funnel runs:
//      runs of 2+ one way                  60.68 / day
//      ...heavy volume (campaign)          18.29   -70%
//      ...then an opposite bar             18.25    -0%
//      ...low volume OR wick               11.21   -39%
//      ...then continues on light volume    4.36   -61%
//
//  TWO WAYS TO CALL A CAMPAIGN HEAVY, because they are not the same test:
//    AVERAGE  - the run averages >= x the last N bars' average volume.
//    PEAK     - the run's loudest bar reaches >= x the loudest bar of the last N. This
//               is what VSISA the EA actually demands (0.70 x the swing peak).
//
//  WHICH ONE IS STRICTER DEPENDS ENTIRELY ON THE THRESHOLDS, and my note here used to say
//  flatly that AVERAGE was the loose one - written when the counting script ran AVERAGE at
//  1.15 and gave 18.29 campaigns a day. At the SHIPPED pair (AVERAGE 1.15 against PEAK
//  0.70) it is the other way round on this chart: 208 setups to PEAK's 257.
//  PEAK asks whether ONE bar in the run was loud, which a single spike satisfies. AVERAGE
//  asks whether the WHOLE run was loud, which a spike beside three quiet bars does not.
//  The second is the better description of a campaign, and the scoreboard agrees - see the
//  sweep recorded at the `method` input below.
//
//  THE WICK IS AN ALTERNATIVE, NOT AN EXTRA. A reaction qualifies on LOW VOLUME or on a
//  REJECTION WICK - measured on OANDA data, the wick nearly doubles the qualifying
//  reactions (148 -> 284), because absorption inside a loud bar is the same information
//  as a quiet bar, arriving differently.
//
//  Volume colour follows TRADINGVIEW's convention throughout: a bar is buying when
//  close > open. Never MT5's "smaller than the previous bar".
// =====================================================================================
//  SHORT NAME + NO ARGUMENT ECHO. Zee, 2026-09-21: "the name of the indicator on top left..
//  it is a longgg line showing all parameters.. can u reduce it to short name of indicator
//  only".
//  TradingView's status line prints EVERY input's value after the title, and this script
//  has about sixty of them - so the chart header became an unreadable string of numbers
//  running the full width. Two fixes, and they do different jobs:
//    shorttitle           - what the status line CALLS it ("VSISA" instead of the full name)
//    display = display.none on each input  - keeps that input OUT of the status line
//  The values are all still there in the settings dialog; they are just not shouted across
//  the top of the price.
indicator("VSISA Setups", shorttitle = "VSISA", overlay = true, max_labels_count = 500, max_boxes_count = 500, max_lines_count = 500)

// ---- inputs -------------------------------------------------------------------------
grpP = "① Timeframe preset"
//  ONE SWITCH INSTEAD OF FIVE FIELDS. Zee, 2026-09-25: "can you check which settings are
//  correct for 1 min timeframe?" then "yes make that toggle on the very top of settings".
//  Declared FIRST because Pine renders inputs in source order, so this is what opens.
//
//  EVERY THRESHOLD IN THIS FILE WAS SWEPT ON M5 AND ONLY M5. Run on M1 unchanged they
//  select almost nothing useful: 563 labels, 51.2% right, +0.082 points each - below any
//  realistic spread. M1 needs its own numbers, and the sweep found them.
//
//      setting                     M5      M1
//      campaign minimum (BUY)       2       4
//      ...for SELL                  3       5
//      reaction <= x the last bar  0.70    0.50
//      OR wick >= x of the range   0.15    0.30
//      heavy: run avg >= x         1.20    1.20   (unchanged - it swept to the same place)
//
//  M1 RESULTS with those, 19 sessions, 96 labels, 5.1 a day:
//      hold 1 bar   55.2%   +0.189      <- LOSES to a 0.30 spread
//      hold 3 bars  58.3%   +0.510
//      hold 5 bars  54.2%   +0.636
//      hold 10 bars 46.9%   +0.195      <- gone again
//
//  ⚠️ THE HOLD IS PART OF THE PRESET, AND THE SCRIPT CANNOT ENFORCE IT. On M5 the edge is
//  in the reaction bar's CLOSE and is gone one candle later - waitDir proved that and it is
//  why the scoreboard scores at hz = 1. ON M1 THAT IS REVERSED: one bar pays nothing and
//  the move needs 3 to 5 bars. Which is the same edge at finer resolution - 3-5 M1 bars is
//  about one M5 bar - not a different one. Taking M1 signals with an M5 exit habit is the
//  one way to use this preset and still lose.
//  The scoreboard follows: switching to M1 moves "Bars to look ahead when scoring" to 4.
//
//  TWO CAUTIONS, because this is a weaker foundation than the M5 numbers:
//  1. 96 labels, chosen as the best of 96 configurations swept. That is overfitting
//     territory. What argues for it is that 3-bar and 5-bar are BOTH positive - a fluke
//     usually appears at one horizon only - but treat this as a fortnight's trial.
//  2. The spread decides it. At 0.30 points the 1-bar version loses and the 3-bar version
//     pays; at 0.60 nothing on M1 works at all. Zee's own Blueberry fills average about
//     0.24 points of GROSS move per trade, so cost is the dominant term at this size.
//     CHECK THE REAL SPREAD before trusting any of it.
//
//  M5 IS STILL THE BETTER INSTRUMENT: +0.448 net per label at one bar and 60% right,
//  against +0.336 at five bars and 54%. Use M1 because the chart is M1, not by choice.
//  ⚠️ THE FOUR ⑴ FIELDS BELOW ARE IGNORED UNLESS THIS SAYS MANUAL, and that caught Zee
//  out on 2026-09-28: "why does it say campaign need 4 for a buy.. when in settings its 2".
//  He was on the M1 preset, which sets the BUY minimum to 4 and the SELL minimum to 5 -
//  so the dialog showed 2 while the chart used 4, with nothing on screen connecting the
//  two. The values were right; the dialog was silently lying about which ones were live.
//  A Pine input cannot grey itself out when another input overrides it, so the next best
//  thing is a mark: every field the preset takes over now carries ⑴, and the Why-not
//  label names the preset beside the number it refused you with.
presetTF = input.string("M5 - tuned (recommended)", "Preset  ⑴ overrides the marked fields below", options = ["M5 - tuned (recommended)", "M1 - tuned", "MANUAL - use my own values below"], group = grpP)

grpSL = "② Stop loss"
//  ⛔ THE KILL SWITCH. Zee, 2026-09-28, and this is the first thing in this file that
//  is about SURVIVING rather than about being right:
//    "on every setup label write: Stop loss: <Price 3-8 pips below the reaction candle's
//     low at end of lower wick> same for buy setup.. without stoploss the market just goes
//     haywire.. and crazy falls happen on buy trades.. since we're trading at higher lot
//     sizes and margin. this becomes a kill switch if it falls without SL placed.. we wanna
//     place notes on the chart somewhere written Caution: Place SL / Exit quick else
//     freefall risk"
//
//  WHERE THE LEVEL COMES FROM: the reaction candle's own extreme - the END OF THE WICK,
//  not the body - plus a buffer. For a BUY that is the low; for a SELL the high. The
//  reaction wick is where the other side was driven and pushed back, so price closing
//  beyond it says the absorption the whole setup rests on did not hold. That is the right
//  place for a stop by this method's own logic, not merely a convenient one.
//
//  ONE PIP = 0.10 ON GOLD (MT5 XAUUSD, two decimals: a point is 0.01, a pip is 0.10), so
//  his 3-8 pips is 0.30 to 0.80 dollars. The size is an input because that convention is
//  not universal and a broker that quotes three decimals moves it.
//
//  ⚠️ MEASURED, AND THE WIDE END IS THE ONE TO USE. Not a fill simulation - CLAUDE.md
//  names that exact failure ("asks did the low reach my stop using the very candle it is
//  trading inside"). This measures only the PRICE PATH: after a setup printed, how far
//  back past the reaction candle's own extreme did price trade?
//      M1, 101 STRONG setups        buffer never touched within
//      buffer        1 bar    3 bars   5 bars
//      3 pips (0.30)  71%      57%      45%
//      5 pips (0.50)  74%      58%      46%
//      8 pips (0.80)  79%      63%      50%
//      15 pips (1.50) 89%      76%      64%
//      M5, 141 STRONG setups: 77% / 60% / 49% at 8 pips.
//  The median dip is 0.07 points - half the time price never revisits the low at all -
//  but the 90th percentile is 2.88 and the 95th is 3.52. THE TAIL IS THE WHOLE PROBLEM,
//  and at his lot size the tail is what a stop exists for.
//  ⚠️ A FIXED PIP BUFFER IGNORES HOW BIG THE CANDLES ARE. 8 pips is 43% of a typical
//  M1 candle here (1.86 points) but only 16% of an M5 one (4.85). In a fast session the
//  same 8 pips is noise. If the stop starts getting taken for no reason, that is the
//  cause, and the repair is a buffer set as a share of the average range - say it and it
//  gets built and swept.
//  ⛔ AND NOTHING HERE SAYS THE STOP IS PROFITABLE. Being touched is not the same as the
//  setup failing, and no P&L has been computed - only MT5 or real fills may claim that.
//  What is claimed is narrow and enough: an unprotected position at this size is the one
//  risk on this chart that is not recoverable, and the label now names the level.
showSL   = input.bool(true, "Write the STOP LOSS on every setup label", group = grpSL, display = display.none)
slPipSz  = input.float(0.10, "One pip, in price (gold = 0.10)", step = 0.01, minval = 0.0001, group = grpSL, display = display.none)
slMinPip = input.float(3.0, "...this many pips past the reaction wick (tight)", step = 0.5, minval = 0, group = grpSL, display = display.none)
slMaxPip = input.float(8.0, "...and this many (wide - the measured one)", step = 0.5, minval = 0, group = grpSL, display = display.none)
slDraw   = input.bool(true, "Also draw the wide stop as a dashed line", group = grpSL, display = display.none)
//  THE STANDING REMINDER, in his own words. It is deliberately NOT tied to a setup: the
//  danger he is describing is an open position with no stop on it, which can be true at
//  any moment whether or not a label has printed recently.
slNote   = input.bool(true, "Standing CAUTION banner (top left)", group = grpSL, display = display.none)

grpC   = "Campaign"
minRunIn = input.int(2,    "⑴ Campaign: minimum bars in the run (BUY setups)", minval = 1, group = grpC, display = display.none)
//  THE TWO SIDES NEED DIFFERENT CAMPAIGNS. Zee, 2026-09-21: "we say BUY SETUP and it does
//  go upwards afterwards with maximum surity". Hunting the hit rate turned up the filter
//  this whole session has been looking for, and it is not on the reaction candle at all -
//  it is the LENGTH of the campaign behind it, and it only matters on one side:
//
//      campaign     BUY hit rate        SELL hit rate
//      2 bars       74.2%  (31)         40.0%  (35)
//      3 bars       90.0%  (10)         72.2%  (18)
//      4+ bars      65.0%  (20)         53.8%  (13)
//      3+ bars      73.3%  (30)         64.5%  (31)
//
//  A SELL AFTER A TWO-BAR RISE WINS 40% OF THE TIME. A two-bar rise is not a campaign, it
//  is a wobble, and selling into it is selling into noise. Buys are indifferent - 74.2%
//  at two bars against 73.3% at three or more - so raising the bar for BOTH sides would
//  throw away 30 good buys to fix the sells. Hence one knob per direction.
//
//      config                setups/day   win     BUY              SELL
//      minRun 2 both             6.7     62.2%    61 73.8% +2.33   66 51.5% +0.81
//      minRun 3 both             3.2     68.9%    30 73.3% +2.67   31 64.5% +1.44
//      buy 2 / sell 3            4.8     70.7%    61 73.8% +2.33   31 64.5% +1.44
//
//  VERIFIED ON THE CHART, 2026-09-21, and it did what it was supposed to:
//
//      engulf admission   STRONG        BUY              SELL             total
//      ON  (shipped)      104 67% 2.07  70 69% +2.05     34 65% +2.09     215
//      OFF                100 68% 2.08  69 68% +1.98     31 68% +2.29     208
//
//  ENGULF STAYS ON. The two columns are the same configuration twice - 67% against 68%,
//  2.07 against 2.08 - and the four setups admission adds are worth +1.83 apiece, just
//  under the average and clearly positive. Nothing there justifies reversing a setting
//  promoted on separate evidence (the orange bucket) over a four-setup difference.
//
//  AND THE SELL SIDE IS FIXED. Across the session:
//      94 sells  51%  +0.78      before any of tonight's changes
//      67 sells  54%  +1.34      after config A (1.20 / 0.70 / 0.15)
//      34 sells  65%  +2.09      after this one
//  SELLS NOW OUT-EARN BUYS - 2.09 against 2.05, 40% of a candle against 37%. The buy/sell
//  asymmetry that survived the regime test, every threshold sweep and both market legs was
//  not a property of selling at all. It was that we were selling into two-bar wobbles.
//
//  THE WHOLE NIGHT, for scale: 56% / 0.91 per setup / ~11 a day  ->  67% / 2.07 / 5.5 a day,
//  with total movement UP from 189 to 215. More money, half the setups, eleven more points
//  of hit rate. That combination is rare enough to be worth re-checking in a week rather
//  than believed on nineteen sessions.
//
//  THE REPLICATION'S SCORE on this one: predicted 70.7% overall (actual 67%), BUY 73.8%
//  (actual 69%), SELL 64.5% (actual 65%). It read the sell side almost exactly and
//  overstated the buys by five points. Directionally sound, not precise - as before.
//
//  TWO HONEST CAVEATS, because this is a 25-point claim on a small sample.
//  1. It is NOT monotone. Three bars scores 72% and four-or-more falls back to 54%. A real
//     structural effect would keep improving, or at least hold. So the DEFENSIBLE half of
//     this finding is the negative one - two-bar sells are bad, on 35 samples - and the
//     "three is the sweet spot" half is shakier. The gate is therefore 3-OR-MORE, not
//     exactly 3: it acts on the part that is well evidenced.
//  2. I REJECTED minRun 3 EARLIER IN THIS SAME SESSION - "3/4/5 gave 0.88 / 0.38 / 0.75, a
//     sawtooth, which is the signature of noise". That test ran on the OLD config (PEAK,
//     relX 0.85, wickX 0.10) and scored AVERAGE MOVE, not hit rate. It was measuring a
//     different landscape with a different yardstick. This is the second time tonight an
//     earlier verdict has been overturned by a later change (wickX 0.10 was the first),
//     and the pattern is worth naming: THESE GATES ARE NOT INDEPENDENT. A sweep is only
//     valid for the configuration it was run in, so a settled question reopens whenever
//     something upstream moves.
minRunSellIn = input.int(3, "⑴ ...and for SELL setups (a rise needs to be real)", minval = 1, group = grpC, display = display.none)
//  SHOW THEM, DON'T HIDE THEM. Zee, 2026-09-25: "the issue is on 5 mins its not labelling
//  the SELL setups on the top today.. maybe coz the volumes are all greyed out".
//  HIS DIAGNOSIS WAS 12% RIGHT AND THE REST WAS THIS GATE. Counting every bar that turned
//  against a green run on 24 Sep:
//      campaign too SHORT (2 bars, needs 3)   25   37%
//      campaign too SHORT (1 bar)             33   49%
//      campaign NOT heavy                      8   12%   <- the greyed-out one
//      passed                                  2    3%
//  minRunSell 3 gave 2 sells that day; minRunSell 2 would have given 8. On a session whose
//  tops are all two-bar rises the sell side goes silent, and that is the bill for a gate
//  shipped on 2026-09-21.
//
//  THE GATE IS STILL RIGHT. Re-measured on 19 sessions with a bigger sample than the one
//  it was promoted on (52 against 35):
//      campaign exactly 2 bars   52   42.3%   +0.03
//      campaign exactly 3 bars   30   56.7%   +1.86
//      4 bars                    16   50.0%   -0.21
//      5+                        12   58.3%   +0.04
//  Two-bar sells earn nothing. And the existing orange flag CANNOT sort them - flagged and
//  unflagged 2-bar sells score 41.7% and 42.5%, which is the same number twice.
//
//  SO: ADMIT AND DOWNGRADE, the pattern this whole file uses instead of blocking.
//      STRONG bucket   45   57.8%   +0.48    unchanged - none of these are 2-bar
//      WEAK bucket     83   39.8%   +0.31    the 2-bar sells land here
//  He sees the top, the label says WEAK in words and in colour, and the STRONG row he
//  actually trades from is untouched. Turn this off to go back to silence.
//  THE COST, stated plainly: the orange bucket nearly doubles (45 -> 83), so most labels
//  on the chart become WEAK. If that trains the eye to ignore orange, this has done more
//  harm than the blindness it fixes - and that is a judgement only screen time can make.
sellSoft = input.bool(true, "...but still SHOW shorter sell campaigns, marked WEAK", group = grpC, display = display.none)
maxRun = input.int(8,    "Campaign: most bars to count back", minval = 2, group = grpC, display = display.none)
excludeRun = input.bool(true, "Judge the campaign against the bars BEFORE it", group = grpC, display = display.none)
//  PROMOTED TO AVERAGE, 2026-09-20, on the sweep Zee ran. This had sat on PEAK since the
//  script was written, on the reasoning that PEAK is what VSISA the EA demands - which is
//  a fact about the EA, not evidence about this chart.
//
//      method / avgX    STRONG   right   avg move   TOTAL    BUY            WEAK
//      AVERAGE 1.05       256     54%      0.71     181.8    126 @ 1.16     78 @ 0.30
//      AVERAGE 1.10       233     55%      0.80     186.4    114 @ 1.32     66 @ 0.36
//      AVERAGE 1.15       208     56%      0.91     189.3    103 @ 1.37     60 @ 0.41
//      AVERAGE 1.25       165     56%      0.93     153.5     76 @ 1.51     41 @ 0.03
//      PEAK    0.70       257     52%      0.67     172.2    125 @ 0.85     94 @ 0.01
//
//  MONOTONE IN EVERY COLUMN as avgX tightens - quality up, buy quality up, counts down.
//  No sawtooth, which is what separates this from the minRun 3/4/5 sweep that was rejected
//  as noise. 1.15 is where the MARGINAL setup stops paying:
//      1.25 -> 1.15   adds 43 setups worth +0.83 each   take them
//      1.15 -> 1.10   adds 25 setups worth -0.12 each   refuse them
//      1.15 -> 1.05   adds 48 setups worth -0.16 each   refuse them
//  The 1.10 row was run because of ONE BAR. Zee, 2026-09-20, on 18 Sep 8:25 AM: campaign
//  111% against the 115% it needed - refused by FOUR POINTS, with the reaction quiet at
//  74%, a 28% wick and capping present. A knife-edge like that deserves a check rather
//  than a shrug, and the check said the threshold is right: bars in the 110-115 band are
//  worth -0.12 apiece. Not harmful - that is indistinguishable from zero - but they pay
//  nothing and they pull the average from 0.91 down to 0.80.
//  NOTE WHAT THAT DOES AND DOES NOT SAY. It is a claim about the GROUP. That particular
//  bar may well have been a good trade; a group average cannot speak to one member of it.
//  What it rules out is moving the threshold to admit its whole cohort.
//  Against PEAK it is +17 points of total movement on 49 FEWER setups - the 49 PEAK admits
//  are worth -0.35 apiece, so they do not dilute the average, they subtract from it.
//
//  TWO THINGS THAT ARGUE THE OTHER WAY, recorded so they are not lost:
//  1. The WEAK bucket discriminates WORSE here. Under PEAK the orange setups average 0.01 -
//     they go nowhere, exactly what a stand-aside flag should predict. At AVERAGE 1.15 they
//     still carry 0.41 against 0.91, so the flag is working but far less sharply.
//  2. THE ENTIRE GAIN IS ON THE BUY SIDE. Sells are unchanged (0.45 against PEAK's 0.50);
//     buys go 0.85 -> 1.37.
//
//  THE REGIME TEST, run 2026-09-20 - and it began by correcting my own premise. I had
//  written here, and said repeatedly, that these 19 sessions were a RISING market. They
//  are not: the span closes 4655 -> 4378, net -277. The rise was early August, before the
//  chart window. The caveat I kept attaching to every result was factually wrong.
//  Split across the two legs in the OANDA table (UP 4198->4659, DOWN 4661->4264):
//
//      regime   method        BUY              SELL             STRONG   total
//      UP       AVERAGE 1.15  40 65% +1.80     34 44% -0.24     74 0.86   +63.9
//      UP       PEAK 0.70     59 59% +0.75     45 56% +0.49    104 0.64   +66.3
//      DOWN     AVERAGE 1.15  75 60% +0.95     85 51% +0.14    160 0.52   +82.8
//      DOWN     PEAK 0.70     92 54% +0.31    102 46% +0.09    194 0.19   +37.6
//
//  AVERAGE IS NEVER WORSE and is far better in the down leg, so the promotion above is not
//  an artefact of the window. And avgX peaks at 1.15 in BOTH regimes -
//      UP    36.9 / 63.9 / 41.3 / 24.6      at 1.05 / 1.15 / 1.25 / 1.60
//      DOWN  67.3 / 82.8 / 71.7 / -7.3
//  - the same ridge with the same peak in two opposite markets.
//
//  WHAT THE TEST ACTUALLY FOUND, which is not what it set out to look for: the buy/sell
//  gap is NOT trend drift. Buys beat sells in BOTH directions (+1.80 vs -0.24 going up,
//  +0.95 vs +0.14 going down). Were it drift, sells would pay in the falling leg; they do
//  not. So the asymmetry belongs to the method on M5 gold, and THE SELL SIDE EARNS ALMOST
//  NOTHING IN ANY REGIME MEASURED. That is a larger open question than any threshold on
//  this page and nothing here addresses it.
//
//  PROVENANCE: the regime split is a PYTHON REPLICATION of this script, not the script -
//  it reproduces 193 STRONG against the Pine's 208 and 240 against 257 (~93%), win rates
//  within 2 points. Sound for comparing regimes to each other, NOT quotable as absolute
//  numbers, and not a backtest under CLAUDE.md - direction only, no entry, stop or spread.
//  One correction inside it: the UP-leg WEAK bucket read -7.10 on the strength of a single
//  corrupt bar (2026.08.07 04:20, -131 points in five minutes). Honest figure -1.18.
//  A THIRD OPTION, 2026-09-27, and it came out of the wrong question.
//  Zee: "on Friday evening the last setup was at 9:30 PM .. and until 2 am the volumes
//  indicator shows all volumes as light grey.. at 10:10 pm a reaction candle appears at a
//  lower volume than previous 4 green bars which were at a rising green volume.. it says
//  campaign not heavy" (its tooltip: run avg 74% of the 20-bar average, needs 120%).
//  THE DEAD WINDOW IS NOT WHAT GOT FIXED - see the note at avgX for why nothing could.
//  But sweeping six yardsticks to chase it turned up one that beats the shipped mean on
//  quality, for a reason that has nothing to do with quiet sessions:
//      yardstick                  STRONG  right   per label   total
//      MEAN   x1.20 (shipped)       140   65.7%   +1.228      +172.0
//      MEDIAN x1.20                 159   63.5%   +0.915      +145.5
//      MEDIAN x1.40                 123   68.3%   +1.317      +162.0
//      MEDIAN x1.60                 102   71.6%   +1.397      +142.5
//  WHY A MEDIAN IS THE BETTER YARDSTICK: a MEAN is dragged up by one enormous bar, so
//  after a spike the whole neighbourhood is judged against a number no ordinary run can
//  reach - and dragged the same way DOWN is impossible, since volume has a floor at zero
//  and no ceiling. The median ignores the spike and describes the typical bar. Win rate
//  and per-label both climb monotonically as it tightens, which is the ridge shape this
//  file trusts.
//  ⚠️ NOT PROMOTED, AND THE DEFAULT IS UNCHANGED. MEDIAN 1.40 wins on per-label (+1.317
//  against +1.228) and on hit rate (68.3% against 65.7%) but LOSES on total movement
//  (+162.0 against +172.0) because it takes a setup a day fewer. That is a 6% gap on ~130
//  setups - noise - so there is no evidence-based winner, only a different trade-off:
//  fewer, better signals. It is offered because this file's doctrine says per-setup
//  quality governs what a human picks off a chart, and the scoreboard on the real chart
//  is the arbiter. Give it a fortnight before believing either.
method = input.string("AVERAGE of last N", "Heavy volume measured against", options = ["AVERAGE of last N", "MEDIAN of last N (ignores one spike)", "PEAK (like the EA)"], group = grpC, display = display.none)
lookN  = input.int(20,   "N bars for that yardstick", minval = 5, group = grpC, display = display.none)
//  1.15 -> 1.20, 2026-09-21, verified on the chart as part of config A below.
//
//  ⛔ THE DEAD WINDOW CANNOT BE FIXED, AND THAT IS THE FINDING - 2026-09-27, after the
//  trailing-average staleness was finally swept properly. Zee: "on Friday evening the
//  last setup was at 9:30 PM .. and until 2 am the volumes indicator shows all volumes as
//  light grey.. at 10:10 pm a reaction candle appears at a lower volume than previous 4
//  green bars which were at a rising green volume.. it says campaign not heavy".
//  HE IS DESCRIBING A REAL MECHANISM. In a decaying session the 20-bar mean sits ABOVE
//  the present, so no run can clear 1.20x it - already recorded below as 43.5% heavy when
//  the average is rising against 9.3% when it is falling. That gap is structural.
//  SIX YARDSTICKS WERE TESTED to close it, scored on 8,674 M5 bars. The last column is
//  the 21:00-02:00 window he is asking about:
//      yardstick                   STRONG  right   per label   total    in the dead hours
//      MEAN20 x1.20 (shipped)        140   65.7%   +1.228      +172.0   13   54%  -0.16
//      MEAN10 x1.00 (faster window)  188   60.1%   +0.636      +119.6   24   50%  -0.97
//      MEAN10 x1.20                  127   64.6%   +0.999      +126.9   17   59%  +0.02
//      MEDIAN x1.40                  123   68.3%   +1.317      +162.0   14   50%  -0.40
//      HOUR   x1.00 (diurnal)        165   58.8%   +0.414       +68.2   30   53%  -0.92
//      HOUR   x1.20 (diurnal)        120   58.3%   +0.381       +45.7   25   60%  -0.11
//      PCTL   top 25% of last 60     110   70.9%   +1.243      +136.7    6   -
//      RISING volume through the run  71   54.9%   -0.177       -12.5   12   58%  +0.07
//
//  1. HIS OWN DESCRIPTION MEASURES WORST OF ALL. "Four green bars at rising green volume"
//     as the definition of a campaign gives 71 setups that LOSE MONEY - -0.177 apiece,
//     total -12.5, the only negative row in any sweep in this file. A run whose volume is
//     merely climbing is not a campaign; it is a rally being chased.
//  2. THE DIURNAL FIX WORKS AND IS NOT WORTH IT. Judging a run against the typical volume
//     AT THAT HOUR is the principled repair, and it does what it promises - the dead
//     window goes from 13 setups to 30. They are junk: 53% and -0.92 apiece, and the
//     whole configuration collapses to +0.414 per label from +1.228.
//  3. AND THAT IS THE ANSWER. Across six yardsticks and fifteen configurations the
//     21:00-02:00 setups are a coin or worse in EVERY ONE - 50% to 62%, -0.97 to +0.57,
//     never better than the daytime rows beside them. THE GATE IS NOT BLIND IN THE QUIET
//     HOURS; THE QUIET HOURS DO NOT PRODUCE TRADEABLE SETUPS. Every method that sees more
//     of them sees worse ones, which is the signature of an empty region rather than a
//     hidden one. The silence after 9:30 PM is the script protecting him, not failing.
//  WHAT CAME OUT OF IT ANYWAY: the MEDIAN yardstick, now offered on the `method` dropdown
//  above - better per-setup than the mean for a reason unrelated to quiet sessions.
//  ⚠️ PROVENANCE: a PYTHON REPLICATION over M1 aggregated to M5. NOT a backtest under
//  CLAUDE.md, absolute figures not quotable; every variant ran on identical bars and
//  differs in one term, so only the COMPARISON is claimed. And the aggregation does not
//  reproduce TradingView's own M5 bars, so no claim is made about the 10:10 PM candle he
//  is looking at - only about the population.
avgX   = input.float(1.20, "AVERAGE method: run avg >= x average", step = 0.05, group = grpC, display = display.none)
//  1.40, not 1.20 - a median sits BELOW a mean in this data (volume is right-skewed), so
//  the same multiplier would be a looser gate. 1.40 is where the sweep put it.
medX   = input.float(1.40, "MEDIAN method: run avg >= x median", step = 0.05, group = grpC, display = display.none)
pkX    = input.float(0.70, "PEAK method: run's loudest >= x peak",  step = 0.05, group = grpC, display = display.none)

grpT   = "Test of a level"
//  THE SECOND AND THIRD TOP. Zee, 2026-09-21: "on a price making 3 tops like an MM.. the
//  first top would have high volume campaign followed by low volume reaction, the second
//  and third top can have LOWER volume campaign and then lower volume reaction.. since the
//  volumes are exhausted in first two tops.. sometimes we don't have enough transactions
//  to support a high volume campaign.. so this is never considered a setup".
//
//  He is describing the VSA TEST, and he is right that this script reads it backwards.
//  Our heavy-volume gate asks "was this run loud in absolute terms?" - so the second and
//  third visit to a level get REFUSED for being quiet, which is exactly when they carry
//  the most information. Price returning to a level on LESS effort than last time is the
//  supply being gone; that decline IS the signal, not a disqualification.
//
//  MEASURED on 232 tops and 219 bottoms over 19 sessions:
//      TOPS      retest, campaign volume LOWER than last visit   36   86.1%  +5.29
//                retest, campaign volume HIGHER                  43   72.1%  +4.82
//      BOTTOMS   retest, LOWER                                   41   78.0%  +5.54
//                retest, HIGHER                                  38   71.1%  +3.36
//  Both sides agree and the gap is 14 points on tops, 7 on bottoms.
//
//  IT IS NOT THE COUNT OF TOPS. 1st / 2nd / 3rd / 4th+ touch score 83 / 79 / 82 / 84 on
//  tops - flat. So "three tops" is not the mechanism; the VOLUME DECLINE against the
//  previous visit is, and that is a cleaner rule than counting peaks.
//
//  AND THE GATE IS REFUSING THEM: retests whose campaign ran UNDER 1.20x the 20-bar
//  average number 131 on the top side and score 82.4%. Every one of those is invisible
//  to the script today.
//
//  THE NUMBERS ABOVE ARE INFLATED AND NOT TRADEABLE. A pivot is confirmed with pivRight
//  bars of hindsight, so the forward move overlaps bars already known to be lower. The
//  COMPARISON survives that - every group carries the same bias - but the absolute hit
//  rates do not. That is exactly why this ships OFF: the scoreboard has no lookahead, so
//  turning it on and watching the table is the honest test.
//  ON by default from 2026-09-21 at Zee's call, and the setups it admits are NAMED on the
//  headline ("BUY SETUP - RETEST") rather than blending in. That matters because the route
//  is still unproven on his chart: a setup that only exists because of it should be
//  identifiable at a glance, so it can be judged on its own and switched off if it is
//  admitting junk. The scoreboard is the arbiter, and it has no lookahead.
useTest   = input.bool(true, "Admit a RETEST of a level on lower volume", group = grpT, display = display.none)
testTolX  = input.float(1.00, "Same level = within x the average range", step = 0.25, group = grpT, display = display.none)
testVolX  = input.float(1.00, "...and this campaign <= x the last visit's volume", step = 0.05, group = grpT, display = display.none)

grpR   = "Reaction"
//  WHAT IS "LOW VOLUME" LOW AGAINST? Zee, 2026-09-19, after a setup he would have taken
//  went unmarked: "i think we compare the reaction candle's volume to the LAST CANDLE of
//  the campaign (the candle before the reaction candle is the last candle of the
//  campaign)". His law says only "then we have a low volume bullish candle" (line 223)
//  and leaves the yardstick open, so this was my choice, not his - and mine was wrong for
//  the bar he was looking at. The 18 Sep 12:50 reaction came in at 2,313 against a
//  campaign AVERAGING 2,162 (107% - failed) but against its LAST bar of 3,280 it is 71%,
//  which passes comfortably. His eye was comparing it to the big red right before it.
quietRef = input.string("LAST bar of the campaign", "Low volume measured against", options = ["LAST bar of the campaign", "campaign AVERAGE", "campaign PEAK"], group = grpR, display = display.none)
//  PROMOTED 0.85 -> 0.75, 2026-09-20, and this one matters more than the rest.
//  0.85 had never been measured. It is a number I chose when the script was written, and
//  it survived a whole night of tuning around it because nobody thought to sweep the gate
//  that admits the most setups. Zee asked for it last. It turns out to sit in a TROUGH:
//
//      relX   STRONG  right  avg move   TOTAL    BUY             SELL            WEAK
//      0.50    150     59%     1.28     192.0    72 67% 1.81     78 51% 0.80     26 -0.76
//      0.65    165     59%     1.37     226.1    79 66% 1.82     86 53% 0.95     40 -0.39
//      0.75    181     58%     1.28     231.7    87 66% 1.81     94 51% 0.78     48  0.26
//      0.85    208     56%     0.91     189.3   103 63% 1.37    105 50% 0.45     60  0.41
//      0.95    231     58%     0.85     196.4   115 64% 1.25    116 51% 0.46     69  0.56
//
//  The 27 setups 0.85 admits over 0.75 are worth -1.57 EACH - the most destructive band
//  found in any sweep this session. Going the other way, 0.65 -> 0.75 adds 16 setups worth
//  +0.35 each, so 0.75 is where the marginal setup stops paying.
//
//  WHY THIS ONE IS DIFFERENT FROM THE avgX SWEEP. There, only the buy side improved, and
//  a change that helps one direction is the shape a trend artefact takes. Here BOTH sides
//  improve: buys 1.37 -> 1.81, and sells 0.45 -> 0.78, reaching 0.95 at 0.65. The sell
//  side has earned essentially nothing in every regime and at every other setting measured
//  tonight - tightening THIS gate is the first thing that has moved it. That is a finding
//  about the method, not about a threshold: what makes a reaction tradeable is how far the
//  volume COLLAPSED, and 0.85 was barely asking for a collapse at all.
//  The WEAK flag sharpens with it - 0.41 -> 0.26 -> -0.39 - so the orange setups start
//  predicting what a stand-aside flag is supposed to predict.
//
//  0.65 IS A DEFENSIBLE ALTERNATIVE and the case for it is not weak: within 2.4% of 0.75
//  on total (226 against 232, which is noise on 170 setups), better per setup (1.37), the
//  best sell side measured anywhere (0.95), and the only setting where the orange bucket
//  goes negative. It costs 16 setups. If the sell side is what needs fixing, take 0.65.
//  CAUTION: the curve is not smooth. 0.75 -> 0.85 is -1.57 a setup and 0.85 -> 0.95 is
//  +0.31, a dip and a recovery that no story explains - some of that is a handful of bars.
//  What IS consistent across all five points, and across both directions, is that 0.65-0.75
//  beats 0.85-0.95. The band is trustworthy; the exact peak inside it is not.
//  ...AND THEN TO 0.65, once the table split the winners from the losers. With only a
//  blended average to look at, 0.75 won on total movement and that settled it. The split
//  shows the two settings are a TIE on money - 231.7 against 226.1 is 2.4% on ~170 setups,
//  which is noise - and that the real difference is elsewhere:
//
//      relX   SELL              WEAK (orange)      per label
//      0.65   86 53% +0.95      40 43% -0.39       1.37  (26% of a candle)
//      0.75   94 51% +0.78      48 50% +0.26       1.28  (24% of a candle)
//
//  THE ORANGE FLAG IS THE DECIDER. At 0.75 a WEAK setup still nets +0.26 - the warning is
//  steering you away from something that mildly pays, which is how a flag becomes a thing
//  you learn to ignore. At 0.65 they run 43% and -0.39: they actually lose. A caution that
//  is right is worth more than six points of total movement, because it is acted on every
//  session, and the whole orange system exists to be trusted or removed.
//  The sell side agrees - 53% / 0.95, the best measured anywhere in any sweep or regime.
//  The cost is 16 setups (8.7 a day instead of 9.5), and those 16 were worth +0.35 apiece
//  against a 1.37 average: they were paying, but they were the worst of the bunch.
//  0.65 -> 0.70, 2026-09-21, as part of config A - see the wickX note below for the
//  whole result. Loosening this ALONE was worse; loosening it alongside a stricter wick is
//  better, because the two gates admit overlapping populations and only move together.
relXIn = input.float(0.70, "⑴ Low volume: reaction <= x that reference", step = 0.05, group = grpR, display = display.none)
//  Zee, 2026-09-19: "if volume of reaction candle is considerably shorter than the
//  campaign's last candle's volume we can say on the label this too: strong imbalance".
//  That is the ideal case in his own notes, line 98: "ideal case: the reaction candle
//  would've been LOW VOLUME while coming down -> means IMBALANCE of demand supply -> means
//  demand has reduced -> LIQUIDITY PROVIDERS HAVE REMOVED ORDERS". Merely clearing 0.85
//  is qualifying; collapsing to half is the other side walking away.
imbX   = input.float(0.55, "STRONG imbalance: reaction <= x that reference", step = 0.05, group = grpR, display = display.none)
//  HIS NUMBER, NOT MINE. Zee, 2026-09-19: "any existance of lower wick greater than 10% is
//  enough to classify it". I applied that to the GRADING test (hasWick, which decides WEAK)
//  and left ADMISSION at 0.25 on my own reasoning that admission "needs to be convincing" -
//  reasoning that appears nowhere in LAWS_VSISA.md. Line 219 makes a binary call: "a wick
//  below means AGGRESSIVE BUYING happened".
//  The 18 Sep 7:00 PM bar is what settled it: campaign 2 bars at 100% of the 20-bar volume
//  high, capping present in the run, reaction wick 18% - refused by 7 points, and by a
//  threshold I invented. Lowered to his figure, so both tests now ask the same question.
//  This does NOT open the floodgates: a loud reaction still needs cappedRun as well
//  (loudOK = wicked and cappedRun), so capping remains the real gate.
//  0.10 -> 0.15, 2026-09-21, AND IT OVERTURNS AN EARLIER DECISION ON THIS SAME PAGE.
//  0.10 was promoted from a sweep run while the script was still on PEAK with relX at 0.85.
//  Both of those changed afterwards, so that sweep was measuring a landscape that no longer
//  exists. A 216-config grid put 0.15 on top of EVERY ranking - expectancy, total movement
//  and hit rate alike, which those three rarely agree on.
//  Verified on Zee's chart, 19 sessions, against the then-shipped 1.15 / 0.65 / 0.10:
//
//                        setups  right   BUY            SELL           WEAK    per label
//      1.15/0.65/0.10      165    59%    79 66% 1.82    86 53% 0.95    -0.39     1.37
//      1.20/0.70/0.15      131    60%    64 67% 1.96    67 54% 1.34    -0.01     1.64
//
//  THE SELL SIDE IS THE POINT: 0.95 -> 1.34, a 41% lift, and sells now pay +4.91 when they
//  work - the largest figure measured in any sweep or regime this session. The sell half
//  has earned close to nothing all along, and this is the first configuration that moves it
//  properly. Buys improve too (1.82 -> 1.96), and a change that lifts BOTH directions is
//  the shape of a real gain rather than a trend artefact.
//
//  WHAT IT COSTS, stated plainly: 1.8 setups a day (8.7 -> 6.9) and 5% of total movement
//  (226 -> 215). Total movement was the right yardstick for comparing GATES, because it
//  assumes every setup is taken. It is the wrong one for choosing what a human trades off a
//  chart - Zee picks a few signals a session, so per-setup quality governs, and that is 20%
//  better here (1.37 -> 1.64).
//
//  THE REPLICATION'S SCORE, recorded so its limits are known: the Python grid predicted the
//  per-label direction correctly (1.15 -> 1.54 predicted, 1.37 -> 1.64 actual) and the hit
//  rate correctly, but got TOTAL MOVEMENT backwards - it had config A ahead on total, and on
//  the real chart it is behind. Rankings partly transfer; absolute totals do not. Shortlist
//  with it, never conclude with it.
wickXIn = input.float(0.15, "⑴ OR rejection wick >= x of the bar range", step = 0.05, group = grpR, display = display.none)
aggWickX = input.float(0.10, "Call it aggressive buying/selling past x", step = 0.05, group = grpR, display = display.none)
//  LAWS_VSISA.md line 125: "it broke below the main bullish candle (ENGULFED THE BODY of
//  previous candle)". Zee, 2026-09-19: "we can also check if the candle body envelopes
//  (engulfs) the last two campaign candle's bodies, we can add to the label Stronger:
//  engulfs last 2". The reaction taking back two or three whole bodies in one bar is the
//  campaign being undone faster than it was built.
showEngulf = input.bool(true, "Count campaign bodies the reaction engulfs", group = grpR, display = display.none)
//  A THIRD WAY IN? Zee, 2026-09-20, on the 18 Sep 4:15 PM bar - loud, no wick, but capped:
//  "reaction is neither wicked nor loud. but it engulfs the past candle beautifully. can
//  that help it qualify as a setup?"
//  The law gives this PARTIAL support. Line 125 lists engulfing as evidence of aggressive
//  selling - "top wick on reaction candle + STRONG BEARISH CLOSING + it broke below the
//  main bullish candle (ENGULFED THE BODY of previous candle)" - but always ALONGSIDE the
//  wick, never instead of it. Line 156's full definition is the same: "supply has hit +
//  has a wick + closing strong bearish".
//  And there is a conceptual objection: VSISA is an ABSORPTION method. Quiet means the
//  other side walked away; a wick means absorption happened inside the bar. A loud bar
//  that engulfs and closes strong is neither - it is MOMENTUM, and admitting it quietly
//  turns this into a breakout tool.
//  Built OFF, then measured and turned ON - see the sweep below. The capping requirement
//  still applies, exactly as it does for the wick route, so this widens one gate rather
//  than removing it, and the momentum objection above is handled by demanding THREE bodies
//  rather than one: that is no longer a bar that merely closed past the previous candle.
//  ANY OF THEM, NOT THE LAST N IN A ROW. Zee, 2026-09-20: "can that be set to like: if the
//  reaction candle engulfs ANY of the 2 candles within the campaign.. then we allow it to
//  declare a setup".
//  The decorative count (engulfN, further down) runs CONSECUTIVELY from the nearest
//  campaign bar and stops at the first body it does not cover - right for the "Stronger:
//  engulfs last 3" line, because that claims the three immediately behind it. For
//  ADMISSION it is the wrong shape: a reaction can swallow the big second-last bar while
//  missing a small one sitting right in front of it, and consecutively that scores zero.
//  So admission scans the first engulfScanN campaign candles and counts how many are
//  covered, wherever they sit in the run.
//  PROMOTED 2026-09-20, on the sweep Zee ran once the counting was fixed. Under the old
//  CONSECUTIVE test this route was dilutive twice and stayed off; under ANY-of-the-last-N
//  it earns its place, and the deciding evidence was not the one I first argued.
//
//      scan/admit   STRONG   avg move   total    WEAK avg
//      off            251      0.66      166       0.03
//      3 / 2          266      0.65      173       0.12
//      3 / 3          257      0.67      172       0.01
//      4 / 4          254      0.69      175       -
//
//  The STRONG money is the same at 2 or 3 (172.2 vs 172.9). What separates them is the
//  ORANGE bucket: at admit 3 the WEAK setups average 0.01 - they go nowhere at all, which
//  is precisely what a stand-aside flag should predict - while at admit 2 they still carry
//  0.12. Zee spotted that; my own case had rested on six setups' marginal value.
//  Demanding THREE whole campaign bodies also matches line 125's use of engulfing as
//  evidence rather than as something that happens most bars.
//  CAUTION, recorded so it is not forgotten: the 0.12 -> 0.01 gap is three setups changing
//  buckets across 19 sessions. It is the best-supported setting available, not a law.
admitEngulf  = input.bool(true,  "...and let ENGULFING admit a loud reaction", group = grpR, display = display.none)
engulfScanN  = input.int(3, "Engulf: scan this many campaign candles", minval = 1, group = grpR, display = display.none)
engulfAdmitN = input.int(3, "...how many of them it must engulf (ANY)", minval = 1, group = grpR, display = display.none)
useWick= input.bool(true,  "Let a wick qualify a LOUD reaction", group = grpR, display = display.none)
//  SHOW THEM, DON'T HIDE THEM - the second time this file has taken that route, and for
//  the same reason as sellSoft. Zee, 2026-09-27, on a loud wicked reaction refused for
//  having no capping behind it: "i think we're missing on too many setups.. yes show
//  these as WEAK".
//  THE MEASUREMENT SAYS THEY ARE NOT TRADEABLE and that is exactly why they are ADMITTED
//  AND DOWNGRADED rather than promoted - see the five-way test at loudOK below. Taking
//  them as ordinary setups costs a third of the total movement. Marking them lets the
//  top be visible without lying about it.
//  WHAT IT DOES TO THE TABLE, measured on the same 8,674 M5 bars:
//      bucket                    before              after
//      STRONG                    140  65.7%  +1.228  140  65.7%  +1.228   <- UNTOUCHED
//      WEAK (orange)             174  44.3%  +0.378  431  ~50%   +0.069
//  The STRONG row he actually trades from does not move by one setup. The orange bucket
//  more than doubles and its expectancy falls to about zero - which IMPROVES the flag:
//  this file has complained since 2026-09-20 that a caution steering you away from
//  something that mildly pays (+0.378) is one you learn to ignore. At +0.069 "orange
//  means there is nothing here" is finally true.
//  ⚠️ THE COST IS 5 MORE LABELS A DAY, ALL ORANGE, on a chart that already carries a lot.
//  If the orange stops being read, this has done more harm than the blindness it fixes -
//  the same judgement sellSoft carries, and only screen time settles it. Turn it off to
//  go back to silence.
loudSoft = input.bool(true, "...but still SHOW an uncapped loud reaction, marked WEAK", group = grpR, display = display.none)

grpE   = "End of rising / falling market"
//  LAWS_VSISA.md line 249-250: "End of rising market: large blue bar with SMALL volume,
//  means there's an imbalance, supply is less, so small volume moved large spread.. then
//  SMALL blue bar even though HIGH volume.. in a bullish trend.. means supply hit.. and
//  its reaction bearish.. that I call end of rising market.. sell setup"
//  Line 258: "end of rising scenario.. as small blue candle has a large volume"
//
//  WHY IT MATTERS, line 111-113 - it is a QUALIFIER, not decoration:
//    "IF no small candle big volume previously AND not wick on top of the red reaction
//     candle -> WE WOULD NOT SELL IT"
//    "but because small blue candle has big volume (end of rising market type) + next bar
//     with wick on top -> this big red volume is of AGGRESSIVE SELLING and not of demand"
//  So it does the same job as the wick: it is what makes a LOUD reaction tradeable.
//  Without either, the law stands down. Mirrored as END OF FALLING for the buy side.
showEOR  = input.bool(true,  "Mark end of rising / falling market", group = grpE, display = display.none)
eorSpread= input.float(0.70, "Its candle's spread <= x average range", step = 0.05, group = grpE, display = display.none)
eorVol   = input.float(1.15, "...while its volume >= x average volume", step = 0.05, group = grpE, display = display.none)
eorPrev  = input.float(1.10, "The bar before it: spread >= x average", step = 0.05, group = grpE, display = display.none)
eorLook  = input.int(3,      "Look back this many bars from the reaction", minval = 1, group = grpE, display = display.none)
//  Zee, 2026-09-20: "if campaign's last candle is very small BODIED, and has a very large
//  volume, and its reaction (next bar) is bearish.. label it end of rising market.. sell
//  setup". That is line 250 word for word: "if choti c candle pe bohot bara volume ayay,
//  means supply hit, aur usska reaction bearish ho, next bar down jaey, ussko END OF
//  RISING MARKET bolta hu.. SELL SETUP".
//  The sequence only completes when the NEXT bar turns - so this is checked at the
//  reaction, not at the capped bar, and it renames the whole setup rather than adding a
//  footnote. Body is measured as well as spread because he said "bodied": a pin bar can
//  have a wide range and still be a tiny body, and that is capping too.
eorBody  = input.float(0.35, "...or its BODY <= x average range", step = 0.05, group = grpE, display = display.none)
//  SMALL COMPARED TO WHAT? Zee, 2026-09-20, on the 16 Sep 11:45 PM sell that the script
//  refused and that made +27 on the very next bar: "the 11:40 PM candle can be classified
//  as end of rising market? because its a small candle with big volume.. this would make
//  the 11:45 candle's capped to true right".
//  By the 20-bar average it is not small at all - range 1.80x, body 0.70x - so capping was
//  false. But inside its own run the ranges were COLLAPSING on sustained double volume:
//      21:30  range 42.98   vol 2.12x
//      21:35  range 29.75   vol 2.14x
//      21:40  range 26.05   vol 1.78x
//  which is effort without result, exactly what capping is meant to catch. The 20-bar
//  average was computed before the volatility burst, so every bar in the burst looks large
//  against it and nothing can ever register as capped.
//  This is the SAME correction he already made me apply to volume - "we compare the
//  reaction candle's volume to the LAST CANDLE of the campaign" - now on the spread axis.
//  CAMPAIGN mode measures each bar against the widest range in the run itself, so a
//  contracting candle inside a loud run registers even when the whole run is wide.
//  Left on AVERAGE by default: this has not been measured, and the scoreboard is there to
//  decide it rather than my judgement.
cappedRef = input.string("20-bar AVERAGE", "Capping: small measured against", options = ["20-bar AVERAGE", "the CAMPAIGN's own candles"], group = grpE, display = display.none)
cappedRelX = input.float(0.70, "CAMPAIGN mode: bar <= x the run's widest", step = 0.05, group = grpE, display = display.none)

grpL   = "Last campaign candle"
//  LAWS_VSISA.md line 279: "if there was a WICK BELOW the red candle, then the bigger
//  volume would mean its DEMAND". And line 35: "if our red volume candles are breaking
//  down below the support line WITH A WICK and closing above the support line, its a
//  strong sign."
//  So the campaign's own last candle can give the tell BEFORE the reaction prints: a red
//  bar pushed back off its low means buyers were already arriving inside it. Mirrored for
//  a buying campaign - an upper wick on its last blue candle means sellers were there.
showLast = input.bool(true,  "Label a wick on the campaign's last candle", group = grpL, display = display.none)
lastWick = input.float(0.25, "That wick >= x of its range", step = 0.05, group = grpL, display = display.none)
//  THE SAME TELL, REPEATED. Zee, 2026-09-20, on a SELL SETUP that failed on the very next
//  candle: "the 3 previous candles before this reaction candle have a large lower wick..
//  suggesting that during this campaign there's some buyer's pushing the price up (after
//  coming down)".
//  This is line 279 widened from one candle to the run, and it is a different claim from
//  the reaction-wick rule that was tried and reverted on this same date. One wick on one
//  bar is a moment, and nearly every bar has one; THREE bars in a row bought off their
//  lows is the market saying the same thing three times - the sellers cannot close on the
//  low. Heavy volume makes that worse, not better: effort without result, which is the
//  measurement the capping rule already runs on a single bar.
//  It also arrives EARLIER than any reading of the reaction candle - it is visible while
//  the campaign is still printing.
//  NOT MEASURED YET. This marks the condition on the chart so it can be judged by eye; it
//  does not gate the setup, and no count of how these behave has been run.
showCampWick = input.bool(true,  "Caution when the campaign keeps wicking", group = grpL, display = display.none)
campWickN    = input.int(3,      "How many of its last candles to check", minval = 2, group = grpL, display = display.none)
campWickX    = input.float(0.15, "Each of those wicks >= x of its range", step = 0.05, group = grpL, display = display.none)
//  AND THE SAME CANDLES READ THE OTHER WAY. Zee, 2026-09-20: "if a red campaign's last 3
//  candles have a lower wick beneath them, then the buy setup that follows can have a star
//  of strength on its hover label that tells us ok this is additional confirmation".
//  This is the EXACT MIRROR of the caution above, on the same bars, and the two do not
//  conflict - a candle has two ends. After a red campaign:
//      UPPER wicks on those bars  ->  sellers still arriving   ->  CAUTION, orange
//      LOWER wicks on those bars  ->  buyers already arriving  ->  STRENGTH, the star
//  Both can be true at once, on candles with wicks at both ends, and that combination is
//  itself worth seeing: the campaign was being fought from both sides.
//  It is also lastHas widened from one candle to three - line 279's "wick below the red
//  candle means DEMAND" said about a run rather than a single bar, which is the same
//  upgrade the caution flag got.
//  IT NEEDED ITS OWN BAR COUNT. Zee, 2026-09-20: "i think the 3 ticks never appear much.
//  could it be that we can set it to 2 instead of 3.. because its not much use if its not
//  there". Measured on 1,776 campaigns across 28 sessions, and the reason is not the wicks
//  at all - it is CAMPAIGN LENGTH:
//      2 bars  864  48.6%      <- can never show a 3-tick star, before any wick is measured
//      3 bars  426  24.0%
//      4+      486  27.4%
//  and how many carry the favouring wick on every one of the last N:
//      N=2   701 campaigns  39.5%
//      N=3   248            14.0%
//      N=4    84             4.7%
//  Nearly half of all campaigns are two bars long, so at N=3 the star is structurally
//  impossible on most of them.
//  SPLIT FROM THE CAUTION rather than moving the shared knob, because they are not
//  symmetric in consequence: the caution turns a setup ORANGE, and loosening it to 2 would
//  recreate exactly the problem the reverted reaction-wick rule caused - a flag so common
//  it stops being read. The star only ADDS a line, so it can afford to be common.
//  Caution stays at 3. Strength goes to 2.
showCampStrong = input.bool(true, "...and star the setup when they wick the OTHER way", group = grpL, display = display.none)
campStrongN    = input.int(2, "...how many candles for THAT test", minval = 1, group = grpL, display = display.none)

grpND  = "No supply / no demand"
//  Zee, 2026-09-20, quoting the classic VSA pair and asking for them on the campaign bars:
//    NO SUPPLY  - "a small bearish candle with a narrow range and trading volume lower than
//                 the previous two bars.. selling pressure is very low because sellers are
//                 not interested in pushing the price down further"
//    NO DEMAND  - "a small bullish candle with a narrow range, an upper wick or rejection,
//                 and volume lower than the previous two bars.. buying pressure is very
//                 weak because buyers refuse to chase higher prices"
//
//  WHERE THEY ARE MARKED, and why it differs slightly from his framing. He described them
//  in trend-continuation terms - a no-supply dip inside a healthy uptrend. Inside THIS
//  script's structure the useful place is INSIDE THE CAMPAIGN: a small narrow low-volume
//  red bar in the middle of a selling run says the sellers driving that run have stopped
//  pressing, which is the same reading the reaction is about to confirm. So no supply is
//  marked in RED campaigns (it supports the coming BUY) and no demand in GREEN ones (it
//  supports the coming SELL).
//  This is the LOW-VOLUME cousin of PRICE CAPPED. Capping is a small bar on BIG volume -
//  effort meeting resistance. These are small bars on SMALL volume - no effort at all.
//  Opposite causes, same conclusion: the campaign has run out of conviction.
//  They only DESCRIBE. Nothing here gates or grades a setup; no count of how they behave
//  has been run, so they stay out of the admission logic until the scoreboard says
//  otherwise.
showND   = input.bool(true,  "Mark no supply / no demand inside campaigns", group = grpND, display = display.none)
ndRangeX = input.float(0.70, "Its range <= x the average range", step = 0.05, group = grpND, display = display.none)
//  MEASURED AT LAST, 2026-09-24, and our version was the dead one.
//  These two labels have sat on the chart since the day they were built, never checked.
//  Tested over 4,922 bars against a 50.1% base rate for "the next bar closed lower":
//
//      NO DEMAND  ours (up + narrow + UPPER WICK + vol<prev2)    190   51.6%   +1.5   dead
//      NO DEMAND  with CLOSE IN THE LOWER HALF instead            66   57.6%   +7.5   works
//      NO SUPPLY  ours (dn + narrow + vol<prev2)                 352   51.4%   +1.4   nothing
//      NO SUPPLY  with close in the upper half                     74   48.6%   -1.4   nothing
//
//  So the CLOSE POSITION is what carries no-demand, not the wick - a narrow up-bar that
//  cannot hold its own high is the thing that means something, and "has an upper wick" was
//  a weaker way of asking the same question. Switched.
//  NO SUPPLY DOES NOT PREDICT either way, on 352 samples or on 74. It stays on the chart
//  as DESCRIPTION - it names a condition the eye can use - but it makes no direction claim
//  and never will unless a much larger sample says otherwise.
ndCloseX = input.float(0.50, "No demand: closes in the lower x of its range", step = 0.05, group = grpND, display = display.none)
ndWickX  = input.float(0.25, "No demand: upper wick >= x of its range (unused by default)", step = 0.05, group = grpND, display = display.none)
ndUseClose = input.bool(true, "Use the CLOSE test rather than the wick test", group = grpND, display = display.none)
ndHeavy  = input.bool(false, "Only inside a HEAVY campaign", group = grpND, display = display.none)

grpA   = "Anomaly"
//  Zee, 2026-09-20: "a candle with unusually large volume but as compared to before, the
//  volume failed to move the candle spread. so for example if previously 500 volume
//  resulted in 700 body height, and now 50,000 volume 10x makes around the same 500 body
//  height. this is an anomally."
//  This is EFFICIENCY - distance travelled per unit of volume - and it is a different
//  measurement from everything else in this script. PRICE CAPPED asks "is this bar small
//  while its volume is big?", comparing each quantity to its own average separately. This
//  asks "how far does volume MOVE this market, and did that just collapse?" - one number
//  against its own recent history.
//  A bar can fail this test while passing the capping one and the other way round: a big
//  bar on enormous volume is not capped (it is not small) but can still be a glaring
//  anomaly if the same volume used to move it three times as far.
//  SPREAD rather than BODY by default, even though his example uses body height. Body goes
//  to zero on a doji, and dividing by volume then makes every doji an infinite anomaly -
//  the degenerate case swamps the real ones. Spread is also what VSA means by the word.
//  The BODY option is there because it is what he described, and on a chart with few dojis
//  it is the more literal reading.
showAnom = input.bool(true, "Mark ANOMALY bars - big volume, no movement", group = grpA, display = display.none)
anomUse  = input.string("SPREAD (high-low)", "Measure the movement as", options = ["SPREAD (high-low)", "BODY (open-close)"], group = grpA, display = display.none)
//  CALIBRATED TO THIS INSTRUMENT, not to the illustration. Zee's example was 500 volume
//  moving 700 points, then 50,000 volume moving 500 - a hundredfold jump in effort. On
//  7,247 bars of OANDA M5 gold that case simply does not occur: NOTHING reaches 3x average
//  volume with a compressed range. Volume here is spread across thousands of participants
//  around the clock, so 2x is already a big bar; the 10x world is a stock or a futures
//  contract where one institutional order can dwarf a quiet bar.
//  HITS PER DAY over those 28 sessions:
//                eff<=40%  <=50%   <=60%   <=70%   <=80%
//      vol>=1.20x    1.75    5.86   12.64   22.11   31.61
//      vol>=1.30x    1.61    5.21   11.14   19.04   26.82
//      vol>=1.50x    1.18    3.93    8.00   13.50   18.14
//      vol>=1.75x    0.82    2.71    5.18    8.39   11.21
//      vol>=2.00x    0.61    1.89    3.29    5.07    6.96
//
//  READ THAT WITH THE ARITHMETIC, or the loose end of the table will mislead:
//      efficiency % = range ratio / volume ratio
//  so the pair of thresholds is really a statement about the bar's RANGE -
//      vol 2.00x at eff 80%  ->  range may be up to 1.60x average
//      vol 1.50x at eff 50%  ->  range must be at or under 0.75x average
//  Past about 60% efficiency the label stops meaning anything: it starts marking LARGE
//  active candles as "volume failed to move it", which is the opposite of the idea.
//  Set at 1.50 / 0.50 - just under 4 a day, and it keeps both halves of the concept
//  intact: volume half again as heavy as normal, range a quarter BELOW normal. Zee,
//  2026-09-20: "i think we need more anomalies.. to detect imbalances. currently they're
//  too rare" - 0.29/day at the first setting was indeed near-invisible.
anomVolX = input.float(1.50, "Its volume >= x the average volume", step = 0.25, group = grpA, display = display.none)
anomEffX = input.float(0.50, "...but it moved <= x as far per unit of volume", step = 0.05, group = grpA, display = display.none)
//  the SQUAT cut - an anomaly whose range is also absolutely narrow. See the note beside
//  anomSquat below for why this grades the label rather than adding one.
anomSquatX = input.float(0.80, "SQUAT: and its range <= x the average range", step = 0.05, group = grpA, display = display.none)

grpF   = "Support / fake break"
//  LAWS_VSISA.md line 35: "if we draw a line called SUPPORT below the LAST LOW before the
//  reaction candle, then if our red volume candles are BREAKING DOWN BELOW the support
//  line WITH A WICK and CLOSING ABOVE the support line, its a strong sign."
//  Line 234: "2 bar setup + FAKE BREAK of this support + forms a wick + closing again
//  inside support (STRONGEST)". Line 243: "previous low (support) is broken by a pinbar
//  (wick).. strong move because fake break of support happened with a wick."
//  The support is the last swing low before the campaign - taken here as the most recent
//  confirmed pivot, which is what "the last low point" means on a chart.
showSup   = input.bool(true, "Draw support / resistance and mark fake breaks", group = grpF, display = display.none)
pivLeft   = input.int(3, "Bars each side that define the pivot", minval = 1, group = grpF, display = display.none)
pivRight  = input.int(3, "...bars to the right (the confirmation lag)", minval = 1, group = grpF, display = display.none)

grpS   = "Strong closing"
//  LAWS_VSISA.md line 277: "the red candle's closing is near the low (NEAR ZERO LOWER
//  WICK)". Line 125: "top wick on reaction candle + STRONG BEARISH CLOSING (on the low the
//  closing of candle happened) + it broke below the main bullish candle.. so this could be
//  taken as AGGRESSIVE SELLING instead of demand".
//  And the full definition, line 156: "aggressive selling: supply has hit + has a wick +
//  closing strong bearish".
//
//  Note it is the OPPOSITE wick from the rejection one. A sell wants a wick ON TOP
//  (sellers hit it) AND almost nothing below (they held it down into the close). The two
//  together are what separate aggressive selling from demand.
//  A FAT BODY IS MOMENTUM, NOT ABSORPTION. Zee, 2026-09-24, passing on a third-party
//  note about candle geometry: "maybe it can help us add some conviction to the VSISA
//  setups we label". Four ideas came with it; this is the one that measured.
//
//      reaction body / range      n    win%    vs baseline (67.0%)
//      under 30%                 40   72.5%       +5.5
//      30 - 60%                  27   70.4%       +3.3
//      over 60%                  24   54.2%      -12.9
//
//  Monotone across the whole range, an 18-point spread end to end, and the mechanism is
//  one this file already argued: VSISA is an ABSORPTION method. A reaction that is mostly
//  BODY is a bar that went somewhere and stayed there - momentum. A reaction that is
//  mostly WICK is a bar that was driven and pushed back - which is the thing the method
//  is actually looking for. The engulf-admission note above raised exactly this objection
//  on 2026-09-20 with no evidence behind it; now there is some.
//
//  IT MARKS, IT DOES NOT BLOCK. Turning the setup orange says "the law would stand down
//  here" - same treatment as a missing wick - and the trader still sees it. Blocking would
//  remove 24 setups that still win 54% of the time.
//
//  WHAT ELSE CAME IN THAT NOTE, and what happened to it:
//    CLOSE POSITION (close in the top quartile)  - DEAD. 66.7 / 67.1 / 66.7 across top
//      quartile, top half, bottom half. Flat. This is the SECOND time this idea has failed
//      here; Zee asked the same question about closing wicks on 2026-09-22 and it measured
//      neutral then too.
//    WICK >= 2x BODY  - 73.3% against 62.5%, real but NOT independent: a small body IS a
//      large wick-to-body ratio. Same finding, different arithmetic. Not shipped twice.
//    SPREAD COMPRESSION  - narrow bars score 75% but on 12 samples; the "low volume, wide
//      spread" warning case is 64.3%, only 2.7 below baseline. Directionally right, too
//      thin to act on. Left for when the sample grows.
showFatBody = input.bool(true, "WEAK when the reaction is mostly BODY (momentum)", group = grpS, display = display.none)
//  0.60 -> 0.65 at Zee's call, 2026-09-24. The measurement above was bucketed, not swept:
//  it says the under-30 / 30-60 / over-60 bands differ, not that 0.60 is the exact edge.
//  Nudging it up makes the flag RARER, which is the safe direction for a caution - a
//  warning that fires often stops being read, and that is the failure the reverted
//  reaction-wick rule already demonstrated on this chart.
//  NOT SWEPT. If the orange bucket's hit rate drifts up towards the strong rows after
//  this, the threshold is now too loose and should come back to 0.60.
fatBodyX    = input.float(0.65, "...its body >= x of its own range", step = 0.05, group = grpS, display = display.none)
showClose  = input.bool(true, "Mark strong bearish / bullish closings", group = grpS, display = display.none)
closeMax   = input.float(0.12, "Closing wick <= x of the bar range", step = 0.02, group = grpS, display = display.none)

grpM   = "Dollar score"
//  Zee, 2026-09-20: "if bearish reaction bar is engulfing + closing on the low + very low
//  volume.. we can make a $ sign on top of the reaction candle for each of these
//  conditions.. additionally we can check if the size of the reaction candle is more than
//  twice the previous candle.. so the more the $ symbols on the SELL label.. the better".
//  A SCORE, which is a different instrument from the notes around it. Every other line in
//  the tooltip argues one point; this collapses four independent confirmations into one
//  symbol you read without thinking. $$$$ and $ are different trades at a glance.
//  The four are deliberately about DIFFERENT things, so they do not simply agree with each
//  other:
//      ENGULFS        - the reaction took back a whole campaign body (price)
//      CLOSES STRONG  - and held it into the close     (price)
//      VERY QUIET     - on collapsed volume            (volume)
//      TWICE THE SIZE - and it is a big bar for this stretch  (range)
//  Reusing measurements already computed for other purposes, so the score costs nothing
//  and can never disagree with the lines it summarises.
//  NOT MEASURED. Nobody has counted whether $$$$ behaves better than $ - that needs the
//  scoreboard split by score, which is the obvious next test once these have been seen.
showDollars = input.bool(true, "Score the setup in $ - one per confirmation", group = grpM, display = display.none)
dollarSizeX = input.float(2.0, "A $ if the reaction is >= x the last campaign bar's range", step = 0.25, group = grpM, display = display.none)
//  Zee, 2026-09-20: "one dollar for strong imbalance if reaction candle has less than half
//  the last campaign candle". Its OWN number, not the 0.55 the STRONG IMBALANCE line uses -
//  that threshold was set for a tooltip sentence, and a score should not quietly inherit a
//  number chosen for something else. Half is his, and it is stricter.
dollarVolX  = input.float(0.50, "A $ if the reaction volume <= x the last campaign bar", step = 0.05, group = grpM, display = display.none)

grpX   = "Confirmation"
//  OFF by default, 2026-09-19. The confirmation bar costs 61% of the setups - 11.21
//  reactions a day become 4.36 - and nobody has measured whether the ones it throws away
//  would have worked. Zee chose to see them all and judge himself, which is the same
//  division of labour as the meter: the machine finds them, he decides.
needCont = input.bool(false, "Require the next bar to continue on light volume", group = grpX, display = display.none)
//  A PATIENT CONFIRMATION. Zee, 2026-09-20: "we declare a setup only if the reaction to the
//  reaction candle is also in the same direction.. if its not.. we wait until candle is in
//  the same direction of reaction candle. and then enter."
//  This is NOT the rule above. needCont demands the VERY NEXT bar continue, on light
//  volume, and throws the setup away otherwise - it costs 61% of them. This one throws
//  nothing away on the first bar: it arms, then waits for the first candle that closes in
//  the reaction's direction, however many bars that takes, and fires there.
//  The label is built at the REACTION and held, then drawn on the confirming bar with a
//  line saying how long it waited - so the reasoning still describes the bar that produced
//  it, while the signal lands where a trade could actually be taken.
//  A cap is needed or a setup could sit armed for an hour and fire into a different market.
//  A new qualifying reaction always replaces whatever was pending.
//  MEASURED AND REJECTED, 2026-09-20. The mechanism works as described - it confirms on
//  the very next bar when that bar continues, and only WAITS when the next bar goes against
//  the reaction. What kills it is that the ENTRY moves to the confirming bar:
//      needCont  waitDir  cap   STRONG  right  avg move
//      off       off       -      257    52%     0.67
//      off       ON        6      256    51%    -0.02
//  Same 256 setups against 257 - essentially the identical population, only the bar you
//  enter on changed - and the move per setup goes from +0.67 to -0.02. Buys 0.85 -> 0.12,
//  sells 0.50 -> -0.13. Every waitDir row landed within noise of zero (0.02, -0.02, -0.04).
//  THE EDGE LIVES IN THE REACTION BAR'S CLOSE AND IS GONE ONE CANDLE LATER. That is not a
//  threshold needing tuning; it is a mechanism that does not work, and it is also why
//  scoring on hz = 1 was the right call.
waitDir  = input.bool(false, "Wait for a candle in the reaction's direction", group = grpX, display = display.none)
waitMax  = input.int(6, "...give up after this many bars", minval = 1, group = grpX, display = display.none)
//  THE SAME INFORMATION, USED THE RIGHT WAY ROUND. Zee, 2026-09-20: "we can use it as an
//  added confirmation to the already existing 0.85 move setups.. by labelling the next
//  candle if it continues in the same direction as the reaction candle. we can tell the
//  trader hey! move confirmed".
//  The entry stays at the reaction, where the edge is. The continuation becomes a note on
//  the FOLLOWING bar saying the move is working - useful for deciding whether to hold or
//  add, which is a different decision from whether to enter.
//  Where the 64% came from: needCont's setups are exactly this subset - reaction plus a
//  continuing bar on light volume - and on the buy side they scored 50 labels at 64% and
//  0.93 against the baseline's 125 at 56% and 0.85. As a GATE that costs 60% of the
//  setups and most of the total movement; as a NOTE it costs nothing.
showConfirm = input.bool(true, "Label the next bar when the move continues", group = grpX, display = display.none)
cfMax       = input.int(3, "...look this many bars for that continuation", minval = 1, group = grpX, display = display.none)
//  AND THE OTHER HALF OF THE SAME COIN. Zee, 2026-09-20: "if after a reaction we put a
//  setup label, and then the next 2 candles are not following the setup's predicted
//  direction. we put a red cross X on top of the two candles.. and finally a setup invalid
//  label on the 2nd candle AFTER the reaction candle. telling us ok this setup was
//  announced but failed."
//  The labels already say when a setup WORKS; this makes them say when one did not, on the
//  chart, at the time - which is what stops a method being remembered more kindly than it
//  behaved. Nothing is gated: the trade was taken at the reaction and this is the verdict.
//  Because any bar that DOES go the setup's way confirms and clears the pending read, the
//  misses counted here are necessarily consecutive - two in a row, starting immediately
//  after the reaction.
showInvalid = input.bool(true, "Cross out setups whose first bars all go the wrong way", group = grpX, display = display.none)
invalidN    = input.int(2, "...how many bars must fail before calling it dead", minval = 1, group = grpX, display = display.none)
//  THE BEST VERSION OF A CONFIRMATION. Zee, 2026-09-20, with a drawing of a hammer: "if on
//  next candle after the setup's reaction candle, we find a very small bodied bullish green
//  candle with a wick downwards AND low volume.. we can label this candle as strong signal!
//  with a golden star".
//  All three parts have to be there and each says something different:
//    tiny BODY          - the bar went nowhere in the end
//    long WICK below    - but it was driven a long way down first, and bought back
//    LOW volume         - and it took almost no selling to drive it there
//  Together: the sellers who were capable of pushing price are gone, and the little that
//  remains is being absorbed. That is the reaction's message repeated one bar later in a
//  different form, which is why it outranks a plain green close.
//  It is an UPGRADE of the MOVE CONFIRMED label, not a second one - for a BUY this candle
//  closes up, so it is already a confirmation; the star just says which kind. Mirrored for
//  a SELL: small-bodied red candle with the wick ABOVE.
//  NOT MEASURED. No count of how these behave has been run, so it changes nothing but the
//  wording and the colour.
//  THE MOVE BREATHING OUT. Zee, 2026-09-20: "if after reaction candle, the volumes start
//  decreasing while going up.. on the next candles.. can you mark a tick above those
//  candles and a high label saying imbalance shifted to buying".
//  Price advancing on FALLING volume is the cleanest statement that nobody is pushing back:
//  each bar buys higher and needs less doing it. In this script's own vocabulary it is the
//  campaign's mirror - a campaign is heavy volume driving price, this is price drifting on
//  nothing, which is what is left once one side has walked away.
//  A tick on every bar that qualifies, the headline once the run reaches shiftN, and the
//  sequence dies the moment a bar either turns or comes in heavier than the one before it.
//  NOT MEASURED. Descriptive only, like no supply / no demand.
showShift = input.bool(true, "Tick the bars that advance on falling volume", group = grpX, display = display.none)
shiftN    = input.int(2, "...say IMBALANCE SHIFTED after this many", minval = 1, group = grpX, display = display.none)
showStar  = input.bool(true, "Star the confirming bar when it is a hammer on low volume", group = grpX, display = display.none)
starBodyX = input.float(0.25, "...its body <= x of its own range", step = 0.05, group = grpX, display = display.none)
starWickX = input.float(0.50, "...its wick >= x of its own range", step = 0.05, group = grpX, display = display.none)
starVolX  = input.float(0.85, "...its volume <= x the campaign average", step = 0.05, group = grpX, display = display.none)

grpH   = "Direction hints"
//  FAINT MARKS ON ANY CANDLE. Zee, 2026-09-24: "separate transparency labels to hover over
//  candles to tell us if market is going up or down.. direction guides not necessarily on
//  the reaction candle but before after it.. like hints.. we already have no-supply /
//  no-demand labels".
//
//  A DIFFERENT QUESTION from everything else in this file. Not "is this a setup" but "does
//  THIS candle, alone, lean up or down". So it was measured over EVERY bar - 4,922 of them
//  across 19 sessions - against the base rate rather than against a setup population.
//  BASE RATE: the next bar closed lower 50.1% of the time. A clean coin, which makes the
//  lift column honest.
//
//  AND ALMOST NOTHING PREDICTS. Every candle-shape idea landed inside +/-3 points:
//      big upper wick >= 50%     758   47.9%   -2.2
//      big lower wick >= 50%     780   52.2%   +2.1
//      tiny body (doji-ish)      979   48.9%   -1.1
//      fat GREEN body            723   52.1%   +2.1
//      heavy volume + wide bar   322   48.8%   -1.3
//      at the 20-bar HIGH        529   52.4%   +2.3
//  Those are samples of 300-1000. A real effect would have shown. They are noise, and
//  drawing eight arrows that mean nothing would make the chart worse, not better - the
//  same failure as the reverted reaction-wick flag, one layer up.
//
//  ONE THING EARNED A MARK, and it is not a candle shape at all - it is WHERE the bar sits:
//      at the 20-bar LOW (pos <= 0.10)   578   42.7% fell   -7.3    leans UP
//      fat RED body                      781   46.0% fell   -4.1    leans UP
//  The position finding is the strongest single reading in the study, and it is ASYMMETRIC:
//  being at the low leans up, being at the high does nothing (+2.3). Lows bounce; highs do
//  not reverse. Combining position with the red body made it WEAKER (-5.6), so position is
//  doing all the work and the body adds nothing.
//
//  IT AGREES WITH THE ANOMALY LABEL, and sharpens it:
//      ordinary bar at the 20-bar low    42.7% fell   - bounces
//      ANOMALY  bar at the 20-bar low    62.5% fell   - keeps falling
//  A twenty-point split. The anomaly is exactly what separates a low that bounces from one
//  that breaks, which is why this hint stands down whenever an anomaly is on the same bar -
//  the two would otherwise point opposite ways on the same candle.
//
//  DRAWN WITH plotchar, not label.new, so 30-odd marks a day cost nothing from the 500-label
//  budget the setup labels need.
showHints = input.bool(true, "Faint UP hint when a bar sits at the 20-bar low", group = grpH, display = display.none)
hintPosX  = input.float(0.10, "...at or below x of the 20-bar range", step = 0.05, group = grpH, display = display.none)
hintFade  = input.int(55, "How faint (0 solid, 90 nearly invisible)", minval = 0, maxval = 95, group = grpH, display = display.none)

grpCo = "Gearing up / down"
//  THE ONE IDEA IN THE CHART-READING NOTE THAT WAS NEW, AND IT MEASURED.
//  Zee, 2026-09-24, passing on a note about context: structure / liquidity sweep /
//  break-and-retest / inside-bar contraction. Three of those four had already been
//  measured on this chart and settled. The fourth had not.
//
//  THE PATTERN: a MOTHER BAR, then a bar that sits entirely inside its high and low -
//  volatility contracting, the coil - then a bar that CLOSES outside the mother bar's
//  range on ordinary-or-better volume. Measured over 4,925 bars, 19 sessions, against
//  a 50.1% base rate for the next close:
//
//      volume     coil then break UP      control: break UP, no coil
//      0.85x      59   61.0%  (+11.1)     820   47.9%  (-2.0)
//      1.00x      47   63.8%  (+13.9)     631   50.1%  (+0.1)
//      1.25x      31   58.1%  ( +8.1)     364   47.5%  (-2.4)
//
//      volume     coil then break DOWN    control: break DOWN, no coil
//      0.85x      67   61.2%  (+11.1)     866   48.7%  (-1.3)
//      1.00x      49   63.3%  (+13.2)     629   48.2%  (-1.9)
//      1.25x      29   65.5%  (+15.5)     377   49.1%  (-1.0)
//
//  THE CONTROL IS THE WHOLE ARGUMENT. A bar that closes past a recent extreme on good
//  volume is just a momentum candle, and this file has already established that momentum
//  candles predict nothing here - 1,450 of them, dead flat, exactly as the fat-body
//  finding said. The SAME bar preceded by a coil is +11 to +14. The coil is doing the
//  work, not the breakout.
//  And it holds in BOTH directions, at EVERY volume threshold, on the 1-bar and the
//  3-bar horizon. That is a ridge. The upthrust, spring and stopping-volume tests
//  rejected earlier this week were spikes that inverted between neighbouring settings.
//
//  WHAT IT IS NOT. It is not a VSISA setup - there is no campaign, no reaction, no
//  absorption. It is a DIRECTION HINT, in the same family as the 20-bar-low arrow, and
//  it gates nothing.
//  SMALL SAMPLE: ~50 a side over 19 sessions, about five a day. The direction is solid;
//  the size of the lift is not yet.
showCoil  = input.bool(true, "Mark GEARING - a break out of a tightening coil", group = grpCo, display = display.none)
coilVolX  = input.float(1.00, "...its volume >= x the average volume", step = 0.05, group = grpCo, display = display.none)

grpEx = "Extended run"
//  DON'T CHASE. Zee, 2026-09-25: "if we have 5+ same colored candles consecutively can we
//  label to say: wait for a retracement to enter.. its a reminder during critical times
//  that we wanna enter at a low.. a retracement in an uptrend when the retracement ends
//  (a low) then we enter".
//  A DISCIPLINE REMINDER RATHER THAN A PREDICTION - but it still makes a claim that can be
//  checked, namely that entering HERE is worse than waiting. Both halves measured over 19
//  sessions, entering WITH the run at the extended bar's close:
//
//      run length      n      1-bar         3-bar         6-bar
//      1 - 4         4613   49.4% -0.05   51.5% +0.04   50.9% +0.10
//      5 or more      299   48.2% -0.12   46.5% -0.44   47.2% -0.89
//        green 5+     143   46.9% -0.08   49.7% -0.01   42.7% -1.00
//        red   5+     156   49.4% -0.15   43.6% -0.84   51.3% -0.79
//
//  Ordinary runs are flat to mildly positive; past five bars chasing costs about a point
//  by the sixth candle. The damage GROWS with the horizon, which is the shape of a real
//  effect - a coin would stay near zero at every distance.
//
//  AND THE WAITING HALF, which is the part a reminder actually rests on:
//      a pullback bar arrived within 10 candles    299 of 299 times
//      waiting for it beat chasing                 183 times (61%)
//  The retracement always comes. Entering at it was better nearly two times in three.
//
//  FIRES ONCE, at exactly the fifth bar. Every bar of a seven-bar run qualifies, which
//  would be 15.7 marks a day saying the same thing - the staircase failure the ▲ hint had
//  on 2026-09-24. At exactly five it is ~8 a day and each one marks a new event.
//  IT IS A REMINDER, NOT A SIGNAL. It says nothing about direction and gates nothing: the
//  1-bar figures above are a coin either way. What it says is "not here, wait for the low".
showExt  = input.bool(true, "Remind me not to chase an extended run", group = grpEx, display = display.none)
//  CEILING, stated because a silent one would be a bug: runNow is counted with the same
//  maxRun window as the campaign (8 by default), so setting this ABOVE maxRun would make
//  the label quietly never fire. Raise "Campaign: most bars to count back" first.
extN     = input.int(5, "...after this many same-coloured candles (<= maxRun)", minval = 3, group = grpEx, display = display.none)

grpTr = "Trend brush"
//  THE INK-BRUSH TREND LINE, brought over from turtle.pine. Zee, 2026-09-25: "we wrote a
//  turtle indicator earlier.. some months ago.. it has a beautifully drawn trendline. can
//  u bring that trend line to our indicator here. its a line that looks like shades of
//  colors and splatters of ink drawing it".
//  HOW THE LOOK IS MADE, because it is not a gradient and not a drawing: it is ONE EMA
//  plotted SIX times. A faint base line always shows, then five more layers at widths
//  6 / 8 / 10 / 12 / 14, each switched on only inside its own band of trend strength, with
//  transparency stepping 80 -> 60 -> 40 -> 20 -> 0. Because only one band is live at a
//  time and the bands change bar to bar, the stroke swells and fades along its length and
//  breaks into blobs where strength jumps - the splattered-ink effect. plot.style_linebr
//  is what allows the gaps; a normal plot would bridge them into one fat sausage.
//
//  THE STRENGTH SCORE, ported unchanged from turtle.pine lines 394-466, is five yes/no
//  questions worth 30 / 25 / 15 / 10 / 10:
//      30  price is breaking out, or trending with volume/volatility behind it
//      25  volume AND range are both above their own averages
//      15  the EMA is actually moving (slope > 0.2 ATR)
//      10  the 4-hour EMAs agree with the direction
//      10  we are NOT inside a dead range
//  Green when the read is bullish, red when bearish, grey when neither.
//
//  ⚠️ IT GATES NOTHING, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE - it is the one piece of this chart that
//  LOOKS authoritative and is not. Trend context has now been measured here four separate
//  ways and failed every time: a higher-timeframe filter (4 of 5 definitions said
//  counter-trend was BETTER), bullish structure as a direction hint (48.6% - a coin),
//  HH+HL on a retracement entry (45.7%, WORSE than no filter), and "above the 20-bar mean"
//  (46.7%). A thick confident green brush is exactly the sort of thing that makes a trader
//  skip a good counter-trend SELL SETUP. Read it for orientation; do not let it veto a
//  label that carries its own numbers.
//
//  ONE HONEST CAVEAT: the 4-hour component uses request.security, so on the CURRENT bar it
//  can shift while that 4-hour candle is still forming. Nothing else in this file does
//  that. It touches only the brush's colour and thickness - no setup, no score, no alert
//  reads it - but the line you see now on the live bar may not be the line you see once
//  the 4-hour bar closes.
//  "IT SHIFTS COLOURS LATE". Zee, 2026-09-25: "can u adjust its settings to match trend on
//  1 min and 5 min timeframes". Measured over 33 sessions, timing each colour flip against
//  confirmed 5-bar swing turns - and the obvious fix is the wrong one:
//
//      M5 rule                    flips/day   lag bars   lag mins
//      34/89 cross (was shipped)      2.4        8.9        44
//      21/55 cross                    4.7       11.2        56
//      13/34 cross                    7.5       12.0        60    <- FASTER EMAs, LATER
//      21/55 slope of the fast EMA   33.1        5.6        28
//      13/34 slope                   42.0        3.9        19
//
//  1. SHORTENING THE EMAs MAKES IT LATER. A fast pair has usually already flipped INTO the
//     pre-turn direction by the time the swing arrives, so it has further to travel back.
//     13/34 turns 16 minutes later than 34/89 and flips three times as often.
//  2. THE COLOUR RULE IS WHERE THE LAG LIVES. Reading the fast EMA's SLOPE instead of its
//     cross cuts M5 lag from 44 minutes to 19 - but at 42 flips a day on M5 and 213 on M1,
//     which is not a trend line any more, it is a flicker.
//  So both are offered and neither is hidden: STEADY is the default because a trend line
//  that changes its mind 42 times a session cannot be glanced at, which is the only job
//  this line has. Switch to RESPONSIVE if you would rather be early and filter the noise
//  by eye.
//  AND IT PREDICTS NOTHING AT ANY SETTING: "when green, higher 10 bars later" measured
//  46.6% to 48.6% across every pair tested, on ~4,000 samples each. That is below a coin
//  at every speed. Tuning this is a LEGIBILITY exercise, not an accuracy one.
showTrend = input.bool(true, "Draw the trend brush (EMA, thickness = strength)", group = grpTr, display = display.none)
//  RESPONSIVE BY DEFAULT at Zee's call, 2026-09-25 - he read the table and picked the
//  early one. Recorded so the trade-off he accepted is not lost: ~19 minutes of lag
//  instead of 44, bought with 42 colour flips a day on M5 (213 on M1) instead of 2.4.
//  The brush will now change its mind often; that is the setting working as measured, not
//  a fault. STEADY is one dropdown away if the flicker becomes tiring.
trColMode = input.string("RESPONSIVE - EMA slope", "How the colour decides", options = ["STEADY - EMA cross", "RESPONSIVE - EMA slope"], group = grpTr, display = display.none)
//  SAME TREND ON M1 AND M5. His actual request: the line should describe the same stretch
//  of MARKET on both charts. An EMA of 34 bars is 170 minutes on M5 and 34 on M1, so the
//  same numbers draw two different lines - which is exactly why the M1 chart disagreed
//  with the M5 one. With this on, the lengths are set in MINUTES and converted to bars for
//  whatever chart it is on, so M1 and M5 show the same trend read at different resolution.
trAuto    = input.bool(true, "Keep the same trend on every timeframe", group = grpTr, display = display.none)
trFastMin = input.int(170, "...fast EMA length in MINUTES", minval = 5, group = grpTr, display = display.none)
trSlowMin = input.int(445, "...slow EMA length in MINUTES", minval = 10, group = grpTr, display = display.none)
trFastIn  = input.int(34, "Fast EMA in BARS (when the above is off)", minval = 2, group = grpTr, display = display.none)
trSlowIn  = input.int(89, "Slow EMA in BARS", minval = 3, group = grpTr, display = display.none)
trHTF     = input.string("240", "Higher timeframe it must agree with", group = grpTr, display = display.none)

grpCm = "Camel humps"
//  THE COMPASS, ON THE CHART. Zee, 2026-09-25: "can you use the camel humps (make them
//  accurate) to identify trend and print a green / red arrow on bottom right of screen..
//  sometimes i'm zoomin into each candle and trading and so i forget we were in which
//  trend".
//  "ACCURATE" MEANS MATCHING THE COMPASS WE ALREADY HAVE, not inventing a new rule. This
//  is BTCUSD/trend_eyes.py auto_call() ported bar for bar - the same engine that drives
//  camel_humps.png and the cockpit:
//      * fractal swings, K bars each side (PIVOT_K = 2)
//      * least-squares slope through the last THREE confirmed swing HIGHS - Zee,
//        2026-08-04: "auto should be the gradient / slant of the line connecting the
//        PEAKS of the humps. slanting upwards -> uptrend, downwards -> downtrend"
//      * flatter than SLANT_EPS (0.10 points per bar) is RANGE, not a weak trend
//      * THE GUARD LOW - 2026-08-04: "until previous lows are safe we can keep buying..
//        if there were two previous lows in a row, the deepest point, the lowest low is
//        considered.. we stop buying if the last low is broken - we say the trend has
//        shifted from BUYS now." Mirrored with highs for sells.
//      * A WICK IS A SWEEP, A CLOSE IS A BREAK. The guard breaks only on a CLOSED
//        candle's close beyond it. That rule exists because of a real failure: on
//        2026-08-05 the 18:50 spring swept the guard with a wick, the compass wrongly
//        shifted DOWN during the launch and blocked the 18:52 breakout.
//
//  IT IS A REMINDER, NOT A FILTER - the same standing as the extended-run label. Trend
//  context has been measured here four ways and failed all four (H1 filter, HH+HL,
//  structure, above-the-mean), so this answers "where am I?" and never "should I take
//  this?". A SELL SETUP inside a green uptrend is still a SELL SETUP.
//  OFF by default from 2026-09-27, and moved to BOTTOM CENTRE. Zee: "on the bottom right
//  there's a panel saying Downtrend and a large arrow. i think we can remove the panel ..
//  and replace it with something that tells us in tiny font.. only about the last candle
//  that is being formed.. like a live commentary".
//  The slot goes to the LIVE COMMENTARY (see grpLc). That is the better use of the one
//  piece of screen that stays put while he is zoomed into a single candle: the compass
//  answers "where am I", which changes a few times a session, and the commentary answers
//  "what is happening right now", which changes every candle.
//  NOTHING IS DELETED - turn this back on and the arrow returns at bottom centre, clear
//  of both the commentary (bottom right) and the imbalance meter (bottom left).
showCamel = input.bool(false, "Show the camel-hump trend arrow (bottom centre)", group = grpCm, display = display.none)
camelK    = input.int(2, "Swing needs this many bars each side (PIVOT_K)", minval = 1, group = grpCm, display = display.none)
camelEps  = input.float(0.10, "Flatter than this per bar is RANGE (SLANT_EPS)", step = 0.05, group = grpCm, display = display.none)
camelSize = input.string("huge", "Arrow size", options = ["normal", "large", "huge"], group = grpCm, display = display.none)
//  OFF by default at Zee's call, 2026-09-25. The polyline is the compass's WORKING, not
//  its answer - the arrow is the answer, and the arrow is the thing he asked for. Turn it
//  back on when the verdict looks wrong and the last three peaks need checking by eye.
camelLine = input.bool(false, "Also draw the hump polyline on the chart", group = grpCm, display = display.none)

grpHt = "Heat - how hard is it to move?"
//  HOT AND ICE. Zee, 2026-09-25: "shade each of the recent last 5 candles with their
//  resistance.. if the candles are getting HOT (red temperature) it means they're not able
//  to move upwards easily.. if the candles are getting cold ICE it means its slippery and
//  easy to go upwards.. that way i can see a candle and say hmm we're easily slipping
//  upwards.. or hmm.. we're having a red hot time going upwards".
//
//  WHAT IT MEASURES: how far this candle travelled per unit of volume, against how far the
//  last 20 bars travelled per unit of THEIR volume. Above 1 = gliding; below 1 = grinding.
//  It is the same arithmetic the ANOMALY label uses; this paints it continuously instead
//  of waiting for the extreme.
//
//  THE SCALE IS CALIBRATED, not guessed. Efficiency deciles over 19 sessions:
//      10%      30%      50%      70%      90%
//      0.58     0.78     0.97     1.21     1.66     on M1  (24,728 bars)
//      0.58     0.80     0.98     1.22     1.65     on M5  ( 4,943 bars)
//  Those two rows are the same to two decimals, which is why this needs no preset: one
//  temperature scale reads correctly on both timeframes.
//
//  ⚠️ IT IS A THERMOMETER, NOT A COMPASS - and this is where it differs from the request.
//  Zee's wording ties the temperature to UP ("not able to move upwards", "easy to go
//  upwards"). Measured against the next close, it does not:
//      M1   HOTTEST  742 bars  50.1% up (+0.8)     ICE 1013 bars  48.2% (-1.1)
//      M5   HOTTEST  140 bars  45.0% up (-4.8)     ICE  218 bars  50.5% (+0.7)
//  On M1 every band sits within a point of the coin, on samples of 700-11,000. A real
//  directional effect would be visible at that size and is not there. The ONE lean is M5's
//  hottest band, it points DOWN, and it is stronger on RED candles (42.6%) than green
//  (47.2%) - so "red hot" is not "struggling to rise", it is "struggling, either way".
//  That is the anomaly measurement again, which is the same quantity at its extreme.
//
//  SO THE COLOURS SAY EFFORT, NOT DIRECTION. Hot = the market is grinding; ice = it is
//  gliding. Which way it glides is a question for the setup labels, and they answer it
//  with their own numbers.
showHeat = input.bool(true, "🔥 / ❄ on each candle by EFFORT (hot = grinding)", group = grpHt, display = display.none)
heatHot  = input.float(0.60, "🔥 at or below this efficiency (10th percentile)", step = 0.05, group = grpHt, display = display.none)
heatIce  = input.float(1.60, "❄ at or above this efficiency (90th percentile)", step = 0.05, group = grpHt, display = display.none)
//  A NEUTRAL MARK, so a blank candle can be told from a broken one. Zee, 2026-09-25:
//  "on 5 mins timeframe i see the emojis on third last and forth last not the recent 2
//  candles.. maybe coz they have other labels?"
//  They do not - plotchar and label.new are different drawing layers and nothing here
//  gates one on the other. Those two bars simply landed between 0.60 and 1.60, which is
//  where about FOUR BARS IN FIVE land by construction: the thresholds ARE the 10th and
//  90th percentiles. Two blank candles in a row is the ordinary case, not a symptom.
//  But "nothing printed" and "the feature is dead" look identical on screen, and that is
//  worth one switch. With this on, every bar carries a mark - a small dot for the middle
//  band - so silence becomes visible rather than ambiguous.
//  OFF by default: a mark on every candle is the crowding the shading versions were
//  rejected for. Turn it on to check the mechanism, then turn it off again.
heatAlways = input.bool(false, "...and a faint dot on the in-between candles", group = grpHt, display = display.none)

grpSt = "The staircase"
//  THE SEQUENCE, NOT THE AVERAGE. Zee, 2026-09-26, describing the reading one step finer
//  than the imbalance meter: "i see the candles as a staircase.. as the reds are making
//  more reds.. i notice how much red bar body height was formed by how much volume.. was
//  it previously making larger body with volume X and now X+1 volume is making smaller red
//  body.. this tells me whether the sellers are in control. then once a green candle
//  appears i ask, how much green volume did it cost to make this candle appear as green..
//  is the cost more or less than what the reds paid to build their bodies."
//
//  THAT IS A DIFFERENT MEASUREMENT FROM THE IMBALANCE METER, and the difference matters:
//  the meter pools three candles into one ratio, which averages away the very thing he is
//  watching - whether each successive red candle is buying LESS body with MORE volume.
//  BODY, not range, throughout - his words are "body height", and the unit is
//  body / volume, "how much price did this volume buy".
//
//  MEASURED on 51 sessions, taking the first GREEN candle after a red run of 2 or more.
//  Base rate for the next close being higher there: 49.6% (5,465 events, 106 a day).
//
//    HIS TEST A - did the sellers decay through the run?      n      1 bar   3 bars  5 bars
//      red efficiency FELL every candle (his case 1)        1,660    49.9%   50.8%   50.5%
//      red efficiency ROSE every candle (his case 2)        1,596    48.1%   49.1%   47.7%
//      control - all first-green bars                       5,465    49.6%   50.3%   49.1%
//    Small, but it leans the way he says and it holds at all three horizons - decaying
//    sellers beat strengthening ones by 1.8, 1.7 and 2.8 points.
//
//    HIS TEST B - was the green candle cheap against the reds?
//      green efficiency 1.0-1.5x the reds                     795    49.4%   50.4%   47.8%
//      green efficiency 1.5-2.5x                              858    47.7%   50.1%   47.9%
//      green efficiency 2.5x+                                 800    49.4%   50.1%   48.0%
//      green efficiency BELOW the reds  (a DEAR green)      3,011    50.3%   50.4%   50.0%
//    ⛔ ON ITS OWN THIS HALF DOES NOT WORK, AND IT POINTS THE OTHER WAY. The best cell is
//    the one where the green candle cost MORE per point than the reds did, which is the
//    opposite of the reading. Non-monotone across the bands, so it is noise either way.
//
//    THE TWO TOGETHER, which is how he actually uses them:
//      decaying reds + green >= 1.5x the reds                 506    51.4%   50.8%   49.0%   +0.139
//      decaying reds + green >= 2.0x                          362    52.2%   51.9%   50.0%   +0.210
//      decaying reds + green >= 2.5x                          259    51.7%   49.4%   49.4%   +0.220
//    Against a 49.6% base and a control that pays +0.052, the 2.0x cell is +2.6 points and
//    four times the drift. That is the best version of his idea that exists in this data.
//
//  ⚠️ AND IT IS STILL INSIDE THE NOISE. 2.6 points on 362 samples is about one standard
//  error; the 2.5x cell breaks the monotone at three bars; every cell is flat by five bars.
//  This is NOT a promoted signal - it is his own reading, drawn so he can stop estimating
//  it by eye, carrying the honest number. Seven a day.
showStair = input.bool(true, "Mark the STAIRCASE - sellers decaying, then a cheap green", group = grpSt, display = display.none)
stairRedN = input.int(2, "...red candles that must decay in a row", minval = 2, group = grpSt, display = display.none)
stairGrnX = input.float(2.0, "...green candle buys >= x the price per point the reds paid", step = 0.25, group = grpSt, display = display.none)
//  THE PER-CANDLE READING, which is the staircase itself rather than its conclusion.
//  Each candle against the one before it, same colour only: did this volume buy MORE body
//  than last time, or less? The mark goes on the side that is LOSING the exchange, because
//  that is the tell he is hunting - a red candle paying more for less is the buy signal
//  forming, one step at a time.
//  A FACE, NOT A SIGNAL BAR. Zee, 2026-09-26: "if The sellers spent more and got less
//  ground -> we have a clear sad emoji.. less bad is -> 😞 sad but not crying, 😢 crying
//  its so bad, 😭 loudly crying, 💔 heart broken (extremely bad).. if we made a smaller
//  red volume and got a larger move than before then we're happy/happier/happiest".
//  THIS SOLVES THE PROBLEM THE LAST THREE ATTEMPTS DID NOT. ▂▅█ had to be compared with
//  each other; 🛜 at three sizes had to be compared with each other; and the emoji killed
//  the colour argument so the side was carried by position alone. A FACE NEEDS NO
//  COMPARISON - one glyph, read on its own, says both how much and which way.
//  AND IT ADDS THE HALF THAT WAS MISSING. Every version until now marked only DECLINES.
//  A candle that bought MORE body with LESS volume is the same measurement pointing the
//  other way, and it was invisible. Now both sides of the step are drawn.
//
//  THE BANDS ARE THE MEASURED DISTRIBUTION of step ratios - this candle's body-per-volume
//  against the previous same-colour candle's. 21,392 steps over 51 sessions, 416 a day on
//  842 candles a day:
//      💔  under a quarter of what the last one did     65.0/day   16%
//      😭  under a half                                 59.8       14%
//      😢  under three quarters                         48.0       12%
//      😞  just below                                   36.9        9%
//         about the same (1.00 - 1.33)                34.3        8%   never marked
//      🙂  a third more                                 46.8       11%
//      😃  twice as much                                58.3       14%
//      🤩  four times or more                           67.3       16%
//
//  ALL EIGHT ARE ON, AT ZEE'S CALL, 2026-09-26 - he read the density warning below and
//  overrode it: "i'm loving the faces. can we have all of them ON by default.. they're
//  really really helpful in guaging the volume/spread imbalance".
//  ⚠️ WHAT THAT COSTS, recorded so the trade-off he accepted is not lost: 416 marks a
//  day on 842 candles - a face on roughly HALF of them, before the heat emoji (~20% of
//  bars) and the multiplier digits (~13%) are counted. I shipped the middle five first
//  for exactly that reason and was wrong about which way he would want it: the faces
//  need no comparison to read, so density hurts them far less than it hurt ▂▅█ or the
//  three 🛜 sizes. Frequency is not the only thing that makes a mark unreadable.
//  If the chart ever does get too busy, these two knobs are the first thing to move -
//  0.75 / 2.00 drops the mildest band each side (~300 a day), 0.50 / 4.00 leaves only the
//  extremes (~190). The feature is not the problem; the bands are.
stairSteps = input.bool(true, "...and show the mood of the side that made each candle", group = grpSt, display = display.none)
stairSadX  = input.float(1.00, "...SAD when it bought <= x of what the last one did", step = 0.05, group = grpSt, display = display.none)
stairHapX  = input.float(1.33, "...HAPPY when it bought >= x of what the last one did", step = 0.25, group = grpSt, display = display.none)

grpLc = "Live commentary"
//  WHAT THE FORMING CANDLE IS COSTING THE SIDE THAT IS MAKING IT. Zee, 2026-09-27:
//    "if its a buy candle: is the force needed by buyers required to move the market up
//     lesser or more than previous candle?
//     if its a red candle: is the force needed by sellers required to move the market
//     down lesser or more than previous candle?"
//
//  FORCE IS THE INVERSE OF EFFICIENCY, and that is the whole idea: everywhere else this
//  file measures BODY PER UNIT OF VOLUME - how much price a unit of volume buys. He is
//  asking the same question the other way up - VOLUME PER POINT, how much effort a point
//  of movement costs. Under 1.00x means this candle is getting its ground more cheaply
//  than the last one the same side made.
//
//  AGAINST THE LAST CANDLE OF THE SAME COLOUR, not simply the previous bar. Buyers' force
//  has to be compared with buyers' force; setting it against a red candle would be
//  comparing two different sides doing two different jobs. The panel says how many bars
//  back that comparison reaches, because a green candle five bars ago is a weaker
//  reference than the one immediately behind.
//
//  IT IS THE MOOD FACE AS A SENTENCE. Same arithmetic as the staircase step (stRatioStep),
//  inverted and put into words, on the LIVE bar only - so the faces mark the history and
//  this reads out the candle actually forming in front of him.
//
//  ⚠️ IT FLICKERS EARLY IN A CANDLE, and that is arithmetic rather than a bug - the same
//  caveat the heat strip carries. Body and volume both start near zero and their ratio is
//  unstable until the bar fills. Treat the reading in the first seconds of a candle as a
//  running estimate; it is honest by the close.
//  ⛔ IT PREDICTS NOTHING. The step ratio it reports measured flat against the next close
//  over 21,392 steps - see the mood-face note at stairSteps. It describes what this
//  candle is costing, which is a fact about now, not about the next bar.
//  TWO BATTERIES, 2026-09-28. Zee: "build a battery icon for both buy and sell. green
//  battery for buy. and red battery for sell.. the batteries tell us how much force the
//  buyers/sellers have to exert to move the candle size".
//  FULL = CHEAP. A full battery means that side is moving price easily - it has charge
//  left. An empty one means it is grinding: every point is costing it dearly. That is the
//  natural reading of a battery and it is the right way round for his question, because
//  "force needed" and "charge remaining" are the same quantity inverted.
//
//  ONE SCALE FOR BOTH, AND THAT WAS CHECKED FIRST. Each side's body-per-volume over the
//  last imbK candles, divided by the 20-bar baseline (average body / average volume).
//  Measured over 43,238 M1 bars the two sides' distributions are the same:
//      side       5%    10%    25%    50%    75%    90%    95%
//      BUYERS    0.16   0.28   0.55   0.93   1.40   1.95   2.35
//      SELLERS   0.16   0.27   0.54   0.92   1.39   1.92   2.31
//  Medians 0.926 against 0.916. Identical to two decimals, so one scale reads correctly
//  for both and a green battery fuller than the red one MEANS something.
//
//  THE FIVE BARS ARE THE QUINTILES of that combined distribution - each level is exactly
//  a fifth of all readings, so "three bars" really is the middle of the road:
//      ▮▯▯▯▯   under 0.46    grinding - every point is expensive
//      ▮▮▯▯▯   0.46 - 0.77   heavy going
//      ▮▮▮▯▯   0.77 - 1.09   ordinary effort
//      ▮▮▮▮▯   1.09 - 1.53   moving well
//      ▮▮▮▮▮   1.53 and up   gliding - price is almost free
//
//  ⭐ WHY TWO BATTERIES RATHER THAN THE ONE RATIO ALREADY IN THE CORNER. The imbalance
//  meter divides one side by the other, so it cannot tell "both moving freely" from
//  "both stuck" - the ratio is 1.0 in both cases and they are opposite markets. Measured:
//      both batteries FULL  (4-5 bars each)   1,512 bars   4.7%   a fast market
//      both batteries EMPTY (1 bar each)      1,378 bars   4.2%   a dead grind
//  8.9% of bars carry a state the ratio is blind to. That is the whole case for drawing
//  the two levels instead of their quotient.
//
//  AND THEY READ WHEN THE PUNCH CANNOT. The punch needs both colours in the window;
//  three greens in a row leaves it silent. Each battery only needs its own colour, so the
//  buyers' battery keeps reporting through a clean run and the sellers' says "no red".
//
//  ⛔ NEITHER BATTERY PREDICTS. This is the same efficiency the mood faces and the
//  imbalance meter are built on, and it measured flat against the next close every way it
//  has been tested. A full green battery says buyers are having an easy time RIGHT NOW.
//  It does not say the next candle is green.
showBat   = input.bool(true, "Buyer / seller force batteries (bottom right)", group = grpLc, display = display.none)
//  EVERY TICK, NOT EVERY CANDLE. Zee, 2026-09-28: "we wanna make the batteries very
//  sensitive.. in a way that during 1 minute candle formation. each second / tick is
//  monitored by the batteries".
//  THE CANDLE VERSION COULD NOT DO THIS. It pools the last imbK candles, so two closed
//  bars outvote the one forming and a burst inside the current minute barely moves the
//  needle. PER TICK throws the closed bars away and measures the move between one tick
//  and the next.
//
//  HOW IT IS BUILT, and it is the only mechanism in Pine that can: `varip`. An ordinary
//  variable is ROLLED BACK and recomputed on every tick of the forming bar, so it cannot
//  remember what happened a second ago. A varip survives, so these four pools accumulate
//  across ticks the way a real tape reader's memory does.
//  EACH TICK contributes the price it moved and the volume it took to move it, to
//  whichever side the move favoured - up to the buyers, down to the sellers. The pools
//  then DECAY by a fixed fraction every tick, which makes them an exponential window over
//  roughly the last batMem ticks rather than a total since the open.
//
//  ⚠️ UPTICK = BUYING IS AN APPROXIMATION. Inside one tick interval both sides traded;
//  attributing the whole of that volume to the direction price moved is the standard
//  reading and it is the SAME convention this file already uses at candle level (a green
//  candle is buying). It is a convention, not a measurement of who actually lifted.
//
//  ⚠️ IT IS A LIVE INSTRUMENT AND IT IS HONEST ONLY IN REAL TIME. On historical bars
//  TradingView replays no ticks, so a varip updates once per bar and the reading quietly
//  degrades to a decaying per-bar version. That is why the mode falls back automatically:
//  with no tick history accumulated the battery shows the CANDLE reading instead, so what
//  you see on old bars is never a tick claim that was never measured.
//  ⛔ PER CANDLE IS NOW THE DEFAULT, because PER TICK shipped BROKEN on 2026-09-28 and
//  the repair is only a day old. Two faults, both caught by Zee from screenshots and both
//  since measured on 13 MILLION REAL TICKS from the PXBT logs - the first tick data this
//  project has ever had:
//    1. SYMMETRY. Each side's move was divided by its OWN tick count, which measures
//       average tick SIZE. Tick sizes are symmetric, so both batteries moved together and
//       could never disagree. Fixed at batTickUpE - the pools now divide by the window's
//       TOTAL ticks, which keeps the up-against-down COUNT that carries all the meaning.
//    2. SCALE. A tick window's gross one-way movement runs about 4.2x a candle's net body
//       per unit of effort, so the candle quintiles put 99% of tick readings in the top
//       band. Tick mode now has its own measured cuts (batLvlTick).
//  ⚠️ AND MY OWN "IT DOES NOT PREDICT" TABLE IS VOID. It was computed against the broken
//  pooling, so it measured the bug rather than the idea. Nothing is currently known about
//  what a tick battery predicts, in either direction.
//  ⚠️ THE NEW CUTS ARE CALIBRATED ON PXBT TICKS, a different broker from the OANDA chart
//  this script runs on. The shape should carry; the exact numbers are a first pass.
batMode   = input.string("PER CANDLE - steady", "Battery speed", options = ["PER TICK - live, most sensitive", "PER CANDLE - steady"], group = grpLc, display = display.none)
//  the exponential memory, in ticks. 40 is about the last few seconds on active gold and
//  a good deal longer when it is quiet - it is a count of TICKS, not of time, so the
//  battery automatically pays more attention when the tape is busy.
batMem    = input.int(40, "...ticks of memory (lower = twitchier)", minval = 5, maxval = 500, group = grpLc, display = display.none)
showLive  = input.bool(true, "Live commentary on the forming candle (bottom right)", group = grpLc, display = display.none)
liveBack  = input.int(20, "...look back this far for the last same-colour candle", minval = 2, group = grpLc, display = display.none)
//  CANDLE ANALYSIS - the forming candle's own SHAPE, read out above the batteries.
//  Zee, 2026-09-28: "above the batteries i want to write in text: Heading: Candle
//  analysis. Information in text about current candle (these help predict the next
//  candle)", with fifteen bullets - close location, wick lengths, body expansion, NR7,
//  body-to-wick ratio, colour, marubozu, doji, wick imbalance, spinning top, the two
//  sweep footprints and the two climax forms.
//
//  ⛔ EVERY ONE OF THE FIFTEEN WAS MEASURED, AND THE DIRECTIONAL HALF ALL FAILED. 43,434
//  M1 bars, against a 49.5% base rate for the next candle being GREEN:
//      the claim                         n      says      measured   verdict
//      close in TOP 20% of range      10727   up next      48.7%     BACKWARDS
//      close in BOTTOM 20%            10753   down next    50.6% grn BACKWARDS
//      long UPPER wick >=50%           6841   lower next   51.5% grn BACKWARDS
//      long LOWER wick >=50%           6906   higher next  48.6% grn BACKWARDS
//      body >70% of range             11538   continuation 49.5%     nothing
//      wick >70% of range             12734   reversal     49.9%     nothing
//      GREEN candle                   21485   buying wins  49.2%     nothing
//      MARUBOZU (no wicks)             1243   continuation 49.0%     nothing
//      DOJI (body <=10%)               4033   balance      50.2%     nothing
//      lower wick >= 2x upper         15232   biases UP    48.8%     BACKWARDS
//      upper wick >= 2x lower         14962   biases DOWN  50.1% grn BACKWARDS
//      SPINNING TOP                    2790   chop         48.7%     nothing
//      BULLISH SWEEP footprint         2705   bull reversal 49.9%    nothing
//      BEARISH SWEEP footprint         2645   bear reversal 51.7% grn BACKWARDS
//  NOT ONE of them clears the base rate by more than 2.2 points, on samples of 1,200 to
//  21,000 where a real effect could not hide. SEVEN point the OPPOSITE way to the claim.
//  This is the third time this file has run that study - the direction-hints note records
//  the same result on eight other candle shapes - and it lands in the same place: CANDLE
//  SHAPE DOES NOT PREDICT DIRECTION ON THIS INSTRUMENT.
//
//  ✅ BUT THE VOLATILITY HALF WORKS, AND IT WORKS WELL. Candle shape does not say WHICH
//  WAY the next bar goes; it says HOW BIG it will be, and that is a different question the
//  bullets happened to ask twice:
//      the claim                    n      next range > 20-bar avg   next bar INSIDE
//      BASE                       43403          41.3%                   14.0%
//      body expansion >=1.5x       9733          49.0%  (+7.7)           20.2%
//      CLIMAX body after a 4+ run   165          67.9%  (+26.6)          22.4%
//      CLIMAX range, tiny body      114          74.6%  (+33.3)          37.7%
//      NR7 - narrowest of seven    6709          36.1%  (-5.2)            3.3%  (-10.7)
//  AND IT REPLICATES ON M5 with the same sign and a bigger gap - climax range 86.2%
//  against a 41.6% base, body expansion 54.0%. Two timeframes agreeing is the ridge this
//  file trusts; the directional column agrees on nothing.
//
//  ⚠️ NR7 IS THE ONE HIS NOTE GETS HALF RIGHT. "An unusually narrow range bar predicts an
//  explosive volatility expansion" - on the RANGE measure that is false here (36.1%
//  against a 41.3% base: the next bar is usually still narrow). On the CONTAINMENT measure
//  it is true and strongly so: only 3.3% of next bars stay inside an NR7, against 14.0%
//  normally. A narrow bar is not followed by a big bar; it is followed by a bar that
//  LEAVES it. Both numbers are printed so the difference is visible.
//
//  SO THE PANEL READS THE SHAPE AND SAYS WHAT THE SHAPE HAS EARNED: the geometry as
//  measured facts, a SIZE forecast that carries real numbers, and a DIRECTION line that
//  prints the honest hit rate for whatever shape is on screen - which is near 50% every
//  time. It describes; it does not forecast direction.
showCandle = input.bool(true, "Candle analysis panel (above the batteries)", group = grpLc, display = display.none)

grpIb = "Volume / spread imbalance"
//  HIS OWN METHOD, PUT ON THE CHART. Zee, 2026-09-26, describing what he actually looked
//  at before each of 79 trades on 25 September:
//    for a BUY  - "sellers couldn't pull the market lower even with high selling (red)
//                 volumes. PLUS. the little volume up candles were raising the price even
//                 with little volume initially.. or atleast more than what the red volumes
//                 were doing"
//    for a SELL - "seller volumes could at low volumes bring the price down whereas even
//                 high buy volumes were struggling to bring the price up"
//    "i just saw the last 3-4 candles to compare volumes/spread imbalance"
//
//  WHAT IT COMPUTES, over the last imbK candles:
//      up efficiency   = total points gained on GREEN bars / total volume of those bars
//      down efficiency = total points lost on RED bars    / total volume of those bars
//      IMBALANCE       = up efficiency / down efficiency
//  Above 1, buyers move price further per unit of volume than sellers do. Below 1, the
//  sellers are the efficient side. It is the first measurement in this file that compares
//  the TWO SIDES to each other rather than one bar to its own 20-bar window.
//
//  ✅ HIS DESCRIPTION IS EXACT - verified against his own live fills, which is the one
//  kind of evidence CLAUDE.md lets us promote. Matching all 79 entries to the minute
//  against the OANDA feed (his broker runs chart time minus 3 hours):
//      population median imbalance          1.02   (32,491 bars)
//      median at his 44 BUY entries         2.38   - 83% above the population median
//      median at his 35 SELL entries        0.42   - only 22% above it
//  He was reading this number off the candles by eye every single time, and reading it
//  correctly. That is why it is drawn: it replaces an eyeball estimate he already makes.
//
//  ⛔ AND IT DOES NOT PREDICT DIRECTION. Swept across the whole table, "did the next close
//  go the way the imbalance points":
//      imbalance >= 2.0   8,808 bars   49.3% up      >= 3.0   5,520   49.5%
//      imbalance <= 1/2   8,557 bars   49.7% down    <= 1/3   5,378   49.1%
//      base rate                       49.7%
//  Flat at every threshold, both directions, at 1, 3 and 5 bars, on samples of 4,000 to
//  12,000. And the control says it is not even a disguised version of "price rose over the
//  last 3 bars": splitting THAT by whether the imbalance agrees gives 49.4% against 49.1%.
//  Adding his retracement condition made it worse (46.1% at five bars).
//
//  ⚠️ THE EXCURSION TEST - the right yardstick for a 34-second trade - is the one that
//  explains his day, and it is worth reading twice. From any bar at all, within 2 candles:
//      reached +0.55 points (his average win)   69.6% UP   70.7% DOWN
//      reached -0.38 points (his average loss)  78.7%      77.2%
//  A small target gets hit about 70% of the time WHICHEVER WAY YOU PICK, because M1 gold
//  swings ~1.5 points inside two minutes. The imbalance does not shift that by one point
//  at any threshold (69.5 / 70.0 / 70.1 for buys). His 81% win rate is that 70% baseline
//  plus his own exits - and note the stop is reached MORE often than the target, so a
//  mechanical version of his trade loses money. His buy entries specifically reached the
//  target only 55% of the time, BELOW the 70% baseline, and he still won 81% of them.
//
//  ⛔ AND "WINNING" IS THE WRONG WORD - SEARCHED FOR PROPERLY, 2026-09-28. Zee asked for
//  the long tooltip to be replaced by a verdict: "is it possible to say: Buyers are
//  winning, or Sellers are winning.. Find out what makes it say so." So twenty variants
//  were tested over 43,323 M1 bars against a 49.60% base rate, and NONE earns it:
//      candidate                              n      next up%   vs base
//      ratio >= 5   (buyers)               2,938      48.2%      -1.4
//      ratio >= 9                          1,480      48.7%      -0.9
//      ratio >= 20                           571      47.1%      -2.5
//      ratio <= 1/5 (sellers)              2,877      50.2%      +0.6
//      ratio <= 1/9                        1,372      51.4%      +1.8
//      2 of 3 candles green and ratio>=2   4,998      49.1%      -0.5
//      crossed UP through 1.0              3,565      48.9%      -0.7
//      crossed UP through 2.0              2,934      49.1%      -0.5
//      buyers 2x+ at the 20-bar HIGH       1,890      47.5%      -2.1
//      sellers 2x+ at the 20-bar LOW       1,875      51.0%      +1.4
//      ratio RISING and above 1            8,578      48.9%      -0.7
//      ratio FALLING and below 1           8,498      50.1%      +0.5
//  THE SIGN IS CONSISTENT AND IT POINTS THE WRONG WAY. Every BUYER-dominant cell lands
//  BELOW the base rate and every SELLER-dominant one ABOVE it, across twenty cells, at
//  one bar and at three. The sizes are small (1 to 2.5 points) and none of it is
//  tradeable - but twenty cells agreeing on a sign is not random scatter. If the meter
//  leans at all it leans CONTRARIAN: the side moving price cheaply has mostly already
//  moved it, which is the same reading the M1 SHOOTING STAR carries on 647 samples.
//  So the label says AHEAD, not WINNING. Ahead is a fact about now; winning is a claim
//  about next, and the only version of that claim the data supports is the opposite one.
//  AND THEN THE NOTE WENT ALTOGETHER. Zee, 2026-09-28, of the four-line version: "instead
//  of a long note just say: buyers ahead. that's it." The hover now carries two words and
//  nothing else; everything above stays HERE, in the source, where it is read once rather
//  than every time the pointer crosses the label.
//  ⚠️ WHAT THAT COSTS: the ⛔ NOT-A-FORECAST caution is no longer on the chart. The
//  refutation above is the reason this meter is allowed to describe and never to predict,
//  and a reader who has not seen it will take "BUYERS AHEAD" for a buy signal. It is not
//  one. That warning now lives only in this file.
//  THE WORD ALSO GOT A LOWER BAR - see ibAheadB below. It used to need imbLoud (2.0x) to
//  name a side at all, so a meter reading 1.65x printed LEVEL while its own tooltip said
//  buyers were getting 1.65x more ground. Ahead now pivots on the measured median, 1.02.
//
//  SO THIS METER DESCRIBES, IT NEVER PREDICTS. Same standing as NO SUPPLY. It tells you
//  what the last three candles did; which way to trade is a question for the setup labels,
//  and they answer it with their own numbers.
//
//  ✅ AND IT IS THE ONE THING THAT STILL SPEAKS IN A DEAD SESSION - the strongest
//  argument for this family, and it is Zee's, 2026-09-26: "i am taking rapid trades in a
//  very very unpredictable environment. any clues are helpful. mostly also because on
//  Friday night the volumes were overall low and at that time no setup was being marked.
//  despite peaks happening in candles n all".
//  MEASURED over 43,238 M1 bars, and it is a stronger property than he claimed - the
//  mark rate does not merely survive, it does not move AT ALL:
//                                  bars    a FACE   a PUNCH   campaign clears HEAVY
//      quietest quarter of days   12,234   45.6%    40.5%     2.81%
//      middle                     20,639   44.9%    40.1%     2.86%
//      busiest quarter of days    10,129   46.1%    40.0%     2.85%
//  And by HOUR, where median volume runs 150 at 01:00 to 1,145 at 17:00 - a factor of
//  7.6 - every hour lands between 43.8% and 47.2% for faces and 36.6% and 42.5% for
//  punches, while the heavy gate swings from 0.70% (hour 06) to 5.65% (hour 02).
//
//  WHY: THESE ARE RELATIVE AND THE CAMPAIGN GATE IS ABSOLUTE. A face divides one candle
//  by the previous candle of its colour; a punch divides one side by the other. Both
//  ratios are scale-free - halve every volume on the chart and neither moves. heavyAbs
//  asks whether the run beats 1.20x a trailing 20-bar average, which is a question about
//  absolute size.
//
//  ONE CORRECTION TO THE DIAGNOSIS, because it is not "volumes were low":
//      20-bar average volume RISING    19,990 bars   face 45.8%  punch 39.9%  heavy 4.13%
//      20-bar average volume FALLING   23,189 bars   face 45.0%  punch 40.5%  heavy 1.76%
//  Hour 01 has the LOWEST volume on the chart and an ABOVE-average heavy rate, because
//  its trailing average is low too. It is FALLING volume that silences the setups - the
//  same staleness recorded at excludeRun (43.5% heavy rising, 9.3% falling on M5).
//
//  SO THIS IS NOT A WEAKER VERSION OF A SETUP LABEL. It is the instrument that works in
//  the conditions the setup logic cannot describe. That is a different job, not a lesser
//  one - and it does NOT upgrade these marks into predictions. Coverage is not accuracy:
//  a mark firing on 45% of bars in a dead session is still flat against the next close.
showImb  = input.bool(true, "Show the buyer / seller efficiency meter", group = grpIb, display = display.none)
//  SWEPT AT LAST, 2026-09-26, and 3 was mine and his by eye until now. Zee: "can you
//  test whether 3 candles is the number.. because we made up this 3. what if we took the
//  last 5 candles into consideration.. what if that can predict the next candle's
//  appearance more accurately than 3?"
//  K 2..10 x five thresholds x four yardsticks INCLUDING the next candle's own COLOUR -
//  180 cells over 43,238 M1 bars. Base rates: next candle green 49.5%, next close 49.6%.
//      K       next candle's COLOUR at 2.0x      samples
//      2       49.8%                             12,915
//      3       49.7%                             17,371
//      4       49.6%                             17,856
//      5       49.9%                             16,778
//      8       50.0%                             12,123
//      10      49.7%                              9,327
//  ⛔ NO WINDOW PREDICTS AND 5 IS NO BETTER THAN 3. All 180 cells fell between 43.6% and
//  52.9%, nearly all within a point of the base rate. The two that looked interesting
//  (K=6 at 9x, 52.9% on 904; K=8 at 5x, 52.0% on 1,252) have neighbours at K=5 and K=7
//  that fall back to 49.9% and 50.3% - spikes, not a ridge.
//
//  SO IT WAS DECIDED ON THE ONLY CRITERION LEFT: which window best reproduces what his
//  EYE read. Measured against his own 79 fills of 25 September (57 readable):
//      K    his buys' median   his sells' median   separation   buys>pop   sells<pop
//      2    1.86               0.57                3.3x         68%        70%
//      3    2.38               0.42                5.7x         83%        78%
//      4    2.04               0.82                2.5x         80%        53%
//      5    2.15               0.77                2.8x         79%        64%
//      8    1.78               0.68                2.6x         86%        68%
//      10   1.45               0.83                1.7x         82%        69%
//  K=3 SEPARATES HIS TWO SIDES BY 5.7x WHERE NOTHING ELSE BEATS 3.3x - more than twice
//  as sharp. Every wider window pulls both medians back towards 1.00, which is what
//  dilution looks like: adding candles averages out the very imbalance he was spotting.
//  K=4 collapses the sell side to 53%, a coin. Widening does not sharpen it, it blurs it.
//
//  3 STAYS, AND IT IS NO LONGER A GUESS - two independent reasons agree: it is what he
//  said he does ("i just saw the last 3-4 candles") and it is the best fit of nine tested.
//  ⚠️ 57 entries, so the fit is suggestive not settled, and K=8's combined score (154) is
//  within noise of K=3's (161). The SEPARATION column is not: 5.7x against 2.6x is a real
//  margin even on 57.
imbK     = input.int(3, "...over the last this many candles", minval = 2, maxval = 10, group = grpIb, display = display.none)
imbLoud  = input.float(2.0, "Call one side DOMINANT past this ratio", step = 0.25, group = grpIb, display = display.none)
//  LABELLED ON THE CANDLE, NOT JUST IN THE CORNER. Zee, 2026-09-26: "i want this
//  labelled properly: Buyers moving 2.4x more per unit of volume when you bought;
//  sellers 2.4x more when you sold."
//  THE CONSTRAINT IS FREQUENCY, and it is why this is drawn in two pieces rather than
//  as one label per occurrence. Measured over 51 sessions:
//      dominance at 2.0x, every bar it is true    171 buy + 167 sell PER DAY
//      ...only the bar it crosses over on          86 +  84 per day
//      ...only when the advantage CHANGES HANDS     13 +  13 per day (at 3.0x)
//  A text label 338 times a day is not a label, it is wallpaper - the staircase failure
//  the ▲ hint had on 2026-09-24, an order of magnitude worse.
//  AND THE HANDOVER IS NOT WHAT HE TRADES: only 3 to 14 of his 57 readable entries sat
//  on one, against 32 that simply had dominance at 2.0x on the entry bar. He reads the
//  LEVEL, continuously - so the level is what gets drawn, continuously.
//    THE NUMBER  rides the live candle, where he is looking when zoomed in.
//    THE MARKS   go on history as tiny chars, the same mechanism as the heat strip.
imbLive  = input.bool(true, "Put the ratio on the live candle", group = grpIb, display = display.none)
//  THE NUMBER ON EVERY CANDLE, NOT A SYMBOL. Zee, 2026-09-26: "i like the buyers 7.7x
//  label very much.. can't we say like labels like: 1x,2x,3x which indicates strength of
//  move (spread) per unit of volume.. like we have in video games.. a power punch is
//  1x,2x,3x i think in street fighter".
//  He is right and the hollow triangles it replaces were my mistake: △ and ▽ carried a
//  THRESHOLD ("past 4.5x") with no way to tell 5x from 20x, and they looked like the
//  filled ▲ direction hint that means something else entirely. A digit says the size AND
//  reads at a glance, which is the whole reason the 7.7x readout worked.
//  TIERS ARE THE MEASURED PERCENTILES, not round numbers: 2.15 / 4.62 / 8.22 are the 75th,
//  90th and 95th, so 2 / 3 / 5 / 9 label "common", "notable", "top decile", "top 5%".
//      tier                            BUYERS/day   SELLERS/day
//        2  👷  building something       64.0         61.9
//        3  🏋  lifting heavy weight     50.3         48.8
//        5  🧚‍♀️  the fairy has arrived    28.3         29.2
//        9  🧙‍♂️  GANDALF - top 5%         28.8         26.7
//  ALL FOUR TIERS ARE ON, AT ZEE'S CALL, 2026-09-26: "can we see them all by default".
//  The same override he made on the mood faces an hour earlier, and for the same reason -
//  a picture is recognised, not decoded, so crowding costs it less than it cost the
//  digits it replaced.
//  ⚠️ WHAT IT COSTS, so the trade-off is recorded rather than discovered: ~338 marks a
//  day, both sides counted, on 842 candles - a punch on about FOUR CANDLES IN TEN. Added
//  to the mood faces (~49% of candles) and the heat emoji (~20%), most bars now carry two
//  marks and many carry three. That is the busiest this chart has ever been, and the
//  three families do not collide: the heat emoji is ABOVE every bar, the punch and the
//  face sit above or below by what they mean.
//  ONE REAL AMBIGUITY TO KNOW ABOUT. Below a red candle you can now get a MOOD FACE (the
//  sellers' mood, this candle against the last red one) and a PUNCH (the buyers being the
//  efficient side over three candles). Different questions, different windows, same side
//  of the bar. Hover either one - the tooltips name themselves.
//  Raise this back to 5 for ~113 marks a day (one bar in five, the heat strip's density),
//  or 3 for ~225.
imbMark  = input.bool(true, "...and mark each candle 👷 🏋 🧚‍♀️ 🧙‍♂️", group = grpIb, display = display.none)
imbTierMin = input.int(2, "...from this tier up (2 👷 / 3 🏋 / 5 🧚‍♀️ / 9 🧙‍♂️)", options = [2, 3, 5, 9], group = grpIb, display = display.none)

grpEd = "End of downtrend"
//  THE BOTTOM, NAMED. Zee, 2026-09-26: "when after a lot of red candles we finally have a
//  few green candles.. and the green volume is reducing per candle but the candle's are
//  pulling the price up .. then in this case we wanna label the bottom which the red
//  candles had made as an End of downtrend."
//
//  ⚠️ THE FALLING VOLUME IS NOT WHAT CARRIES IT, and that is the one part of his
//  description this label does not implement. Measured over 19 sessions, asking whether
//  the red run's LOW then held for 20 bars. Base rate for any red run plus one green
//  bar: 21.8%.
//
//      M1, red run >= 5           n     low HELD 20 bars   up @5 bars   avg move
//      2 green, volume FALLING    47        38.3%            48.9%       -0.09
//      2 green, volume not        175       38.9%            52.0%       +0.08
//      3 green, volume FALLING     8        (too few)
//      3 green, volume not        101       56.4%            59.4%       +0.43
//
//  The falling-volume cell is never the best one, and at THREE green bars it is 8 samples
//  against 101. What actually lifts the hold rate is simply HOW MANY green bars arrive -
//  21.8% base, ~38% at two, 56.4% at three. So the label counts candles, not volume, and
//  the volume reading is printed in the tooltip for the eye rather than gated on.
//  At red run >= 3 the same test gave 36.5% for his exact pattern against 33.1% for its
//  complement - three points on 203 samples, and INVERTED at G=3 (33.3% against 44.0%).
//  Non-monotone across settings is this file's standing signature of noise.
//
//  WHAT THE HEADLINE MAY AND MAY NOT SAY. At its best cell the low still fails 44% of the
//  time, so "END OF DOWNTREND" as a verdict would be wrong nearly half the time. It is
//  therefore worded as a LEAN - the bottom is marked, and the number that marks it travels
//  with it.
//  A line is drawn back across the run to the low itself, because the claim is about THAT
//  price, not about the bar the label prints on.
//  ~2.6 a day on M1 at the shipped settings.
showEod  = input.bool(true, "Mark the low when a long red run turns", group = grpEd, display = display.none)
eodRedN  = input.int(5, "...after this many red candles", minval = 2, group = grpEd, display = display.none)
eodGrnN  = input.int(3, "...and this many green ones (3 is where it works)", minval = 2, group = grpEd, display = display.none)

grpSs = "Shooting star"
//  HIS NAME, HIS MEANING - AND IT IS NOT THE TEXTBOOK'S. Zee, 2026-09-26: "when the price
//  makes a very strong candle (without much resistance) we can label it Shooting Star!
//  what it means is that in a very few seconds the price moved up very much. means we're
//  making a shooting star".
//  ⚠️ NAMING COLLISION, RECORDED SO NOBODY IS CAUGHT BY IT. In standard candlestick
//  language a "shooting star" is a SMALL body with a long UPPER wick at a top - a bearish
//  reversal pin. Zee means the opposite shape: a big impulsive candle that covered a lot
//  of ground fast. This file uses HIS meaning because it is his chart and his vocabulary
//  (the same call that made GEARING the headline), but anyone reading this script against
//  a VSA textbook must not assume the two words mean the same thing.
//
//  WHAT IT MEASURES: a big range (>= revStarRngX the 20-bar average) that cost LITTLE
//  volume to make - efficiency >= revStarEffX, the same points-per-unit-volume the heat
//  strip and the anomaly use. Far, and cheaply: nobody was standing in the way.
//
//  AND THE TWO TIMEFRAMES SAY OPPOSITE THINGS, which is the whole finding.
//  Measured over 19 sessions, both directions, "did it CONTINUE the way the candle went":
//
//      M1  range >= 1.8x, eff >= 1.6        n      1 bar      3 bars     5 bars
//        GREEN continued up?               314   48.1%      48.4%      46.8%
//        RED   continued down?             333   43.2%      48.0%      48.9%
//        FADING it instead                 647   54.1% +0.20 51.8% +0.33 52.1% +0.49
//
//      M5  range >= 1.5x, eff >= 1.6
//        GREEN continued up?                89   57.3% +0.53 58.4% +1.07 56.8% +1.00
//        RED   continued down?              91   56.0% +0.30 53.8% +0.26 51.6% +0.17
//        FADING it instead                 180   43.3% -0.42 43.9% -0.66 45.8% -0.57
//
//  ON M1 IT IS EXHAUSTION. Both sides lose when chased, the loss GROWS with the horizon
//  (-0.15 / -0.30 / -0.50 on the green side), and fading it pays at all three distances
//  on 647 samples. A move that came easily on M1 has already happened.
//  ON M5 IT IS MOMENTUM. Both sides continue, ~57%, and fading loses. Same pattern, same
//  arithmetic, opposite conclusion.
//  That is not a contradiction to explain away - it is the preset doctrine again. A
//  1.8x M1 candle is about a third of an M5 candle: on the five-minute chart the same
//  event is a bar still being built, and on the one-minute chart it is a bar already
//  finished. So the label reads the PRESET and says which of the two it is.
//
//  ⚠️ THE M5 HALF IS THE WEAKER ONE. Its sample is 89 and 91, and it SAWTOOTHS across the
//  range threshold - at 2.0x the green side falls to 45.5% and at 2.5x to 35.5%, which is
//  the noise signature this file rejects elsewhere. The M1 half is monotone on 647.
//  Trust the M1 reading; treat the M5 one as a fortnight's trial.
//  ABOUT 10 A DAY on M1 at the shipped 2.0 / 1.6, both sides counted. Loosen the range to
//  1.8 for the better-sampled cell above, at roughly 16 a day.
showStarBar  = input.bool(true, "Mark SHOOTING STAR - a big candle that cost little", group = grpSs, display = display.none)
starRngX     = input.float(2.0, "...its range >= x the average range", step = 0.1, group = grpSs, display = display.none)
starEffX     = input.float(1.6, "...and it moved >= x as far per unit of volume", step = 0.1, group = grpSs, display = display.none)

grpRv = "Reversal expected"
//  STOPPING VOLUME, IN HIS WORDS. Zee, 2026-09-26: "on M1 when a very large red volume
//  fails to move the price down much further than the previous candle.. we want a label
//  saying.. reversal expected".
//
//  THIS FILE HAD ALREADY REJECTED STOPPING VOLUME, and the note at the GEARING input says
//  so: "the upthrust, spring and stopping-volume tests rejected earlier this week were
//  spikes that inverted between neighbouring settings". That test asked whether the bar
//  was NARROW. His asks whether it FAILED TO EXTEND PAST THE PREVIOUS CANDLE'S LOW, which
//  is a different question and the better one: a bar can be wide and still not get
//  anywhere new. It is the same correction he has now made three times - compare it to the
//  candle beside it, not to a twenty-bar average - and it has beaten my yardstick each time.
//
//  MEASURED on M1, 19 sessions, base rate 49.7% for a higher close. Win rate at 3 bars,
//  anomaly bars excluded (see below):
//
//      vol >=   ext<=0.00    ext<=0.10    ext<=0.20    ext<=0.35    ext<=0.50
//      1.40     52.0 (373)   51.8 (434)   50.9 (489)   51.3 (594)   51.6 (725)
//      1.60     56.3 (183)   55.6 (205)   54.6 (227)   55.4 (271)   53.9 (332)
//      1.75     62.6 (107)   62.0 (121)   61.2 (134)   60.7 (163)   57.7 (196)
//      2.00     61.2  (49)   58.9  (56)   56.5  (62)   58.6  (70)   55.4  (83)
//      2.25     78.3  (23)   74.1  (27)   66.7  (30)   64.7  (34)   57.9  (38)
//
//  A RIDGE, NOT A SPIKE - monotone down every column as volume rises and across every row
//  as the extension tightens. That is the shape that separates this from the rejected
//  tests, which inverted between neighbouring cells. 1.75 / 0.10 is where the sample is
//  still honest; 2.25 scores better on 27 bars and is not worth believing yet.
//
//  THE ANOMALY EXCLUSION IS NOT COSMETIC - IT IS THE WHOLE FINDING. On M1 at 1.75 / 0.10:
//      NOT also an anomaly   121   57.0% / 62.0% / 57.9%   +0.53 / +0.62 / +1.01
//      also an ANOMALY bar    45   31.1% / 37.8% / 40.0%   -0.32 / -0.62 / -0.55
//      the two together      166   50.0% / 55.4% / 53.0%
//  A 26-point split, and lumping them gives a coin - which is exactly what my first run
//  reported before the filter went in. The reading is consistent with what this file
//  already knows: a loud bar that goes nowhere at a LOW is someone large still selling
//  (the anomaly measured 62.5% FALLING), while the same bar without that signature is
//  supply being absorbed. Same two ways to be quiet that the campaign rules distinguish.
//
//  ⚠️ IT IS THE M1 EDGE, AND IT NEEDS THE M1 HOLD. On M5 the same test peaks at 1.60 and
//  falls apart by 2.00 (50.0% on 24), and its 5-bar column goes negative. M1 pays at 1, 3
//  and 5 bars alike - which is the 3-to-5-bar hold the M1 preset note already warns is
//  mandatory. Taking this with an M5 exit habit throws the edge away.
//  ABOUT 3 A DAY on M1, 1.4 on M5.
showRev  = input.bool(true, "Mark REVERSAL EXPECTED - loud red bar that goes nowhere", group = grpRv, display = display.none)
revVolX  = input.float(1.75, "...its volume >= x the average volume", step = 0.05, group = grpRv, display = display.none)
revExtX  = input.float(0.10, "...and it got <= x an average range past the last low", step = 0.05, group = grpRv, display = display.none)

grpDv = "RSI divergence"
//  MEASURED BEFORE IT WAS BUILT, AND MOST OF IT DIED. Zee, 2026-09-28: "the rsi indicator
//  in tradingview calculates divergence.. i think divergence helps us.. can u check?" and
//  then "there is something called hidden divergence. can we make a label out of it?"
//
//  THE ALGORITHM IS TRADINGVIEW'S OWN Divergence Indicator, ported exactly - because that
//  is the thing on his chart. It pivots on the RSI (not on price), compares the PRICE at
//  those same pivot bars, and needs them 5-60 bars apart. No lookahead: a pivot confirms
//  dvPiv bars late, so every figure below is measured from the CONFIRMATION bar's close.
//
//  FOUR SIGNALS, 43,434 M1 bars, 53 days, base rate 49.6% for a higher close:
//      signal                      n     /day    1 bar    3 bars   5 bars   10 bars
//      REGULAR BULLISH (says UP)  341     6.4    46.9%    48.5%    51.2%    53.2%
//      HIDDEN  BULLISH (says UP)  329     6.2    46.5%    50.8%    50.8%    45.3%
//        control: other RSI lows 2251    42.5    49.4%    48.5%    49.8%    48.6%
//      REGULAR BEARISH (says DN)  330     6.2    46.7%    48.8%    49.4%    47.3%
//      HIDDEN  BEARISH (says DN)  346     6.5    41.9%    46.0%    49.4%    50.7%
//        control: other RSI highs 2229   42.1    48.8%    49.4%    50.3%    51.0%
//
//  ⛔ HIDDEN DIVERGENCE MEASURES NOTHING, AND THAT IS THE ANSWER TO THE QUESTION HE ASKED.
//  Bare it is a coin. With the trend filter the textbook always attaches (it is a
//  CONTINUATION signal, so it only counts in the direction of the prevailing trend) it is
//  still a coin - 287 samples above the EMA50 at 44.9% / 49.8% / 49.5% / 43.9%. Narrowed
//  further to trend + pullback it is 53 samples and sawtooths. And the RIDGE CHECK settles
//  it: across pivot lookbacks 3 / 4 / 5 / 7 / 9 the hidden-bearish 3-bar reading runs
//  50.5 / 48.0 / 46.0 / 50.0 / 55.7 - neighbouring settings disagree by 10 points, which
//  is this file's standing signature of noise. Same for hidden bullish.
//  It is offered OFF below so it can be watched by eye, marked with plotchar so it costs
//  nothing from the label budget. It is NOT a label and it claims nothing.
//
//  ✅ ONE CELL SURVIVED, and only one: REGULAR BULLISH at an OVERSOLD 20-BAR LOW.
//  His note is right that location matters - "divergence inside a random consolidation
//  range is noise" - and it is right that the first RSI low must be under 30. Applying
//  both, and nothing else:
//      group                                        n    /day   1bar   3bar   5bar  10bar
//      A  DIVERGENCE + oversold + at the 20-bar low  60    1.1  40.0%  61.7% 58.3%  68.3%
//      B  control: RSI pivot low, same place, no div 61    1.2  52.5%  54.1% 54.1%  54.1%
//      C  control: ANY oversold bar at that low    1998   37.7  50.6%  52.6% 52.3%  55.9%
//      D  base rate: every bar                    43409  819.0  49.6%  49.5% 49.3%  49.5%
//  ...and as average move: A pays +0.45 / +0.33 / +0.80 where C pays +0.22 / +0.31 / +0.65.
//
//  ⚠️ READ THE CONTROL HONESTLY - THE LOCATION IS DOING MOST OF THE WORK. Simply being an
//  oversold bar at a 20-bar low is worth +0.65 by ten bars on 1,998 samples. The
//  divergence adds about 12 points of hit rate and 0.15 points of move on 60 samples,
//  which is roughly one and a half standard errors. This is a REFINEMENT of a location
//  this chart already marks with the ▲ hint, not a new edge.
//  ⛔ AND IT IS NEGATIVE AT ONE BAR - 40.0%, the worst cell in the row. The confirmation
//  bar's close is the final flush; the move is in bars 3 to 10. That is the M1 hold this
//  file already insists on, and taking this signal with a one-bar exit habit loses.
//  ✅ IT SPLITS: first half 63.0% / 74.1% at 3 and 10 bars, second half 60.6% / 63.6%.
//  Both halves agree in sign and both beat the control. On 27 and 33 samples.
//
//  ⛔ NO BEARISH MIRROR, and the reason is one this file has now measured three times.
//      A  divergence + overbought + at the 20-bar high  71  43.7% / 53.5% / 56.3%  +0.03/-0.13/+0.36
//      B  control: the same place, no divergence        57  47.4% / 47.4% / 49.1%  -0.41/+0.35/+0.89
//  The control BEATS it on average move at 10 and 20 bars. That is the standing asymmetry
//  from the ▲ hint study - "at the 20-bar LOW is -7.3 against the base, at the HIGH only
//  +2.3: lows bounce, highs do not reverse" - showing up for the third time.
//
//  NO ANOMALY FILTER NEEDED, and that was checked because it has been the deciding term
//  twice here (REVERSAL EXPECTED, and the ▲ hint). ZERO of the 60 land on an anomaly bar:
//  an RSI pivot low confirmed five bars later is never a squat. The filter is a no-op, not
//  a help.
//  ⚠️ PROVENANCE: a PYTHON study over the OANDA M1 table. NOT a backtest under CLAUDE.md -
//  no entry, stop, target, spread or slippage, and no P&L is claimed. It is a hypothesis
//  with a control attached, which is the most this evidence supports.
//  ABOUT ONE A DAY on M1.
showDiv   = input.bool(true, "Mark BULLISH RSI DIVERGENCE at an oversold low", group = grpDv, display = display.none)
dvRsiN    = input.int(14, "RSI length", minval = 2, group = grpDv, display = display.none)
dvPiv     = input.int(5, "Bars each side that define an RSI pivot", minval = 1, group = grpDv, display = display.none)
dvRangeLo = input.int(5, "The two pivots at least this far apart", minval = 1, group = grpDv, display = display.none)
dvRangeHi = input.int(60, "...and at most this far", minval = 2, group = grpDv, display = display.none)
//  HIS CONDITION, AND IT IS LOAD-BEARING: bare regular-bullish is 341 samples at 48.5%,
//  and this is what lifts the surviving cell. "The first RSI low MUST be inside the
//  oversold zone."
dvOversold = input.float(30, "...and the FIRST low's RSI below this", step = 1, group = grpDv, display = display.none)
//  THE LOCATION TEST, in this chart's own terms. His note says "major HTF support, demand
//  zone or key Fibonacci level"; none of those has ever measured here, and the 20-bar
//  extreme is the one location test that has (the ▲ hint, 578 bars, -7.3 against base).
dvPosX    = input.float(0.25, "...and the bar sits in the bottom x of its 20-bar range", step = 0.05, group = grpDv, display = display.none)
//  OFF, because it measured flat in every form - see the tables above. Turn it on to watch
//  it by eye for a fortnight; plotchar costs nothing from the 500-label budget.
dvHidden  = input.bool(false, "...also mark HIDDEN divergence (measured FLAT - see note)", group = grpDv, display = display.none)

grpVd = "Volume delta"
//  AGGRESSIVE BUYING AGAINST AGGRESSIVE SELLING. Zee, 2026-09-28: "use the information
//  from volume delta indicator in tradingview to create labels from the following:
//    [ Price UP + Positive Delta ]   --> Healthy Trend (Buyers Aggressive)
//    [ Price UP + Negative Delta ]   --> Bearish Absorption (Limit Sellers Capping Price)
//    [ Price DOWN + Negative Delta ] --> Healthy Trend (Sellers Aggressive)
//    [ Price DOWN + Positive Delta ] --> Bullish Absorption (Limit Buyers Floor Price)"
//
//  ⚠️ FIRST, WHAT DELTA ACTUALLY IS HERE, because the definition in the note is not what
//  any chart can compute. "Volume at ask minus volume at bid" needs the tape, and neither
//  TradingView nor this feed has it. TradingView's own Volume Delta indicator takes
//  INTRABAR bars from a lower timeframe and calls each one buying or selling by its OWN
//  close-against-open, then sums the volume. That is what this computes, with
//  request.security_lower_tf - the same convention this file already uses for candle
//  colour. It is an approximation, not a measurement of who crossed the spread.
//  ⚠️ AND IT NEEDS LOWER-TIMEFRAME DATA. On an M5 chart the intrabars are M1 and this is
//  solid. On an M1 chart it needs SECONDS data, which is a paid TradingView tier and may
//  not exist for this symbol - the reading then goes blank rather than guessing, and the
//  panel row says so.
//
//  ⛔ ALL FOUR OF HIS QUADRANTS MEASURED BACKWARDS OR FLAT. M5 built from its own M1
//  intrabars, 8,680 bars, 53 days, base rate 49.99% for the next close being higher:
//      quadrant                              n     says   next up   3-bar   5-bar
//      A  price UP + POSITIVE delta        3487    UP     49.70%   49.76%  49.13%
//      B  price UP + NEGATIVE delta         833    DOWN   52.10%   50.90%  49.22%
//      C  price DOWN + NEGATIVE delta      3537    DOWN   50.07%   49.02%  48.37%
//      D  price DOWN + POSITIVE delta       784    UP     48.60%   46.05%  45.54%
//      BASE                                8649           49.99%   49.24%  48.53%
//  A and C - the two "healthy trend" readings - are coins to within a third of a point on
//  3,500 samples each. B says DOWN and measures UP. D says UP and measures DOWN.
//
//  ✅ ONE CELL IS REAL, AND IT IS THE ONE THAT CONTRADICTS THE NOTE HARDEST. Quadrant D -
//  "Bullish Absorption, limit buyers floor the price, go LONG" - is the most BEARISH cell
//  in the table: -1.4 / -3.2 / -3.0 points against the base at 1 / 3 / 5 bars on 784
//  samples, and it REPLICATES on M15 (46.50% against a 49.35% base on 286). Two
//  timeframes, same sign, growing with the horizon: that is the ridge this file trusts,
//  and it points the opposite way to the label he was given.
//  WHY, AND IT IS THIS FILE'S OWN PRINCIPLE: aggressive buying that FAILED to lift price
//  is effort without result, and effort without result means the other side is in
//  control. It is the ANOMALY reading exactly - "an ANOMALY bar at the 20-bar low FELL
//  62.5% of the time" - and the capping logic, arriving through a different instrument.
//  The textbook calls it absorption by limit buyers; the measurement says the buyers were
//  run over.
//  B is the mirror and is weaker: selling into a rise that failed measures 52.10% UP at
//  one bar, fading to 49.22% by five. Shown, with its number.
//
//  ⛔ AND THE OTHER TWO READINGS IN THE NOTE DID NOT SURVIVE EITHER:
//      DELTA CLIMAX (|delta| >= 3x its 20-bar average)  309 bars  56.63% up on M5
//        ...but 48.59% on M15, against a 49.35% base. It inverts between timeframes, so
//        it is noise - the same test the upthrust and the spring failed.
//      DELTA DIVERGENCE (price lower low, delta higher)  2,122 bars  49.81% - a coin.
//        The mirror, 2,121 bars, 50.97%. Neither moves.
//  Neither is drawn.
//
//  ⚠️ PROVENANCE: a PYTHON study aggregating the OANDA M1 table into M5 and M15, which is
//  EXACTLY the computation the Pine performs (an M5 bar decomposed into its M1 intrabars).
//  NOT a backtest under CLAUDE.md - no entry, stop, target, spread or slippage.
showDelta = input.bool(true, "Volume delta - the quadrant, on the panel", group = grpVd, display = display.none)
vdMode    = input.string("AUTO", "Intrabar precision", options = ["AUTO", "1S", "5S", "1", "5"], group = grpVd, display = display.none)
//  ONLY THE TWO DISAGREEING QUADRANTS GET A LABEL. A and C fire 66 times a day each on M5
//  and measure nothing - a text label that often is wallpaper, the failure this file has
//  named half a dozen times. They are still reported, on the panel row, every bar.
vdLabel   = input.bool(true, "...and label the two DISAGREEING bars on the chart", group = grpVd, display = display.none)
//  ⛔ 0 = NO SIZE FILTER, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. This shipped at 1.0, which was a bug of
//  a subtle kind: the label quoted quadrant D's measured numbers (784 samples) while only
//  drawing on the subset whose |delta| cleared its own average. Re-measured at the
//  threshold that actually fired, the subset does not merely differ - it INVERTS between
//  timeframes and the finding disappears:
//      quadrant D (price DOWN, delta POSITIVE)     n    next up   3-bar   5-bar
//      M5   no filter                             784    48.60%  46.05%  45.54%
//      M5   |delta| >= 1.0x its 20-bar average    105    50.48%  45.71%  46.67%
//      M15  no filter                             286    46.50%  49.65%  49.30%
//      M15  |delta| >= 1.0x                        53    58.49%  54.72%  56.60%
//  The unfiltered cell is the one that replicates across both timeframes; the big-delta
//  subset reads 50.5% on M5 and 58.5% on M15, which is a 8-point disagreement on ~100
//  samples. So the shipped filter is OFF, the labelled bars ARE the measured population,
//  and the number on the label describes the bar it is attached to.
//  ⚠️ THE COST IS DENSITY: about 15 labels a day per side on M5, 30 in total. They are
//  tiny and they are the only two quadrants that carry anything. Raise this to thin them
//  out - but the quoted figures then stop applying, which is exactly the bug this note
//  records.
vdBigX    = input.float(0.0, "...only when |delta| >= x its own 20-bar average (0 = all)", step = 0.25, minval = 0, group = grpVd, display = display.none)

grpNr = "Not a retracement"
//  THE RED CANDLE THAT NEVER GOT ANYWHERE. Zee, 2026-09-28: "when we're in an uptrend
//  (confirmed by camel humps), then when moving up. if we encounter a red candle which
//  doesnot break below the previous green candle's low with its body. then we can label
//  this weak red candle as: Not retracement. also test this one so we know if the next
//  candle forms green always or by which percentage in this case."
//
//  HIS CONDITION, EXACTLY: the compass says UPTREND, this candle is RED, the one before it
//  is GREEN, and the red candle's BODY BOTTOM (its close) stays at or above that green
//  candle's LOW. The trend is the real camel-hump compass - cmTrend, computed above from
//  the same fractal swings, three-peak slant and guard low that drive the arrow.
//
//  ⛔ HE ASKED FOR THE NUMBER AND THE NUMBER IS A COIN. 43,434 M1 bars, 53 days, against a
//  49.51% base rate for the next candle being GREEN:
//      group                                       n    /day  NEXT GRN  next up  3-bar  5-bar
//      A  HIS SIGNAL: uptrend + body holds       3072    58.0    50.9%    51.1%  49.6%  50.3%
//      B  control: uptrend, body BREAKS below    1310    24.7    49.3%    49.8%  48.6%  48.7%
//      C  control: body holds, NO uptrend        4426    83.5    47.7%    47.9%  49.0%  48.7%
//      D  control: body holds, any trend         7498   141.5    49.0%    49.2%  49.2%  49.3%
//      red-after-green, compass UPTREND          4382    82.7    50.4%    50.7%  49.3%  49.8%
//      every red-after-green                    10870   205.1    49.2%    49.4%  49.3%  49.4%
//  THE NEXT CANDLE IS GREEN 50.9% OF THE TIME. That is 1.4 points over the base on 3,072
//  samples - about one and a half standard errors, and it is gone by three bars (49.6%).
//  Average move +0.016 / -0.002 / +0.113 points at 1 / 3 / 5 bars: nothing.
//
//  AND THE BODY RULE ITSELF ADDS ALMOST NOTHING. Red-after-green in an uptrend is already
//  50.4%; adding "the body held above the last low" moves it to 50.9%. Half a point. What
//  little lift exists belongs to the COMPASS - row C, the same body rule with no uptrend
//  behind it, scores 47.7%, BELOW the base.
//
//  ⛔ AND M5 INVERTS IT, which is the finding that settles it. There the signal scores
//  49.1% against a 49.95% base while the CONTROL - the red candle whose body DID break
//  below - scores 55.1%. The opposite condition wins by six points on the other timeframe.
//  A real structural effect does not reverse when the bars are five times longer; that is
//  this file's standing signature of noise, the same one that rejected the upthrust, the
//  spring and hidden divergence.
//
//  SO IT SHIPS AS DESCRIPTION, NOT AS A SIGNAL - the same standing as NO SUPPLY, which
//  also names a real condition and predicts nothing (51.4% on 352 samples). It is telling
//  you a true fact about STRUCTURE: this pullback never took out the last low, so the
//  up-leg is still intact. That is worth seeing while scalping inside a trend, and it
//  pairs with the ⏳ extended-run reminder, which says "wait for a retracement" - this one
//  says "that red candle was not one". It makes NO claim about the next candle beyond the
//  50.9% above.
//  ⚠️ 58 MARKS A DAY ON M1. Drawn with plotshape, not label.new, so it costs nothing from
//  the 500-label budget the setup labels need - but it is still text on a busy chart.
//  Turn it off here if it crowds.
showNotRetr = input.bool(true, "Mark NOT A RETRACEMENT (description - measured flat)", group = grpNr, display = display.none)
//  HIS RULE IS THE BODY. The whole-candle version is offered because it is the stricter
//  reading of the same idea, and it measured no better (2,317 samples, 49.8% next green).
nrUseWick   = input.bool(false, "...require the whole candle to hold, not just the body", group = grpNr, display = display.none)

grpBk = "Level broken"
//  A CONFIRMED LOW GETS TAKEN OUT. Zee, 2026-09-25: "if the price breaks a prior confirmed
//  low.. we can label it and say ⚠️ Low Broken and on hover: expect Downwards".
//  THE LABEL IS HIS. THE ARROW IS THE MEASUREMENT'S, AND THEY DISAGREE.
//  Tested over 5,188 bars, 19 sessions, against a 50.3% base rate for the next close
//  being lower - one label per level, not one per bar spent beyond it:
//
//      any break of a confirmed low          249   47.0% fell   (-3.3)
//        ...and CLOSED below it              137   45.3% fell   (-5.0)
//        ...closed below, volume >= 1.0x      78   46.2% fell   (-4.1)
//        ...closed below, volume <  1.0x      59   44.1% fell   (-6.2)
//        ...wicked through, closed back      112   49.1% fell   (-1.1)
//
//  A broken low is followed by a HIGHER close 54.7% of the time, and it holds at three
//  bars (-3.1) and six (-5.3). The expectation runs the other way from the request.
//
//  THE LOW-VOLUME ROW IS THE MECHANISM, and it is Zee's own law read one step further.
//  Line 35 already says a support break "with a wick, closing above the support line" is a
//  strong BUY sign - the fake break this script marks in purple. The measurement says the
//  recovery is not required: a low taken out on volume BELOW average is a break nobody is
//  supporting, and it gets bought back whether or not the close made it home. -6.2 / -5.5
//  / -8.0 across the three horizons, monotone in the direction of "quieter is more likely
//  to reverse", which is the shape of a real effect rather than a lucky bucket.
//
//  IT AGREES WITH TWO THINGS ALREADY ON THE CHART:
//      an ordinary bar at the 20-bar LOW   42.7% fell   - lows get bought
//      an ANOMALY bar at the 20-bar low    62.5% fell   - unless someone large is there
//  So this label stands DOWN on an anomaly bar, exactly as the ▲ hint does, and for the
//  same reason: the anomaly is what separates a low that bounces from one that breaks.
//
//  NO MIRROR FOR HIGHS. The same test on resistance measured +0.7 / +0.9 / -1.0 on 117
//  samples - flat. Highs broken say nothing here, which is the same asymmetry the ▲ hint
//  found (lows bounce, highs do not reverse), so only the low side gets a label.
//
//  SAMPLE: 137 for the headline figure. Enough to act on the direction, not enough to
//  lean on the exact number.
showBroke  = input.bool(true, "Mark a confirmed LOW being broken", group = grpBk, display = display.none)
brokeQuietX = input.float(1.00, "Call it UNSUPPORTED when volume <= x the reference", step = 0.05, group = grpBk, display = display.none)
//  AGAINST THE LAST BREAK, NOT AGAINST THE AVERAGE. Zee, 2026-09-26: "it should show
//  whether the last low was broken at a lower volume than the candle that broke the low
//  last time. and usually when a low is broken it goes further down".
//  This is the TEST OF A LEVEL applied to breaks - the same correction he already made
//  me apply to the reaction ("compare it to the LAST CANDLE of the campaign") and to the
//  capping rule. A 20-bar average is a fact about the last hour; the last break is a fact
//  about THIS fight, and it is the one the eye is actually making.
//
//  MEASURED on the same 19 sessions, 270 M5 breaks, base rate 50.0% for a lower close:
//
//      yardstick                         n     next close LOWER at 1 / 3 / 6
//      vol <= 20-bar average  (old)     126    47.6   44.4   50.8
//      vol >  20-bar average            144    51.4   50.0   50.0     spread 3.8
//
//      QUIETER than the last break      136    44.1   41.9   52.2
//      LOUDER than the last break       133    55.6   53.4   48.9     spread 11.5
//
//  HIS YARDSTICK IS THREE TIMES SHARPER, and it splits the two cases in OPPOSITE
//  directions rather than grading one of them: a low taken out on LESS effort than the
//  last one gets bought back (55.9% higher), and one taken out on MORE effort keeps
//  going (55.6% lower). The old test could only ever say "up, a bit more or a bit less".
//
//  ⚠️ IT IS M5 ONLY, AND THAT IS THE REASON TO KEEP THE OLD OPTION. On M1 the same test
//  over 1,399 breaks - five times the sample - measures 49.5% against 49.9%. Nothing.
//  An effect real at one resolution and absent at five times the data is not yet proved;
//  11.5 points on 135-a-side is under two standard errors. Ships as the default because
//  it is the better-reasoned measurement and it beats the incumbent where both were
//  taken, NOT because the sample settles it. Watch it for a fortnight.
brokeVolRef = input.string("the LAST break of a low", "Break volume measured against", options = ["the LAST break of a low", "the 20-bar average"], group = grpBk, display = display.none)

grpW   = "Why not?"
//  Zee, 2026-09-20: "i still dont see the 12:10 setup labelled". Rather than keep
//  diagnosing from my copy of the OANDA table - which may not be the bar he is looking at
//  at all - this puts the verdict on HIS chart. Every bar that turns against a run of
//  minRun or more, but does not become a setup, says which gate refused it and with what
//  numbers. A disagreement then points at a specific figure instead of a mystery.
showWhy = input.bool(false, "Explain every bar that ALMOST qualified", group = grpW, display = display.none)

grpD   = "Drawing"
showRun  = input.bool(false, "Arrow every bar in a 2+ run", group = grpD, display = display.none)
showAll  = input.bool(true, "Mark reactions that never confirmed (only when confirmation is ON)", group = grpD, display = display.none)
//  OFF by default, 2026-09-21. Zee: "can you make the table on the top right go away?"
//  The funnel answered one question - how often does this pattern appear, and which gate
//  costs the most setups - and that question is now answered and written down. It is a
//  tuning instrument, not a trading one: nothing in it changes what to do with the bar in
//  front of you, and it sat over the price. Turn it back on from Settings -> Drawing when
//  a gate is being swept again.
showTbl  = input.bool(false, "Show the counter", group = grpD, display = display.none)

// ---- KEEPING THE LABELS NARROW ----------------------------------------------------------
//  Zee, 2026-09-20: "some labels become too wide with text. can u break those label texts
//  into a multi-line label so each printed text is max 4 words wide".
//  Pine does no wrapping of its own - a label is exactly as wide as its longest line - and
//  these labels grew line by line until they covered the candles they were describing.
//  This splits every line at a word count, leaving the explicit "\n" breaks alone so the
//  structure of the label (headline, then one fact per line) survives.
wrapWords = input.int(4, "Max words per line in a label", minval = 2, maxval = 12, group = "Drawing", display = display.none)

//  ...OR PUT THE DETAIL ON HOVER INSTEAD. Zee, 2026-09-20: "is it possible that labels are
//  short and then we HOVER over them to extend the label to full text?"
//  Yes - label.new takes a tooltip, which TradingView shows on mouseover. So the chart
//  carries only the headline (BUY SETUP / PRICE CAPPED / FAKE BREAK) and every reason,
//  measurement and caution moves into the tooltip. The chart stays readable at a glance and
//  the full reasoning is one hover away, which is the better division: the label answers
//  "is there something here?", the tooltip answers "why?".
//  With this ON the wrapping above barely matters, since a headline is already short; it
//  still applies to the tooltip text so that does not run away either.
compact = input.bool(true, "Short labels - full text on HOVER", group = "Drawing", display = display.none)

//  AND STOP THEM SITTING ON TOP OF EACH OTHER. Zee, 2026-09-20: "the 6:55 PM candle on
//  Friday.. has two labels.. one dark green and one light green. is it possible to
//  rearrange the two labels so they're not overlapping".
//  Several of these can land on the SAME bar - PRICE CAPPED fires on the capped bar and
//  the BUY/SELL SETUP can fire on that same bar, and both anchor at the candle's low with
//  label_up, so the second one covers the first. Each kind of label now gets its own lane,
//  pushed away from the candle by a multiple of the average range, so they stack in a
//  readable column instead of a pile. Measured in ranges rather than points so the spacing
//  holds whatever the instrument or the zoom.
labelGap = input.float(0.8, "Gap between stacked labels (x average range)", step = 0.1, minval = 0, group = "Drawing", display = display.none)

// ---- THE PRESET, APPLIED ------------------------------------------------------------
//  Declared HERE, after every input it can override, and it feeds the names the rest of
//  the file already uses - so nothing downstream knows a preset exists.
//  MANUAL passes the dialog's own values straight through, which is what makes the four
//  fields still meaningful: pick MANUAL and they are yours again.
isM1 = presetTF == "M1 - tuned"
isManual = presetTF == "MANUAL - use my own values below"
minRun     = isManual ? minRunIn     : isM1 ? 4    : 2
minRunSell = isManual ? minRunSellIn : isM1 ? 5    : 3
relX       = isManual ? relXIn       : isM1 ? 0.50 : 0.70
wickX      = isManual ? wickXIn      : isM1 ? 0.30 : 0.15

//  A LOUD REMINDER THAT THE PRESET DOES NOT MATCH THE CHART. Picking the M1 preset on an
//  M5 chart (or the reverse) is silent otherwise - every gate quietly moves and the labels
//  look normal. This is the one case where a warning on the price is worth the space.
presetWrong = not isManual and (isM1 != (timeframe.isintraday and timeframe.multiplier == 1))
if presetWrong and barstate.islast
    var table pw = table.new(position.top_center, 1, 1, bgcolor = color.new(color.orange, 0))
    table.cell(pw, 0, 0, "⚠️ PRESET IS " + (isM1 ? "M1" : "M5") + " BUT THIS CHART IS " + timeframe.period + " - the gates are tuned for the other one", text_color = color.white, text_size = size.small)

wrapN(src) =>
    parts = str.split(src, "\n")
    res   = ""
    for i = 0 to array.size(parts) - 1
        words = str.split(array.get(parts, i), " ")
        acc = ""
        n   = 0
        for j = 0 to array.size(words) - 1
            w = array.get(words, j)
            //  the strings use double spaces as separators, which split into empty
            //  tokens - they must not count towards the four
            if str.length(w) > 0
                acc := acc + (n == 0 ? "" : (n % wrapWords == 0 ? "\n" : " ")) + w
                n += 1
        res := res + (i == 0 ? "" : "\n") + acc
    res

//  THE BATTERY, drawn from a level of 1 to 5. Declared up here with the other helpers -
//  Pine only allows function declarations at global scope.
batBar(lvl) =>
    lvl <= 1 ? "▮▯▯▯▯" : lvl == 2 ? "▮▮▯▯▯" : lvl == 3 ? "▮▮▮▯▯" : lvl == 4 ? "▮▮▮▮▯" : "▮▮▮▮▮"

//  the quintile cuts, measured - see the table at the showBat input
batLvl(x) =>
    na(x) ? 0 : x < 0.461 ? 1 : x < 0.769 ? 2 : x < 1.085 ? 3 : x < 1.530 ? 4 : 5

//  TICK MODE HAS ITS OWN CUTS, measured on 95,902 readings of real PXBT ticks. They are
//  nothing like the candle ones (3.02 / 3.82 / 4.67 / 5.93 against 0.46 / 0.77 / 1.09 /
//  1.53) because a tick window's GROSS one-way movement is about 4.2x a candle's NET body
//  per unit of effort. Judged by the candle cuts, 99% of tick readings land in the top
//  band - see the note at batMode.
batLvlTick(x) =>
    na(x) ? 0 : x < 3.023 ? 1 : x < 3.821 ? 2 : x < 4.674 ? 3 : x < 5.925 ? 4 : 5

//  ONE ROW OF THE CANDLE PANEL. Zee, 2026-09-28: "i need all the bullets as rows. and
//  each row reads something about the next candle. next: up / next: down .. also don't
//  skip the measured backwards things. include them as well. i need a concrete decision
//  on each line of whether up or down."
//  THE CALL ON EVERY ROW IS THE MEASURED LEAN, NOT THE TEXTBOOK ONE - which is why seven
//  of the fifteen now say the opposite of the note he sent. See the table at showCandle.
caRow(lbl, fires, up, pct) =>
    lbl + (fires ? (up ? "   next: UP  " : "   next: DOWN  ") + pct : "   -")

batWord(lvl) =>
    lvl <= 1 ? "grinding" : lvl == 2 ? "heavy going" : lvl == 3 ? "ordinary" : lvl == 4 ? "moving well" : "gliding"

//  THE AVERAGE VOLUME OF THE RUN THAT LED INTO A BAR `off` BARS AGO.
//  Used by the test-of-a-level route to remember how much effort the LAST visit to a
//  level cost, so this visit can be compared with it. `up` picks which kind of run to
//  walk back through - an up-run into a top, a down-run into a bottom.
campVolAt(off, up) =>
    n = 0
    tot = 0.0
    for k = off to off + 7
        d = close[k] > open[k] ? 1 : close[k] < open[k] ? -1 : 0
        if (d > 0) != up or d == 0
            break
        n += 1
        tot += volume[k]
    n > 0 ? tot / n : 0.0

// ---- the bar's own direction, TradingView convention --------------------------------
dir = close > open ? 1 : close < open ? -1 : 0

// ---- find the run that ENDED on the previous bar -------------------------------------
// this bar goes the other way, so the run behind it is a candidate campaign
runDir  = dir[1]
runLen  = 0
runVol  = 0.0
runPeak = 0.0
runHi   = 0.0
runLo   = 1e12
if dir != 0 and runDir != 0 and dir != runDir
    for k = 1 to maxRun
        d = close[k] > open[k] ? 1 : close[k] < open[k] ? -1 : 0
        if d != runDir
            break
        runLen  += 1
        runVol  += volume[k]
        runPeak := math.max(runPeak, volume[k])
        //  the extreme the run REACHED - needed by the test-of-a-level route, which has
        //  to know whether this run came back to the same price as the last one
        runHi   := math.max(runHi, high[k])
        runLo   := math.min(runLo, low[k])

campAvg = runLen > 0 ? runVol / runLen : 0.0

// ---- WAS THE CAMPAIGN'S LAST CANDLE CAPPED? --------------------------------------------
//  Computed HERE, before the reaction is judged, because it decides whether a LOUD
//  reaction is allowed at all. Zee, 2026-09-20: "please note that here the reaction
//  candle's volume CAN BE LARGER than the campaign's last volume.. its the ONLY case where
//  we can allow a larger volume (instead of the usual lower volume case)".
//  Line 250 spells out the negative: "the red reaction candle has a large volume, IF
//  previous candles were NOT end of rising market.. then we would be CAUTIOUS that there's
//  some demand.. if there were no wick on top of the red reaction candle then we would say
//  we have a demand still WE WOULD WAIT."
avgRange = ta.sma(high - low, lookN)
volSma   = ta.sma(volume, lookN)
//  prevCapped is finished further down, once the run's own widest bar is known - it has to
//  follow the same cappedRef choice as cappedRun or the END OF RISING MARKET headline would
//  disagree with the capping that admitted the setup.

//  ...AND ANYWHERE ELSE IN THE CAMPAIGN, not only on its last candle.
//  Zee, 2026-09-20: "but the second last bar before the 12:10 AM bar was capping.. can u
//  check?" He was right and the rule was too narrow. On 18 Sep 22:10 the campaign ran
//  21:55 / 22:00 / 22:05, and 21:55 is capped on the script's own thresholds - spread 0.67
//  of average on 1.31x volume - but sat three bars back where nothing looked. The supply
//  that caps a rise does not have to land on the very last candle before the turn.
//  prevCapped is kept separately because line 250's end-of-rising SEQUENCE specifically
//  needs the capped bar to be the last one, with the reaction right after it.
//  The best candidate is remembered even when it fails, so the Why-not label can show how
//  close it came. On 18 Sep 22:10 the capping bar cleared the volume test by ONE PERCENT
//  in my own check and landed just under it on the chart, because Pine's rolling average
//  covers a slightly different window - a knife-edge that looked like a flat "no".
//  CAMPAIGN mode needs the run's own widest range first, so each bar can be measured
//  against the move it belongs to rather than against a 20-bar window that predates it.
runWidest = 0.0
if runLen > 0
    for k = 1 to runLen
        runWidest := math.max(runWidest, high[k] - low[k])
campMode = cappedRef == "the CAMPAIGN's own candles"

cappedRun = false
capBestSpread = 99.0
capBestBody   = 99.0
capBestVol    = 0.0
if runLen > 0
    for k = 1 to runLen
        //  the yardstick: the 20-bar average, or the widest bar of this run
        yard = campMode ? runWidest : avgRange[k]
        //  in CAMPAIGN mode the thresholds are relative to the run's widest bar, so they
        //  use cappedRelX rather than the average-based eorSpread / eorBody
        //  The BODY limit must stay tighter than the spread limit, or CAMPAIGN mode is no
        //  gate at all: almost every bar has a body under 70% of its run's widest RANGE,
        //  so a single shared 0.70 would make cappedRun true nearly always. The average
        //  mode already encodes the right relationship (0.35 body against 0.70 spread), so
        //  campaign mode keeps that same ratio off one knob instead of adding a second.
        sLim = campMode ? cappedRelX : eorSpread
        bLim = campMode ? cappedRelX * (eorBody / eorSpread) : eorBody
        sR = yard > 0 ? (high[k] - low[k]) / yard : 99.0
        bR = yard > 0 ? math.abs(close[k] - open[k]) / yard : 99.0
        vR = volSma[k] > 0 ? volume[k] / volSma[k] : 0.0
        if (sR <= sLim or bR <= bLim) and vR >= eorVol
            cappedRun := true
        if math.min(sR, bR) < math.min(capBestSpread, capBestBody)
            capBestSpread := sR
            capBestBody   := bR
            capBestVol    := vR

//  ...and the campaign's LAST candle, on the same yardstick. Line 250's end-of-rising
//  SEQUENCE specifically needs the capped bar to be the one right before the reaction.
prevYard = campMode ? runWidest : avgRange[1]
prevSLim = campMode ? cappedRelX : eorSpread
prevBLim = campMode ? cappedRelX * (eorBody / eorSpread) : eorBody
prevCapped = prevYard > 0 and ((high[1] - low[1]) <= prevYard * prevSLim or math.abs(close[1] - open[1]) <= prevYard * prevBLim) and volume[1] >= volSma[1] * eorVol

// ---- is it heavy? ---------------------------------------------------------------------
//  THE CAMPAIGN WAS INSIDE ITS OWN YARDSTICK. Found 2026-09-25 while chasing Zee's "the
//  volumes are all grey.. see? on the volume pine script".
//  ta.sma(volume, lookN)[1] averages the 20 bars ending ONE bar back - and the campaign IS
//  those last 2-8 bars. So a loud run raised the very average it was being judged against,
//  and the louder it was the harder it had to work. Self-defeating.
//  WORSE, THE TWO SCRIPTS DISAGREED. VSISA_Volume.pine has always used volAvgN[runLen],
//  which excludes the run properly. This file did not. So a bar could be grey in the volume
//  pane and refused here for different reasons, or coloured there and refused here - and
//  there was no way to tell from the chart which yardstick had spoken. The same drift the
//  two scripts were re-synced for on 2026-09-21, one level deeper.
//  Measured over 19 sessions:
//      current  (bug)     150 setups   58.0%   +0.91    falling-volume runs heavy:  9.3%
//      excl-run (fixed)   167 setups   57.5%   +0.88    falling-volume runs heavy: 14.2%
//  The 17 setups it adds are worth +0.61 apiece against a 0.88 average - marginal, and on
//  its own that is the profile of a band this file has refused before. IT SHIPS ANYWAY,
//  because this is not a threshold choice: a measurement must not include the thing it is
//  measuring. The quality is unchanged either way, so correctness decides it.
//  WHAT IT DOES NOT FIX: the yardstick is still a TRAILING average, so in a decaying
//  session it sits above the present and almost nothing can be heavy -
//      20-bar average volume RISING    538 runs   43.5% heavy
//      20-bar average volume FALLING   670 runs    9.3% heavy  ->  14.2% after this fix
//  A third of that gap closes; the rest is structural and is the same staleness Zee caught
//  in the capping rule on 2026-09-20. Broker hour 07:00 still scores 0.0% on 56 runs.
volOff   = excludeRun ? 1 + runLen : 1
volAvgN  = ta.sma(volume, lookN)[volOff]
volPeakN = ta.highest(volume, lookN)[volOff]
//  ta.median must be called on EVERY bar, never inside an `if`, or its history is built
//  only from the bars a branch happened to take - the same rule as every other ta.* here.
volMedN  = ta.median(volume, lookN)[volOff]
// ---- TEST OF A LEVEL: the second and third visit, on less effort ------------------------
//  Remembers the last confirmed swing high and low, AND how much volume the run into it
//  cost, so a later run reaching the same price can be compared with it.
//  These pivot calls are separate from the support/resistance ones further down, because
//  this has to be known BEFORE isCampaign is decided and Pine runs top to bottom.
var float prevTopLvl = na
var float prevTopVol = na
var int   prevTopBar = 0
var float prevBotLvl = na
var float prevBotVol = na
var int   prevBotBar = 0
tph = ta.pivothigh(high, pivLeft, pivRight)
tpl = ta.pivotlow(low, pivLeft, pivRight)
if not na(tph)
    prevTopLvl := tph
    prevTopVol := campVolAt(pivRight, true)
    prevTopBar := bar_index - pivRight
if not na(tpl)
    prevBotLvl := tpl
    prevBotVol := campVolAt(pivRight, false)
    prevBotBar := bar_index - pivRight

//  The remembered pivot must PREDATE this run, or the run would be compared against its
//  own top. A pivot confirms pivRight bars late so this is usually true already - the
//  guard is for the longer runs where it is not.
testTol  = avgRange * testTolX
testSell = runDir > 0 and not na(prevTopLvl) and prevTopVol > 0 and prevTopBar < bar_index - runLen and math.abs(runHi - prevTopLvl) <= testTol and campAvg <= prevTopVol * testVolX
testBuy  = runDir < 0 and not na(prevBotLvl) and prevBotVol > 0 and prevBotBar < bar_index - runLen and math.abs(runLo - prevBotLvl) <= testTol and campAvg <= prevBotVol * testVolX
isTest   = useTest and runLen > 0 and (testSell or testBuy)

heavyAbs = method == "AVERAGE of last N" ? campAvg >= volAvgN * avgX : method == "MEDIAN of last N (ignores one spike)" ? campAvg >= volMedN * medX : runPeak >= volPeakN * pkX
//  TWO WAYS TO BE A CAMPAIGN NOW. The absolute one - this run was loud against the last 20
//  bars. And the RELATIVE one - this run came back to a level it has been to before and
//  needed LESS to get there, which is the test Zee described. Off unless useTest is on.
heavy = heavyAbs or isTest
//  ONE MINIMUM PER DIRECTION. runDir > 0 means the campaign was BUYING, so the setup that
//  follows it is a SELL - and that is the side that needs the longer run. See the evidence
//  table at the minRunSell input.
//  With sellSoft on, a SELL is ADMITTED at the buy-side minimum and the shortfall is
//  carried into warnNow instead - see the evidence at the sellSoft input. minRunSell is
//  still the line between STRONG and WEAK; it just stops being the line between visible
//  and invisible.
minRunSellAdmit = sellSoft ? math.min(minRun, minRunSell) : minRunSell
minRunHere = runDir > 0 ? minRunSellAdmit : minRun
//  true when this sell only got in because of that softening - it must go orange
sellShort = runDir > 0 and runLen < minRunSell
isCampaign = runLen >= minRunHere and heavy and campAvg > 0
//  ...AND THE SAME TEST WITHOUT THE RETEST ROUTE, for everything that only DRAWS.
//  Zee, 2026-09-21: "toggling the test of a level setting also makes appear disappear
//  labels called: upper wick suggests selling". It does, and logically that is consistent -
//  a run that counts as a campaign should get its campaign readings. But FIVE things hang
//  off isCampaign, so flipping one switch under evaluation repainted four unrelated
//  decorations as well, and then there is no way to see what the route itself did.
//  While the route is off by default and being judged, the drawings stay pinned to the
//  ABSOLUTE test so the toggle changes exactly one thing: whether setups appear.
//  REVISIT ON PROMOTION - if the retest route earns its place, these should widen with it,
//  because a test-route campaign deserves its own context labels like any other.
isCampaignDraw = runLen >= minRunHere and heavyAbs and campAvg > 0

// ---- the reaction: THIS bar, opposite to the campaign ---------------------------------
rng      = high - low
lowWick  = math.min(open, close) - low
highWick = high - math.max(open, close)
wick     = runDir < 0 ? lowWick : highWick          // rejection against the campaign
quietBase = quietRef == "LAST bar of the campaign" ? volume[1] : quietRef == "campaign PEAK" ? runPeak : campAvg
quiet    = quietBase > 0 and volume <= quietBase * relX
strongImb = quietBase > 0 and volume <= quietBase * imbX
wicked   = useWick and rng > 0 and (wick / rng) >= wickX

// ---- HOW MANY CAMPAIGN BODIES DID THE REACTION SWALLOW? --------------------------------
//  Bodies only, not wicks - line 125 says "engulfed the BODY of previous candle". Counted
//  backwards from the nearest campaign bar and stopping at the first one it does not
//  cover, so "engulfs last 3" really means the three immediately behind it.
//  Computed HERE rather than down by the label, because with admitEngulf on it is no
//  longer just decoration - it can let a loud reaction through.
bodyTop = math.max(open, close)
bodyBot = math.min(open, close)
engulfN = 0
if runLen > 0
    for k = 1 to runLen
        if bodyTop >= math.max(open[k], close[k]) and bodyBot <= math.min(open[k], close[k])
            engulfN += 1
        else
            break

//  ...AND THE NON-CONSECUTIVE COUNT, which is what admission uses. Same body test, but it
//  keeps scanning past a bar it does not cover instead of stopping there.
engulfAnyN = 0
if runLen > 0
    for k = 1 to math.min(runLen, engulfScanN)
        if bodyTop >= math.max(open[k], close[k]) and bodyBot <= math.min(open[k], close[k])
            engulfAnyN += 1

//  TWO WAYS IN, AND THEY ARE NOT EQUAL.
//    QUIET  - the usual case. Low volume qualifies on its own; that IS the imbalance.
//    LOUD   - allowed ONLY with the capping AND the wick together. This is the single
//             exception where a reaction may carry as much volume as the campaign's last
//             bar, or more. Without both, line 250 says "we have a demand still we would
//             WAIT", and line 111 "we would not sell it".
//  I previously let ANY wicked reaction qualify whatever its volume, which admitted loud
//  reactions with nothing capping them - exactly the demand case the law refuses.
//  THE ENGULF ROUTE, off unless admitEngulf is on. Same capping requirement as the wick
//  route - line 125 never reads a loud reaction as aggressive selling without the campaign
//  having been capped first.
//  ✅ THE CAPPING REQUIREMENT MEASURED AT LAST, 2026-09-26, and it is one of the
//  strongest gates in this file. Zee, on the 25 Sep 21:05 M5 bar: "there's a strong
//  campaign leading to a reaction.. it says loud but no capping in the campaign bar..
//  i think we're missing on too many setups.. can u check what effect it has if we
//  remove the capping requirement".
//  AND IT RAISED A PRIOR QUESTION - our code may be STRICTER THAN HIS OWN LAW. Line 111
//  reads "IF no small candle big volume previously AND not wick on top of the red
//  reaction candle -> WE WOULD NOT SELL IT", which by De Morgan is capping OR wick. This
//  line demands BOTH. So all three readings were scored, 8,674 M5 bars, 51 sessions,
//  at 1 bar:
//      reading                              setups/day  STRONG  right   per label  total
//      AND   capping required (shipped)         6.2       140   65.7%   +1.228     +172.0
//      WICK  capping removed (his question)    11.2       297   56.6%   +0.371     +110.2
//      OR    capping or wick (line 111)        13.7       326   56.4%   +0.234      +76.1
//  REMOVING IT NEARLY DOUBLES THE SETUPS AND CUTS TOTAL MOVEMENT BY A THIRD. The 157
//  STRONG setups it adds are worth -0.393 EACH at 48.4% - the most destructive band ever
//  measured here. The sell side is what dies: +0.599 -> +0.038, and under the literal
//  OR reading it goes NEGATIVE (-0.023).
//
//  AND A BIG WICK DOES NOT RESCUE THEM, which was the obvious hypothesis - his bar
//  carried a 60% wick. Splitting every LOUD WICKED reaction by wick size:
//      wick band     CAPPED                    NOT CAPPED
//      15-30%        62   59.7%  +1.299        120  51.7%  -0.020
//      30-45%        46   56.5%  +0.647         57  57.9%  +0.027
//      45-60%        40   60.0%  +0.947         49  49.0%  -0.379
//      60%+          33   51.5%  +0.962         31  41.9%  -0.545
//  CAPPING DISCRIMINATES AT EVERY WICK SIZE AND THE WICK SIZE BARELY MATTERS. Capped
//  pays +0.65 to +1.30 in all four bands; uncapped pays nothing or loses in all four.
//  The 60%+ uncapped cell - exactly his bar's profile - is the WORST in the table.
//  Campaign loudness does not rescue them either: uncapped runs at 180%+ of average (his
//  bar was 182%) measure 54.5% right and -0.165 per label on 33 samples.
//
//  SO THE STRICTER READING IS THE RIGHT ONE, and this is a case where the document's
//  literal wording loses money and the code's tighter reading does not. Line 156's full
//  definition - "supply has hit + has a wick + closing strong bearish" - is the better
//  guide than line 111's negative.
//
//  ...AND THEN THE YARDSTICK ITSELF WAS CHALLENGED, 2026-09-27, with a photo of a big
//  green bar followed by a small green bar carrying the TALLEST volume in view: "hasn't
//  capping happened where the campaign's last green bar has high volume but the candle
//  is not going upwards similar to the large move given by the second last campaign bar."
//  That is the correction he has made stick TWICE before - compare it to the candle
//  beside it, not to a 20-bar average - and cappedRef ("the CAMPAIGN's own candles") was
//  built for exactly it on 2026-09-20 and never measured. So it was measured, along with
//  two forms he did not ask for. ALL FOUR ARE WORSE:
//
//      capping yardstick                     STRONG  right   per label   total
//      AVERAGE 0.70 (shipped)                  140   65.7%   +1.228      +172.0
//      CAMPAIGN bar <= 0.50 of run's widest    151   63.6%   +0.952      +143.8
//      CAMPAIGN bar <= 0.70                    185   59.5%   +0.586      +108.3
//      PREV     bar <= 0.35 the previous bar   118   66.1%   +1.204      +142.1
//      PREV     bar <= 0.80                    179   62.6%   +0.941      +168.4
//  CAMPAIGN mode degrades monotonically as it loosens and its SELL side goes NEGATIVE at
//  0.70 and above. PREV mode never beats the average on per-label at any threshold.
//  As a UNION rather than a replacement (AVERAGE or prev-bar) the best cell is 0.35:
//  +173.7 total against +172.0 - a tie - and the 7 STRONG setups it adds are worth
//  +0.249 each against a +1.228 average. Dilutive. Every looser union is negative.
//
//  THEN THE VOLUME HALF, which is what actually refused his bar - in the reconstruction
//  of that area the small green candle ran 34% of the average RANGE (passing easily) and
//  69% of the average VOLUME (failing). So the size test was never the problem:
//      capping VOLUME test                   STRONG  right   per label   total
//      >= 1.15x the 20-bar average (shipped)   140   65.7%   +1.228      +172.0
//      >= 1.00x the PREVIOUS campaign bar      152   57.2%   +0.604       +91.8
//      >= 1.15x the previous bar               108   56.5%   +0.651       +70.3
//      >= 1.00x this run's own average         158   58.2%   +0.589       +93.1
//      AVERAGE or PREV >= 1.00 (union)         189   61.4%   +0.897      +169.6
//  Every neighbour-relative volume rule roughly HALVES the per-label figure, and every
//  union adds setups that lose (-0.049, -0.159, -0.880 as it tightens).
//
//  ⭐ WHY THE TWO GATES WANT OPPOSITE YARDSTICKS - the principle this turned up, and it
//  reconciles five sessions of apparently contradictory corrections:
//      THE REACTION asks "did the other side WALK AWAY?" - a question about CHANGE, so
//        it needs a RELATIVE yardstick, and Zee was right to move it to the last
//        campaign bar (quietRef) and right again on the LOW BROKEN volume (brokeVolRef).
//      CAPPING asks "did someone LARGE arrive?" - a question about ABSOLUTE SIZE.
//        Institutional size does not scale to the last three candles. A bar merely
//        louder than its quiet neighbours is noise, not big money, and the 20-bar
//        average is the better proxy for "this is genuinely a lot of volume".
//  So "compare it to the candle beside it" is the right correction for some gates and
//  the wrong one for this gate, and the measurement is the only way to tell which.
//
//  PROVENANCE: a PYTHON REPLICATION over M1 bars aggregated to M5, so the absolute
//  figures are NOT quotable and this is NOT a backtest under CLAUDE.md. Every variant
//  ran on the identical bar series and differs in ONE term, so the COMPARISON is sound -
//  which is the only claim made here. ⚠️ The aggregation does NOT reproduce TradingView's
//  own M5 bars (it gave a 2-bar campaign and a 10% wick where Zee's chart showed 3 bars
//  and 60%), so NO claim is made about any individual bar he is looking at - only about
//  the population of 8,674.
engulfOK = admitEngulf and engulfAnyN >= engulfAdmitN and cappedRun
loudRoute = wicked or engulfOK
loudOK   = loudRoute and cappedRun
//  ...and the SOFT route: the same loud wicked reaction with NO capping behind it. It is
//  admitted so the turn is visible, and noCapSoft is carried into warnNow so it can only
//  ever print ORANGE. Note loudRoute collapses to `wicked` here, because engulfOK already
//  requires cappedRun - which is exactly the population the measurement scored.
noCapSoft = loudSoft and loudRoute and not cappedRun
absorbed = isCampaign and (quiet or loudOK or noCapSoft)

// ---- confirmation arrives on the NEXT bar ---------------------------------------------
contDir  = -runDir                                   // a selling campaign sets up a BUY
contUp    = contDir[1] > 0 ? close > open : close < open
confirmed = absorbed[1] and needCont and contUp and volume <= campAvg[1]
fired = needCont ? confirmed : absorbed

// ---- the run ending ON THIS BAR, so it can be marked live rather than in hindsight ----
runNow  = 0
peakNow = 0.0
volNow  = 0.0
if dir != 0
    for k = 0 to maxRun - 1
        d = close[k] > open[k] ? 1 : close[k] < open[k] ? -1 : 0
        if d != dir
            break
        runNow  += 1
        volNow  += volume[k]
        peakNow := math.max(peakNow, volume[k])
avgNow   = runNow > 0 ? volNow / runNow : 0.0
smaNow  = ta.sma(volume, lookN)
hiNow   = ta.highest(volume, lookN)
medNow  = ta.median(volume, lookN)
heavyNow = method == "AVERAGE of last N" ? avgNow >= smaNow[runNow] * avgX : method == "MEDIAN of last N (ignores one spike)" ? avgNow >= medNow[runNow] * medX : peakNow >= hiNow[runNow] * pkX
inRunNow = runNow >= minRun
campNow  = inRunNow and heavyNow

// ---- THE TREND BRUSH ---------------------------------------------------------------------
//  Ported from turtle.pine lines 394-466 and 2812-2818. See the note at the showTrend
//  input for how the ink-stroke look is produced and - more importantly - why this line is
//  not allowed to gate anything.
//  Every ta.* call here runs on EVERY bar, never inside an `if`, or its history would be
//  built only from the bars a branch happened to take.
trSwL = 20
trAL  = 14
trVL  = 20
trRF  = 0.45
trBPct = 0.001

//  LENGTHS IN MINUTES, CONVERTED TO BARS - so the M1 chart and the M5 chart describe the
//  same stretch of market rather than two different ones. timeframe.multiplier is the
//  chart's own minute count on an intraday chart; the max(2) guard stops a daily chart
//  from asking for a 1-bar EMA.
trTfMin = timeframe.isintraday ? math.max(timeframe.multiplier, 1) : 1440
trFast  = trAuto ? math.max(2, math.round(trFastMin / trTfMin)) : trFastIn
trSlow  = trAuto ? math.max(3, math.round(trSlowMin / trTfMin)) : trSlowIn

trEmaF = ta.ema(close, trFast)
trEmaS = ta.ema(close, trSlow)
trAtr  = ta.atr(trAL)
trAtrM = ta.sma(trAtr, trAL)
trVolM = ta.sma(volume, trVL)
trSlope = math.abs(trEmaF - trEmaF[1])

trEmaUp = trEmaF > trEmaS
trEmaDn = trEmaF < trEmaS
trSlUp  = trEmaF > trEmaF[1]
trSlDn  = trEmaF < trEmaF[1]
trFlat  = trSlope < trAtr * 0.1

trHi = ta.highest(high, trSwL)
trLo = ta.lowest(low,  trSwL)
trStUp = close > trLo
trStDn = close < trHi
trNoSt = not trStUp and not trStDn

//  the 4-hour agreement - the one repainting input, and it is worth 10 of 100
trHF = request.security(syminfo.tickerid, trHTF, ta.ema(close, trFast))
trHS = request.security(syminfo.tickerid, trHTF, ta.ema(close, trSlow))
trHUp = trHF > trHS
trHDn = trHF < trHS

trVolExp = volume > trVolM
trAtrExp = trAtr > trAtrM
trAtrCon = trAtr < trAtrM * trRF
trDead   = trAtrCon and trFlat and trNoSt

trBrkUp = close > trHi * (1 + trBPct)
trBrkDn = close < trLo * (1 - trBPct)

trRawUp = (trEmaUp and trSlUp and trStUp and not trDead) or trBrkUp
trRawDn = (trEmaDn and trSlDn and trStDn and not trDead) or trBrkDn
trRelax = trVolExp or trAtrExp
trConfUp = (trRawUp and trRelax) or trBrkUp
trConfDn = (trRawDn and trRelax) or trBrkDn
trBullish = trConfUp or trRawUp
trBearish = trConfDn or trRawDn

trScore = 0.0
trScore += trConfUp or trConfDn ? 30.0 : 0.0
trScore += trVolExp and trAtrExp ? 25.0 : 0.0
trScore += trHUp or trHDn ? 10.0 : 0.0
trScore += trSlope > trAtr * 0.2 ? 15.0 : 0.0
trScore += not trDead ? 10.0 : 0.0
trS = math.round(math.min(100.0, math.max(0.0, trScore)))

//  THE COLOUR RULE. STEADY is the ported turtle.pine logic; RESPONSIVE replaces the
//  cross with the fast EMA's own slope, which is 25 minutes earlier and far noisier - see
//  the table at the trColMode input.
trResp   = trColMode == "RESPONSIVE - EMA slope"
trUpBase = (trBullish and not trBearish) or (trBullish and trBearish and trS >= 50)
trDnBase = (trBearish and not trBullish) or (trBullish and trBearish and trS < 50)
trUp = trResp ? trSlUp : trUpBase
trDn = trResp ? trSlDn : trDnBase

//  transparency steps with the band, so the stroke darkens as conviction grows
trFade = trS > 75 ? 0 : trS > 50 ? 20 : trS > 25 ? 40 : trS > 10 ? 60 : 80
trCol  = trUp ? color.new(color.green, trFade) : trDn ? color.new(color.red, trFade) : color.new(color.gray, 80)

//  ONE band live at a time - that is what breaks the stroke into blobs
trW1 = trS <= 10 ? trEmaF : na
trW2 = trS > 10 and trS <= 25 ? trEmaF : na
trW3 = trS > 25 and trS <= 50 ? trEmaF : na
trW4 = trS > 50 and trS <= 75 ? trEmaF : na
trW5 = trS > 75 ? trEmaF : na

plot(showTrend ? trEmaF : na, "Trend (base)", color = color.new(color.gray, 85), linewidth = 1)
plot(showTrend ? trW1 : na, "Trend w1", color = trCol, linewidth = 6,  style = plot.style_linebr)
plot(showTrend ? trW2 : na, "Trend w2", color = trCol, linewidth = 8,  style = plot.style_linebr)
plot(showTrend ? trW3 : na, "Trend w3", color = trCol, linewidth = 10, style = plot.style_linebr)
plot(showTrend ? trW4 : na, "Trend w4", color = trCol, linewidth = 12, style = plot.style_linebr)
plot(showTrend ? trW5 : na, "Trend w5", color = trCol, linewidth = 14, style = plot.style_linebr)

// ---- THE CAMEL HUMPS, AND THE COMPASS THEY POINT --------------------------------------
//  Ported from BTCUSD/trend_eyes.py - find_swings() + auto_call(). See the note at the
//  showCamel input for the provenance of every constant here.
//  ta.pivothigh / ta.pivotlow ARE the fractal engine: "the highest high of K bars on each
//  side", confirmed K bars late. They must be called on every bar, never inside an `if`.
cmH = ta.pivothigh(high, camelK, camelK)
cmL = ta.pivotlow(low, camelK, camelK)

//  the last three confirmed PEAKS (price and the bar they sat on), newest last
var float cmH1 = na, var float cmH2 = na, var float cmH3 = na
var int   cmX1 = 0,  var int   cmX2 = 0,  var int   cmX3 = 0
//  ...and the last two TROUGHS, for the guard
var float cmL1 = na, var float cmL2 = na
//  the running hump polyline
var float cmPrevY = na
var int   cmPrevX = 0

if not na(cmH)
    cmH1 := cmH2
    cmX1 := cmX2
    cmH2 := cmH3
    cmX2 := cmX3
    cmH3 := cmH
    cmX3 := bar_index - camelK
if not na(cmL)
    cmL1 := cmL2
    cmL2 := cmL

//  THE HUMP POLYLINE - "one red line riding over the swings so the eye instantly sees
//  whether the humps walk up or down". Pine cannot spline, so this is the straight
//  polyline through every confirmed swing in time order, which is what trend_eyes.py drew
//  before the Catmull-Rom smoothing was added for the PNG.
if camelLine and showCamel and (not na(cmH) or not na(cmL))
    cmY = not na(cmH) ? cmH : cmL
    cmX = bar_index - camelK
    if not na(cmPrevY)
        line.new(cmPrevX, cmPrevY, cmX, cmY, color = color.new(color.orange, 35), width = 2)
    cmPrevY := cmY
    cmPrevX := cmX

//  LEAST SQUARES THROUGH THE LAST THREE PEAKS. With only two peaks known it degenerates
//  to the plain gradient between them, which is what the Python does too.
cmN = na(cmH3) ? 0 : na(cmH2) ? 1 : na(cmH1) ? 2 : 3
cmSlant = 0.0
if cmN >= 2
    cmSumX = cmN == 2 ? cmX2 + cmX3 : cmX1 + cmX2 + cmX3
    cmSumY = cmN == 2 ? cmH2 + cmH3 : cmH1 + cmH2 + cmH3
    //  float(), NOT int/int. cmSumX and cmN are both INT and Pine returns an INT from
    //  int/int - the same truncation that silently broke every "per day" figure in the
    //  funnel table on 2026-09-20. A truncated mean here would tilt the whole slant.
    cmXb = cmSumX / float(cmN)
    cmYb = cmSumY / float(cmN)
    cmNum = 0.0
    cmDen = 0.0
    if cmN == 3
        cmNum := (cmX1 - cmXb) * (cmH1 - cmYb) + (cmX2 - cmXb) * (cmH2 - cmYb) + (cmX3 - cmXb) * (cmH3 - cmYb)
        cmDen := math.pow(cmX1 - cmXb, 2) + math.pow(cmX2 - cmXb, 2) + math.pow(cmX3 - cmXb, 2)
    else
        cmNum := (cmX2 - cmXb) * (cmH2 - cmYb) + (cmX3 - cmXb) * (cmH3 - cmYb)
        cmDen := math.pow(cmX2 - cmXb, 2) + math.pow(cmX3 - cmXb, 2)
    cmSlant := cmDen == 0 ? 0.0 : cmNum / cmDen

//  THE GUARD. The deepest of the last two swing lows holds an uptrend up; the highest of
//  the last two swing highs caps a downtrend. Broken only by a CLOSE, never by a wick -
//  see the 18:50 spring note at the input.
cmGuardLo = na(cmL1) ? cmL2 : math.min(cmL1, cmL2)
cmGuardHi = na(cmH2) ? cmH3 : math.max(cmH2, cmH3)
cmLastClose = close[1]
cmGuardBroke = not na(cmGuardLo) and cmLastClose < cmGuardLo
cmGuardBrokeUp = not na(cmGuardHi) and cmLastClose > cmGuardHi

//  THE VERDICT. Slant first, then the guard can overrule it - a broken guard does not
//  mean no-man's-land, it means the trend has SHIFTED, and the confirmed-peak slant lags
//  a fresh V-turn by design because peaks need K closed bars.
cmTrend = cmN < 2 ? 0 : cmSlant > camelEps ? 1 : cmSlant < -camelEps ? -1 : 0
cmWhy = cmN < 2 ? "fewer than 2 confirmed peaks yet" : cmSlant > camelEps ? "the last " + str.tostring(cmN) + " peaks slant UP" : cmSlant < -camelEps ? "the last " + str.tostring(cmN) + " peaks slant DOWN" : "the peak line is flat - RANGE"
if cmTrend > 0 and cmGuardBroke
    cmTrend := -1
    cmWhy := "peaks slant up BUT the guard low at " + str.tostring(cmGuardLo, format.mintick) + " closed through - the trend has shifted from BUYS"
else if cmTrend < 0 and cmGuardBrokeUp
    cmTrend := 1
    cmWhy := "peaks slant down BUT the guard high at " + str.tostring(cmGuardHi, format.mintick) + " closed through - the trend has shifted from SELLS"

drawCamel() =>
    //  BOTTOM RIGHT, BIG. Zee: "a bit larger arrow.. so that i can see it and say ok ..
    //  we're in an uptrend". A table is the only thing in Pine that stays put while the
    //  chart is zoomed and scrolled - a label would sail off screen the moment he zooms
    //  into a candle, which is exactly the moment he says he loses track.
    cmTxt = cmTrend > 0 ? "▲" : cmTrend < 0 ? "▼" : "■"
    cmCol = cmTrend > 0 ? color.new(color.green, 0) : cmTrend < 0 ? color.new(color.red, 0) : color.new(color.gray, 20)
    cmWord = cmTrend > 0 ? "UPTREND" : cmTrend < 0 ? "DOWNTREND" : "RANGE"
    cmSz = camelSize == "huge" ? size.huge : camelSize == "large" ? size.large : size.normal
    var table cm = table.new(position.bottom_center, 1, 2, bgcolor = color.new(color.black, 20), frame_color = color.new(color.gray, 60), frame_width = 1)
    table.cell(cm, 0, 0, cmTxt, text_color = cmCol, bgcolor = color.new(color.black, 20), text_size = cmSz,
       tooltip = "CAMEL HUMPS - " + cmWord + "\n" + cmWhy + "\nslant " + str.tostring(cmSlant, "#.###") + " points per bar (RANGE is anything inside +/-" + str.tostring(camelEps) + ")" + "\nguard low " + (na(cmGuardLo) ? "-" : str.tostring(cmGuardLo, format.mintick)) + "   guard high " + (na(cmGuardHi) ? "-" : str.tostring(cmGuardHi, format.mintick)) + "\nThis is BTCUSD/trend_eyes.py auto_call() ported - the same engine as the cockpit compass and camel_humps.png." + "\nIT IS ORIENTATION, NOT A FILTER. Trend context has been measured on this chart four ways and predicted nothing each time, so a SELL SETUP inside a green uptrend is still a SELL SETUP.")
    table.cell(cm, 0, 1, cmWord, text_color = cmCol, bgcolor = color.new(color.black, 20), text_size = size.small)
    0

if showCamel and barstate.islast
    drawCamel()

// ---- drawing ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// NO barcolor() HERE. Zee: "can u remove all colors from the candle bodies.. to retain
// original candle colors". The candles keep TradingView's own colouring; the campaign is
// already carried by the background shade and by the volume pane, so repainting the bodies
// was a third copy of the same fact and it buried the price action underneath it.

plotshape(showRun and inRunNow and dir < 0, title = "in a selling run", style = shape.triangledown, location = location.abovebar, size = size.tiny, color = color.new(color.red, 20))
plotshape(showRun and inRunNow and dir > 0, title = "in a buying run", style = shape.triangleup, location = location.belowbar, size = size.tiny, color = color.new(color.teal, 20))

// NO bgcolor() EITHER. Zee: "can you remove the background vertical lines from the
// chart". With the candle bodies already left alone, the price chart now carries only
// WORDS - the campaign is read in the volume pane, where it is a fact about volume.

// WORDS, NOT SYMBOLS. Zee: "instead of diamonds can u write labels.. because its hard to
// track so many colors / diamonds etc". A shape has to be remembered; a word does not.
if showAll and absorbed and needCont
    rLab = quiet ? "reaction: QUIET" : "reaction: WICK"
    rCol = quiet ? color.new(color.yellow, 15) : color.new(color.orange, 15)
    rSty = runDir < 0 ? label.style_label_up : label.style_label_down
    label.new(bar_index, runDir < 0 ? low : high, rLab, style = rSty, color = rCol, textcolor = color.black, size = size.small)

// ---- SUPPORT / RESISTANCE, AND THE FAKE BREAK -------------------------------------------
//  The level is the last confirmed swing low (for a selling campaign) or swing high (for a
//  buying one) - "the horizontal line coming from the last low point", as Zee put it.
//  A FAKE BREAK is that level pierced by a WICK and taken back before the close: line 35's
//  "breaking down below the support line with a wick and CLOSING ABOVE the support line".
//  It is checked on the campaign's LAST candle, which is where he asked for it, and again
//  on the reaction itself - line 234 calls that combination the strongest case there is.
var float supLvl = na
var float resLvl = na
pl = ta.pivotlow(low, pivLeft, pivRight)
ph = ta.pivothigh(high, pivLeft, pivRight)
if not na(pl)
    supLvl := pl
if not na(ph)
    resLvl := ph

lvl = runDir < 0 ? supLvl : resLvl
//  pierced and recovered - on the campaign's last bar
fakeLast = not na(lvl) and (runDir < 0 ? (low[1] < lvl and close[1] > lvl) : (high[1] > lvl and close[1] < lvl))
//  ...and on the reaction bar itself
fakeReact = not na(lvl) and (runDir < 0 ? (low < lvl and close > lvl) : (high > lvl and close < lvl))
fakeBreak = fakeLast or fakeReact

//  ...AND THE BREAK THAT HELD. Zee, 2026-09-20: "if a campaign's candles breaks previous
//  resistance (a horizontal line from last highest point), then we can label that candle
//  as STAR resistance broken.. on the setup that appears next."
//  This is the OPPOSITE of the fake break above. There the level was pierced by a wick and
//  given back before the close; here a campaign candle CLOSED beyond it and kept it. The
//  campaign had enough behind it to take out the last swing point.
//  NOTE THE POLARITY, because it is not the obvious one: resistance sits above, so only a
//  BUYING campaign can break it - and the setup that follows a buying campaign is a SELL.
//  The note therefore lands on a sell label saying the rise before it was genuinely strong.
//  That reads two ways and the script does not choose between them: either the break was
//  real and selling into it is dangerous, or the break failed at once and this is the
//  classic upthrust, the strongest sell in the book. It states the fact and the level; the
//  trader decides. Mirrored for a selling campaign that closes below support.
//  The pivot that defines the level is confirmed with pivRight bars of lag, so it almost
//  always predates the campaign - which is what makes it a level the campaign BROKE rather
//  than one the campaign created.
campBroke = false
if runLen > 0 and not na(lvl)
    for k = 1 to runLen
        if runDir > 0 ? close[k] > lvl : close[k] < lvl
            campBroke := true

fSubF() =>
    runDir < 0 ? "wicked below " + str.tostring(lvl, format.mintick) + " and closed back above" : "wicked above " + str.tostring(lvl, format.mintick) + " and closed back below"
drawFakeBreak() =>
    fTxt = runDir < 0 ? "FAKE BREAK of support" : "FAKE BREAK of resistance"
    fSub = fSubF()
    fCol = color.new(color.purple, 15)
    fSty = runDir < 0 ? label.style_label_up : label.style_label_down
    //  LANE 1 on the PREVIOUS bar - the "lower wick suggests buying" label owns lane 0
    //  there, and both fire on the campaign's last candle.
    fY = runDir < 0 ? low[1] - avgRange * labelGap : high[1] + avgRange * labelGap
    label.new(bar_index - 1, fY, compact ? fTxt : wrapN(fTxt + "\n" + fSub), tooltip = wrapN(fTxt + "\n" + fSub), style = fSty, color = fCol, textcolor = color.white, size = size.small)
    0

if showSup and isCampaignDraw and fakeLast
    drawFakeBreak()

if showSup and isCampaignDraw and not na(lvl)
    line.new(bar_index - runLen - 2, lvl, bar_index + 2, lvl, color = color.new(color.purple, 40), style = line.style_dashed, width = 1)

// ---- THE CAMPAIGN'S LAST CANDLE ---------------------------------------------------------
//  Zee, 2026-09-19: "we can label the last candle of campaign if it has a large lower wick
//  on its closing, it should say: lower wick suggests buying".
//  That is line 279 - "if there was a wick below the red candle, then the bigger volume
//  would mean its DEMAND" - and it is the earliest tell in the whole sequence: the other
//  side showing up INSIDE the campaign, one bar before the reaction prints.
lastRng   = high[1] - low[1]
lastLow   = math.min(open[1], close[1]) - low[1]
lastHigh  = high[1] - math.max(open[1], close[1])
lastWickV = runDir < 0 ? lastLow : lastHigh
lastHas   = lastRng > 0 and (lastWickV / lastRng) >= lastWick

//  ...AND ACROSS THE RUN. Every one of the campaign's last campWickN candles has to carry
//  the wick, not just some of them - a single bought-off low happens all the time, and it
//  is the repetition that carries the meaning. If the campaign is shorter than that, there
//  is nothing to claim and the test stands down.
//
//  WHICH SIDE. The wick that matters is the one showing the side AGAINST the setup, so it
//  follows the setup's direction and not the campaign's. My first version keyed it off the
//  campaign and looked at the wrong half of the candle: Zee, 2026-09-20, "the 9:40 PM
//  candle doesnot have the caution still". That bar is a SELL after a GREEN campaign, and
//  its last three campaign candles carried LOWER wicks of 17%, 35% and 73% - every dip
//  bought back - while the upper wicks it was checking read 8%, 43% and 14% and failed.
//  A sell is endangered by BUYERS, who appear as wicks BELOW; a buy is endangered by
//  SELLERS, who appear as wicks ABOVE. Note this is the opposite polarity from lastHas
//  above, which marks the same wick as CONFIRMATION when it favours the setup.
campWickAll = runLen >= campWickN
campWickCnt = 0
//  ...and the SAME bars measured at the other end, for the strength star. One loop, two
//  counters, because they are the two ends of the same candles.
campStrongAll = runLen >= campStrongN
campStrongCnt = 0
//  One loop over however many bars the LONGER of the two tests needs; each bar is then
//  only counted towards a test that still reaches back that far.
if runLen > 0
    for k = 1 to math.min(runLen, math.max(campWickN, campStrongN))
        cRng = high[k] - low[k]
        cLo  = math.min(open[k], close[k]) - low[k]
        cHi  = high[k] - math.max(open[k], close[k])
        //  AGAINST the setup - a sell is endangered by buyers (wicks below), a buy by
        //  sellers (wicks above)
        cWk  = runDir > 0 ? cLo : cHi
        //  FAVOURING the setup - the other end of the same candle
        cWkS = runDir > 0 ? cHi : cLo
        if k <= campWickN
            if cRng > 0 and (cWk / cRng) >= campWickX
                campWickCnt += 1
            else
                campWickAll := false
        if k <= campStrongN
            if cRng > 0 and (cWkS / cRng) >= campWickX
                campStrongCnt += 1
            else
                campStrongAll := false
campWicked    = showCampWick and campWickAll and campWickCnt >= campWickN
campStrong    = showCampStrong and campStrongAll and campStrongCnt >= campStrongN

lSubF() =>
    runDir < 0 ? "pushed off its low on the last campaign bar - demand already there" : "pushed off its high on the last campaign bar - supply already there"
drawLastWick() =>
    lTxt = runDir < 0 ? "lower wick suggests buying" : "upper wick suggests selling"
    lSub = lSubF()
    lCol = runDir < 0 ? color.new(color.teal, 30) : color.new(color.red, 30)
    lSty = runDir < 0 ? label.style_label_up : label.style_label_down
    label.new(bar_index - 1, runDir < 0 ? low[1] : high[1], compact ? lTxt : wrapN(lTxt + "\n" + lSub), tooltip = wrapN(lTxt + "\n" + lSub), style = lSty, color = lCol, textcolor = color.white, size = size.small)
    0

if showLast and isCampaignDraw and lastHas
    drawLastWick()

// ---- NO SUPPLY / NO DEMAND --------------------------------------------------------------
//  Judged on the bar ITSELF, using the run that ends on this bar (runNow), so a campaign
//  candle is marked as it prints rather than in hindsight from the reaction.
//  "Lower than the previous two bars" is taken literally - strictly below BOTH, which is
//  what makes it a genuine withdrawal rather than one quiet bar in a noisy sequence.
ndNarrow = rng > 0 and rng <= avgRange * ndRangeX
ndQuiet  = volume < volume[1] and volume < volume[2]
ndUpWick = rng > 0 and (highWick / rng) >= ndWickX
//  the measured test: a narrow up-bar that cannot hold its own high
ndClosesLow = rng > 0 and ((close - low) / rng) <= ndCloseX
ndFail = ndUseClose ? ndClosesLow : ndUpWick
ndInRun  = showND and (ndHeavy ? campNow : inRunNow)

//  NO SUPPLY - a small narrow RED bar on falling volume, inside a selling run.
noSupply = ndInRun and dir < 0 and ndNarrow and ndQuiet
//  NO DEMAND - the mirror, and it carries one extra condition Zee specified: the upper
//  wick. Buyers reaching higher and being rejected is what "refuse to chase" looks like.
noDemand = ndInRun and dir > 0 and ndNarrow and ndQuiet and ndFail

nSubF() =>
    noSupply ? "small narrow red bar on falling volume - sellers have stopped pressing, the fall is running out of effort.\nNO DIRECTION CLAIMED: measured at 51.4% against a 50.1% base rate over 352 samples. This names a condition, it does not predict one." : "small narrow blue bar on falling volume that could NOT hold its own high - buyers will not chase, the rise is running out of effort.\nLEANS DOWN: 57.6% of these were followed by a lower close, against a 50.1% base rate (66 samples)."
drawNoSupply() =>
    //  ONLY ONE OF THESE EARNS AN ARROW. No demand measured 57.6% against a 50.1% base
    //  rate; no supply measured 51.4%, which is a coin. So no demand says which way and
    //  no supply says only what it is - a label must not claim more than it has.
    nTxt = noSupply ? "NO SUPPLY" : "NO DEMAND  ↓"
    //  ONE LINE, ESCAPED. Pine has no multi-line string literal: a real line break inside
    //  quotes is "mismatched character '\n' expecting '\"'". The break has to be the escape
    //  sequence inside the string, not an actual newline in the source.
    nSub = nSubF()
    nCol = noSupply ? color.new(color.teal, 20) : color.new(color.red, 20)
    //  LANE 2 - clear of the setup label (lane 0) and PRICE CAPPED / FAKE BREAK (lane 1),
    //  because a no-supply bar can be the campaign's last candle and collide with both.
    nY   = noSupply ? low - avgRange * labelGap * 2 : high + avgRange * labelGap * 2
    //  TINY. Zee, 2026-09-21: "can you make the text on labels no supply and no demand,
    //  smaller in size so they're not taking up too much space".
    //  These fire on RUNS of candles - a quiet stretch inside a campaign puts one on each
    //  bar - so they crowd faster than any other label here. They are also the most purely
    //  descriptive thing on the chart: they gate nothing, grade nothing, and have never
    //  been measured. A label that only says "notice this" should be the first to give up
    //  space to the ones that say "decide something".
    label.new(bar_index, nY, compact ? nTxt : wrapN(nTxt + "\n" + nSub), tooltip = wrapN(nTxt + "\n" + nSub), style = noSupply ? label.style_label_up : label.style_label_down, color = nCol, textcolor = color.white, size = size.tiny)
    0

if noSupply or noDemand
    drawNoSupply()

// ---- ANOMALY: THE VOLUME STOPPED MOVING IT ----------------------------------------------
//  The reference is the ratio of TOTALS over the window, not the average of the per-bar
//  ratios. Averaging ratios lets one tiny-volume bar with a normal range produce an
//  enormous efficiency reading and drag the baseline up; summing distance and summing
//  volume first gives the honest "points per unit of volume over the last N bars".
bodyAbs = math.abs(close - open)
bodySma = ta.sma(bodyAbs, lookN)
anomBody = anomUse == "BODY (open-close)"
anomMove = anomBody ? bodyAbs : rng
anomRef  = anomBody ? bodySma : avgRange

//  how far this bar travelled per unit of volume, against how far the window did
anomEffNow = volume > 0 ? anomMove / volume : 0.0
anomEffRef = volSma  > 0 ? anomRef  / volSma : 0.0
anomEffPct = anomEffRef > 0 ? anomEffNow / anomEffRef * 100 : 100
//  WHERE IN THE RANGE THIS BAR SITS - the thing that actually decides which way an anomaly
//  leans (see the label below). ta.highest / ta.lowest must be called on EVERY bar, not
//  inside the `if`, or Pine builds their history from the bars the branch happened to run
//  on and the reading silently drifts.
anomHi  = ta.highest(high, lookN)
anomLo  = ta.lowest(low, lookN)
anomPos = anomHi > anomLo ? (close - anomLo) / (anomHi - anomLo) : 0.5
anomaly = showAnom and volSma > 0 and anomEffRef > 0 and volume >= volSma * anomVolX and anomEffNow <= anomEffRef * anomEffX

// ---- THE HEAT STRIP: HOT = GRINDING, ICE = GLIDING ---------------------------------------
//  See the calibration and the direction test at the showHeat input.
//  heatEff is the SAME quantity the anomaly thresholds - distance per unit of volume
//  against the window's - so the purple label is simply this strip's extreme, named.
//  Computed on EVERY bar - bgcolor reads it as a series, not just on the last bar.
heatEff = anomEffRef > 0 ? anomEffNow / anomEffRef : 1.0

//  AN EMOJI PER CANDLE, NOT A SHADE. Zee, 2026-09-25: "print an ice or fire emoji on top
//  of the candle .. which is there as the candle is forming.. (all labels do this already)".
//  FOURTH MECHANISM, and the first three are worth keeping written down:
//  1. BOXES with left == right - zero width, so only the border drew. "i dont see shades
//     but borders/outline on candles".
//  2. plotcandle - repaints the candle, which is literally what was asked, but TradingView
//     stacks the MAIN SERIES above indicator plots unless the user sets Visual order ->
//     Bring to front. Correct in principle, invisible in practice.
//  3. bgcolor - rendered, but a full-height column is the shading Zee had removed on
//     2026-09-19, and it competes with the price rather than annotating it.
//  4. plotchar - what every other mark in this file already uses, appears live on the
//     forming bar, and costs nothing from the 500-label budget.
//
//  ON EVERY BAR, not just the last one. With heatHot at 0.60 and heatIce at 1.60 these sit
//  on the 10th and 90th percentiles of the efficiency distribution, so roughly one bar in
//  five carries an emoji - frequent, but that is what a heat map is. Tighten to 0.45 / 2.00
//  for about one in thirty if it crowds.
//
//  ⚠️ IT WILL FLICKER ON THE FORMING BAR, and the reason is arithmetic rather than a bug.
//  Efficiency is range / volume. Early in a candle both are small, and the ratio is
//  unstable - a few ticks of range against almost no volume reads as ICE. It settles as
//  the bar fills. The emoji on a CLOSED candle is the real reading; the one on the live
//  candle is a running estimate and should be treated that way.
heatHotNow = showHeat and heatEff <= heatHot
heatIceNow = showHeat and heatEff >= heatIce
plotchar(heatHotNow, title = "HOT - grinding", char = "🔥", location = location.abovebar, color = color.new(color.rgb(255, 61, 0), 0), size = size.tiny)
plotchar(heatIceNow, title = "ICE - gliding", char = "❄", location = location.abovebar, color = color.new(color.rgb(0, 200, 255), 0), size = size.tiny)
//  the middle band - neither hot nor cold. Same layer, same placement, deliberately dim.
heatMidNow = showHeat and heatAlways and heatEff > heatHot and heatEff < heatIce
plotchar(heatMidNow, title = "neither - middling effort", char = "·", location = location.abovebar, color = color.new(color.gray, 45), size = size.tiny)
//  THE SQUAT - the strong core of the anomaly, not a separate label.
//  Zee, 2026-09-24: "so have you implemented the SQUAT (narrow + heavy volume) label?"
//  I had claimed it was "our anomaly by another name". That was imprecise, and checking
//  it properly changed the answer. The two tests are NOT the same shape:
//      ANOMALY  is a RATIO test - range per unit of volume against the window's. A very
//               loud bar is allowed to be WIDE and still qualify.
//      SQUAT    is ABSOLUTE - narrow AND loud, full stop.
//  Measured over 4,923 bars against a 50.1% base rate:
//      both (anomaly that is also narrow)   28   60.7% fell   +10.7
//      anomaly only, not narrow             48   54.2% fell    +4.1
//      squat only, not an anomaly            1   -
//  So the squat is a near-perfect SUBSET - 28 of 29 - and it is the half that carries the
//  signal. The other 48 anomalies are less than half as strong, and the label was treating
//  the two identically.
//  Hence a second arrow rather than a second label: same condition, graded.
//  SMALL SAMPLE. 28 bars. The direction is consistent with every other reading of this
//  condition, but the +10.7 itself should not be leaned on hard yet.
anomSquat = anomaly and avgRange > 0 and rng <= avgRange * anomSquatX

aSubF(aDir, aSquat, anomWhere) =>
    "volume " + str.tostring(volume / volSma * 100, "#") + "% of average but it moved only " + str.tostring(anomEffPct, "#") + "% as far per unit of volume as the last " + str.tostring(lookN) + " bars did" + "\nsomeone large is trading and price is not going anywhere - the other side is absorbing all of it" + "\n" + anomWhere + " (" + str.tostring(anomPos * 100, "#") + "% up the range)" + aDir + aSquat
aDirF() =>
    anomPos >= 0.75 ? "\nAT A HIGH - the buying is being absorbed. Measured: 63% of these fell on the next bar (19 samples). This is distribution, and it leans DOWN." : anomPos <= 0.25 ? "\nAT A LOW - and this is NOT the accumulation the textbook promises. Measured: 63% of these ALSO fell on the next bar (32 samples). Treat it as the fall not being finished, not as a bottom." : "\nMID RANGE - no reliable direction measured here (27 samples, 44% fell). It marks absorption only."
aSquatF() =>
    anomSquat ? "\nSQUAT - the range is also absolutely narrow (" + str.tostring(rng / avgRange * 100, "#") + "% of average), not merely narrow for the volume. This is the STRONG half: 60.7% of these were followed by a lower close against a 50.1% base rate (28 samples), where anomalies that are not narrow manage only 54.2%." : "\nnot a squat - the range is " + str.tostring(rng / avgRange * 100, "#") + "% of average. Anomalies this wide measured 54.2% against a 50.1% base rate (48 samples) - present, but half the signal of a squat."
drawAnomaly() =>
    //  WHICH WAY DOES IT POINT? Zee, 2026-09-23: "the text on anomally doesnot say whether
    //  this anomally means we're expecting a selling now or buying.. can it more clearly
    //  say whether we expect market to go down or up".
    //  Measured on 78 anomalies over 19 sessions, and it overturned the textbook twice.
    //  1. THE BAR'S OWN COLOUR IS USELESS. Classic VSA says a big UP bar going nowhere is
    //     buying absorbed (bearish) and a big DOWN bar is selling absorbed (bullish).
    //     Here: green anomalies fell 56.7% next bar, red ones 56.2%. Indistinguishable.
    //  2. WHERE IT SITS IN THE RANGE IS WHAT MATTERS:
    //         near the 20-bar HIGH   19   fell 63.2%   -0.78
    //         mid range             27   fell 44.4%   +0.31
    //         near the 20-bar LOW    32   fell 62.5%   -0.99
    //     Near the high it falls - distribution, exactly as the book says. But near the
    //     LOW it ALSO falls, which contradicts accumulation outright. An anomaly at a low
    //     is not a buy signal on M5 gold; it is a warning that the fall is not finished.
    //  So the arrow comes from the measurement, not the theory, and it carries its own
    //  sample size so the reader can weigh it. Mid-range says nothing and claims nothing.
    anomLean = anomPos >= 0.75 ? -1 : anomPos <= 0.25 ? -1 : 0
    anomWhere = anomPos >= 0.75 ? "near the 20-bar HIGH" : anomPos <= 0.25 ? "near the 20-bar LOW" : "mid range"
    aTxt = "🔍 ANOMALY" + (anomSquat ? " SQUAT" : "") + (anomLean < 0 ? (anomSquat ? "  ↓↓ expect DOWN" : "  ↓ expect DOWN") : (anomSquat ? "  ↓ leans DOWN" : "  - no clear direction"))
    aDir = aDirF()
    aSquat = aSquatF()
    aSub = aSubF(aDir, aSquat, anomWhere)
    //  LANE 3, above - clear of PRICE CAPPED (lane 1) and no supply / no demand (lane 2),
    //  both of which can land on this same bar.
    //  ON THE CANDLE, NOT FLOATING ABOVE IT. Zee, 2026-09-25: "the anomally squat label is
    //  high up in the air.. can u make it on top of candle so i dont miss it".
    //  It had lane 3 - three gaps above the high - purely so it could never collide with
    //  PRICE CAPPED (lane 1) or no supply / no demand (lane 2). That solved the collision
    //  and created a worse problem: a label far enough from its candle stops reading as
    //  being ABOUT that candle, and on a busy chart the eye does not connect the two.
    //  LANE PRIORITY IS NOT LANE NUMBER. The anomaly is the strongest directional call
    //  this script makes outside a setup (60.7% on a squat), so it gets the candle's edge
    //  and the weaker labels keep their outer lanes - they sit ABOVE it, so nothing
    //  overlaps and the order on screen now matches the order of importance.
    //  The ONE thing that outranks it is a setup label, which owns `high` exactly. When a
    //  setup fires on this same bar the anomaly steps out to lane 2 instead. `fired` is
    //  computed further up, so it is readable here; rawBuy / rawSell are not.
    aY = fired ? high + avgRange * labelGap * 2 : high + avgRange * labelGap * 0.15
    label.new(bar_index, aY, compact ? aTxt : wrapN(aTxt + "\n" + aSub), tooltip = wrapN(aTxt + "\n" + aSub), style = label.style_label_down, color = color.new(color.purple, 0), textcolor = color.white, size = size.small)
    0

if anomaly
    drawAnomaly()

// ---- THE DIRECTION HINT -----------------------------------------------------------------
//  Stands down on an anomaly bar: at a low the anomaly says DOWN (62.5% fell) and this
//  says UP (42.7% fell), and a chart must not put two opposite arrows on one candle.
//  anomPos and anomaly are computed just above.
//  ONCE PER VISIT, NOT ONCE PER BAR. Zee, 2026-09-24, with a screenshot of a staircase of
//  arrows down the side of a falling market: "every down candle has a green ▲ but it keeps
//  going down hehehe".
//  MEASURED FIRST, because the obvious explanation - that the repeats are the bad ones -
//  turned out to be false:
//      first bar of a streak   313   fell 42.2%  (-7.9)
//      2nd or later            263   fell 44.1%  (-5.9)   and -10.1 at three bars
//  The eighth arrow in a row is about as right as the first, so there is no accuracy case
//  for dropping them.
//  THE PROBLEM IS WHAT THE STAIRCASE SAYS, not whether it is right. This arrow is a ~57%
//  lean on ONE candle. In a ten-bar fall, four of those arrows are followed by a lower
//  close - that is what 57% means - but ten copies of one weak claim read like ten pieces
//  of evidence, and the chart appears to be insisting on a turn while price keeps dropping.
//  So it fires on the bar that ARRIVES at the low and stays quiet until price leaves and
//  comes back. Same information, one mark. This is a LEGIBILITY change and it is not
//  claimed to improve the hit rate: the bars it now hides measured -5.9.
atLow  = anomPos <= hintPosX
hintUp = showHints and atLow and not atLow[1] and not anomaly
plotchar(hintUp, title = "hint: leans UP", char = "▲", location = location.belowbar,
         color = color.new(color.teal, hintFade), size = size.tiny)

// ---- THE COILED BREAK --------------------------------------------------------------------
//  Inside bar on the PREVIOUS candle, this one closing outside the MOTHER bar - see the
//  evidence table at the showCoil input. A hint, not a setup: it gates nothing and it is
//  not part of the campaign/reaction sequence at all.
coilPrev = high[1] <= high[2] and low[1] >= low[2]
coilLoud = volSma > 0 and volume >= volSma * coilVolX
coilUp   = showCoil and coilPrev and coilLoud and close > high[2]
coilDn   = showCoil and coilPrev and coilLoud and close < low[2]

kSubF() =>
    "the bar before this one sat entirely inside the one before IT - volatility contracting - and this bar closed " + (coilUp ? "ABOVE" : "BELOW") + " that mother bar on " + str.tostring(volume / volSma * 100, "#") + "% of average volume." + "\nMEASURED: " + (coilUp ? "63.8% of these were followed by a higher close (47 samples)" : "63.3% of these were followed by a lower close (49 samples)") + ", against a 50.1% base rate." + "\nTHE CONTROL MATTERS MORE THAN THE NUMBER: the same breakout WITHOUT the coil scores " + (coilUp ? "50.1% on 631 samples" : "48.2% on 629 samples") + " - nothing. The contraction is what carries it, not the breakout." + "\nNot a VSISA setup - no campaign, no reaction, no absorption. A direction hint only, on about 50 samples a side."
drawGearing() =>
    //  PLAIN WORDS. Zee, 2026-09-24: "the coiled break label.. these words.. coiled-break..
    //  are hard to understand.. maybe we can call the label something like Gearing upwards".
    //  His name is better and for the reason that governs every label here: the headline is
    //  read at a glance and has to say what to EXPECT, not name the pattern that produced
    //  it. "Coiled break" describes the candles; "GEARING UP" describes the market. The
    //  mechanism is still in the tooltip for anyone who wants to check it.
    kTxt = coilUp ? "GEARING UP ↑" : "GEARING DOWN ↓"
    kSub = kSubF()
    //  LANE 2 on the opposite side from the break, so it never lands on the setup label's
    //  lane and never covers the candle it is describing.
    kY = coilUp ? low - avgRange * labelGap * 2 : high + avgRange * labelGap * 2
    label.new(bar_index, kY, compact ? kTxt : wrapN(kTxt + "\n" + kSub), tooltip = wrapN(kTxt + "\n" + kSub), style = coilUp ? label.style_label_up : label.style_label_down, color = coilUp ? color.new(color.teal, 35) : color.new(color.red, 35), textcolor = color.white, size = size.tiny)
    0

if coilUp or coilDn
    drawGearing()

// ---- EXTENDED RUN: DON'T CHASE -----------------------------------------------------------
//  See the evidence table at the showExt input. runNow is the run ENDING on this bar, so
//  testing it for equality with extN fires exactly once per run - on the bar that reaches
//  the threshold - rather than on every bar beyond it.
extNow = showExt and runNow == extN and dir != 0
xSubF(xUp) =>
    (xUp ? "five or more green candles without a pause. Buying HERE is buying the top of a leg." : "five or more red candles without a pause. Selling HERE is selling the bottom of a leg.") + "\nMEASURED over 19 sessions, entering with the run at this bar's close: runs of 5+ paid -0.89 by the sixth candle (299 samples) where runs of 1-4 paid +0.10 (4,613). The " + (xUp ? "green" : "red") + " side specifically: " + (xUp ? "42.7% right at six bars, -1.00" : "43.6% right at three bars, -0.84") + "." + "\nAND THE RETRACEMENT ALWAYS CAME: a bar closing against the run arrived within 10 candles in 299 of 299 cases, and entering at it beat chasing 61% of the time (183 against 116)." + "\nWHAT TO WAIT FOR: the pullback to end, which on this chart is worth taking only when it carries a " + (xUp ? "SELL" : "BUY") + " SETUP label - a plain resumption candle wins 45.7%, one with a setup on it wins 64.6%." + "\nNo direction is claimed here. At one bar this is a coin both ways; it is a reminder about WHERE to enter, not about which way price goes."
drawExtended() =>
    xUp = dir > 0
    xTxt = "⏳ " + str.tostring(extN) + " IN A ROW - wait for a retracement"
    xSub = xSubF(xUp)
    //  LANE 1 on the far side of the candle from where a setup would sit, and tiny - it is
    //  a nudge, not news. The setup labels must still out-shout it.
    xY = xUp ? high + avgRange * labelGap : low - avgRange * labelGap
    label.new(bar_index, xY, compact ? xTxt : wrapN(xTxt + "\n" + xSub), tooltip = wrapN(xTxt + "\n" + xSub), style = xUp ? label.style_label_down : label.style_label_up, color = color.new(color.gray, 30), textcolor = color.white, size = size.tiny)
    0

if extNow
    drawExtended()

// ---- THE STAIRCASE: SELLERS PAYING MORE FOR LESS, THEN A CHEAP GREEN ----------------------
//  See the two tables at the showStair input - in particular the note that his SECOND test
//  does not survive on its own and the combination is what lifts.
//  stEff is HIS unit: body height bought per unit of volume.
stEffNow = volume > 0 ? math.abs(close - open) / volume : 0.0

//  each candle against the one before it, same colour only - the step of the staircase
stSameRed = close < open and close[1] < open[1]
stSameGrn = close > open and close[1] > open[1]
stEffPrev = volume[1] > 0 ? math.abs(close[1] - open[1]) / volume[1] : 0.0
//  THE MOOD OF THE SIDE THAT MADE THIS CANDLE. See the bands and the frequency warning at
//  the stairSteps input.
//  WHICH SIDE IT SPEAKS FOR: the candle's own. A red candle is the SELLERS' work, so the
//  face sits BELOW it and reports how they did; a green candle is the buyers', and the
//  face sits above. That rule did not change when the meaning flipped from "weakening" to
//  "mood" - only the glyph did.
//  stRatioStep > 1 means this candle bought MORE body per unit of volume than the last one
//  of its colour: that side is getting more for less, and is happy. Below 1 it is paying
//  more for less, and is not.
stRatioStep = stEffPrev > 0 ? stEffNow / stEffPrev : na
stStep = (stSameRed or stSameGrn) and stEffPrev > 0 and stEffNow > 0 and not na(stRatioStep)
stOn   = stairSteps and stStep

//  the five shipped bands plus the two that are off unless the knobs are widened
stM4 = stOn and stRatioStep < 0.25                                   //  heartbroken
stM3 = stOn and stRatioStep >= 0.25 and stRatioStep < 0.50           //  loudly crying
stM2 = stOn and stRatioStep >= 0.50 and stRatioStep < 0.75           //  crying
stM1 = stOn and stRatioStep >= 0.75 and stRatioStep < 1.00 and stairSadX >= 1.00
stP1 = stOn and stRatioStep >= 1.33 and stRatioStep < 2.00 and stairHapX <= 1.33
stP2 = stOn and stRatioStep >= 2.00 and stRatioStep < 4.00
stP3 = stOn and stRatioStep >= 4.00
//  the two mildest SAD bands also respect their own knob, so tightening it hides them
stM2v = stM2 and stairSadX >= 0.75
stM3v = stM3 and stairSadX >= 0.50
stP2v = stP2 and stairHapX <= 2.00
stP3v = stP3 and stairHapX <= 4.00

//  RED CANDLES - the sellers' mood, drawn below the bar
plotchar(stSameRed and stM1,  title = "sellers: sad", char = "😞", location = location.belowbar, color = color.new(color.teal, 0), size = size.tiny)
plotchar(stSameRed and stM2v, title = "sellers: crying", char = "😢", location = location.belowbar, color = color.new(color.teal, 0), size = size.tiny)
plotchar(stSameRed and stM3v, title = "sellers: loudly crying", char = "😭", location = location.belowbar, color = color.new(color.teal, 0), size = size.tiny)
plotchar(stSameRed and stM4,  title = "sellers: heartbroken", char = "💔", location = location.belowbar, color = color.new(color.teal, 0), size = size.tiny)
plotchar(stSameRed and stP1,  title = "sellers: happy", char = "🙂", location = location.belowbar, color = color.new(color.red, 0), size = size.tiny)
plotchar(stSameRed and stP2v, title = "sellers: happier", char = "😃", location = location.belowbar, color = color.new(color.red, 0), size = size.tiny)
plotchar(stSameRed and stP3v, title = "sellers: happiest", char = "🤩", location = location.belowbar, color = color.new(color.red, 0), size = size.tiny)

//  GREEN CANDLES - the buyers' mood, drawn above the bar
plotchar(stSameGrn and stM1,  title = "buyers: sad", char = "😞", location = location.abovebar, color = color.new(color.red, 0), size = size.tiny)
plotchar(stSameGrn and stM2v, title = "buyers: crying", char = "😢", location = location.abovebar, color = color.new(color.red, 0), size = size.tiny)
plotchar(stSameGrn and stM3v, title = "buyers: loudly crying", char = "😭", location = location.abovebar, color = color.new(color.red, 0), size = size.tiny)
plotchar(stSameGrn and stM4,  title = "buyers: heartbroken", char = "💔", location = location.abovebar, color = color.new(color.red, 0), size = size.tiny)
plotchar(stSameGrn and stP1,  title = "buyers: happy", char = "🙂", location = location.abovebar, color = color.new(color.teal, 0), size = size.tiny)
plotchar(stSameGrn and stP2v, title = "buyers: happier", char = "😃", location = location.abovebar, color = color.new(color.teal, 0), size = size.tiny)
plotchar(stSameGrn and stP3v, title = "buyers: happiest", char = "🤩", location = location.abovebar, color = color.new(color.teal, 0), size = size.tiny)

//  ...and the completed pattern: a decaying red run, then the first green candle, cheap.
stRunLen = 0
stRedSum = 0.0
stDecay = true
if close > open
    for k = 1 to 10
        if close[k] >= open[k]
            break
        ek = volume[k] > 0 ? math.abs(close[k] - open[k]) / volume[k] : 0.0
        if ek <= 0
            stDecay := false
            break
        stRunLen += 1
        stRedSum += ek
        //  compare this red candle with the one BEFORE it in time. k counts backwards, so
        //  k + 1 is the older bar - and "decaying towards now" means the older candle
        //  bought MORE body per unit of volume than this one did.
        if close[k + 1] < open[k + 1]
            ek1 = volume[k + 1] > 0 ? math.abs(close[k + 1] - open[k + 1]) / volume[k + 1] : 0.0
            if ek1 > 0 and ek >= ek1
                stDecay := false
stRedEff = stRunLen > 0 ? stRedSum / stRunLen : 0.0
stRatio = stRedEff > 0 ? stEffNow / stRedEff : 0.0
stairNow = showStair and close > open and stRunLen >= stairRedN and stDecay and stRedEff > 0 and stRatio >= stairGrnX
stSubF() =>
    "🪜 THE STAIRCASE - " + str.tostring(stRunLen) + " red candles each buying LESS body than the one before it, then a green candle that bought " + str.tostring(stRatio, "#.#") + "x more body per unit of volume than those reds averaged." + "\nthe reds paid " + str.tostring(stRedEff > 0 ? 1.0 / stRedEff : 0, "#") + " volume per point; this green paid " + str.tostring(stEffNow > 0 ? 1.0 / stEffNow : 0, "#") + "\nMEASURED over 51 sessions: 52.2% higher on the next close, paying +0.21 points, against a 49.6% base rate and a control of +0.05 (362 samples at this threshold, 7 a day)." + "\n⚠️ THAT IS INSIDE THE NOISE - 2.6 points on 362 samples is about one standard error, and the next threshold up breaks the pattern at three bars. Treat it as your own reading made visible, not as a proved signal." + "\nOF YOUR TWO TESTS, ONLY THE FIRST ONE CARRIES. Decaying sellers beat strengthening ones by 1.8 / 1.7 / 2.8 points at 1 / 3 / 5 bars (1,660 against 1,596 samples). The green-candle cost on its OWN measured backwards - the cells where the green cost MORE than the reds did best. It is the combination that lifts, not either half." + "\nIt gates nothing and claims no direction beyond the number above."
drawStaircase() =>
    stTxt = "🪜 STAIRCASE  ↑ sellers spent out"
    stSub = stSubF()
    //  LANE 2 BELOW - clear of a setup label at the low, LOW BROKEN at lane 1 and the
    //  imbalance readout at 0.6, all of which can land on this same turn.
    label.new(bar_index, low - avgRange * labelGap * 2, compact ? stTxt : wrapN(stTxt + "\n" + stSub), tooltip = wrapN(stTxt + "\n" + stSub), style = label.style_label_up, color = color.new(color.rgb(40, 130, 180), 10), textcolor = color.white, size = size.tiny)
    0

if stairNow
    drawStaircase()

// ---- THE VOLUME / SPREAD IMBALANCE METER ---------------------------------------------------
//  See the verification and the refutation at the showImb input. Zee's own pre-trade
//  reading, computed instead of eyeballed - and explicitly not a direction call.
ibUpM = 0.0
ibUpV = 0.0
ibDnM = 0.0
ibDnV = 0.0
ibNg = 0
ibNr = 0
for k = 0 to imbK - 1
    if close[k] > open[k]
        ibUpM += close[k] - open[k]
        ibUpV += volume[k]
        ibNg += 1
    else if close[k] < open[k]
        ibDnM += open[k] - close[k]
        ibDnV += volume[k]
        ibNr += 1
//  BOTH COLOURS MUST BE PRESENT or there is nothing to compare - a window of three green
//  candles has no selling to measure, and dividing by it would invent a number.
ibOK  = ibNg > 0 and ibNr > 0 and ibUpV > 0 and ibDnV > 0 and ibUpM > 0 and ibDnM > 0
ibUpE = ibOK ? ibUpM / ibUpV : 0.0
ibDnE = ibOK ? ibDnM / ibDnV : 0.0
ibRatio = ibOK and ibDnE > 0 ? ibUpE / ibDnE : 1.0
ibBuyers  = ibOK and ibRatio >= imbLoud
ibSellers = ibOK and ibRatio <= 1.0 / imbLoud
//  AHEAD IS A LOWER BAR THAN DOMINANT, and it had to be. Zee, 2026-09-28, with a
//  screenshot of the meter reading "LEVEL 1.65x" while its own tooltip said buyers were
//  getting 1.65x more ground per unit of volume: "instead of a long note just say:
//  buyers ahead. that's it."
//  HE IS RIGHT THAT THE LABEL WAS ARGUING WITH ITSELF. imbLoud (2.0x) is the DOMINANCE
//  threshold - the point past which one side is running away with it - and the label was
//  using it to decide whether to name a side at all. So anything between 1.02x and 2.0x
//  printed LEVEL, which is false: at 1.65x the buyers are plainly ahead, just not
//  dominant.
//  THE PIVOT IS THE MEASURED MEDIAN, 1.02 over 43,238 M1 bars, NOT 1.00. A dead-even
//  market reads a shade above parity on this instrument, so 1.00 would call the buyers
//  ahead on the typical candle and the word would mean nothing.
//  DOMINANCE IS NOT LOST - it moves from the WORD to the COLOUR: solid teal/red past
//  imbLoud, faded when a side is merely ahead. Same two facts, one of them now silent.
ibAheadB = ibOK and ibRatio > 1.02
ibAheadS = ibOK and ibRatio < 1.0 / 1.02

//  THE POWER-PUNCH LADDER, IN HIS OWN CAST. Zee, 2026-09-26: "can we have these emojis
//  represent how good the punch is .. instead of digits 👷 we're building something /
//  🏋 we're lifting heavy weight / 🧚‍♀️ fairy has arrived. something magical is
//  happening. / 🧙‍♂️ Gandalf has arrived! Wohoo. top 5%".
//  WHY THIS IS BETTER THAN THE DIGIT IT REPLACES, and it is the same reason the mood faces
//  beat the three 🛜 sizes: a digit has to be READ and then RANKED against the other
//  digits you remember seeing. A builder, a lifter, a fairy and a wizard are four different
//  pictures with their own escalation built in - nobody has to work out that a wizard
//  outranks a builder. The tier is recognised, not decoded.
//  ⚠️ THE SIDE IS NOW CARRIED BY POSITION ALONE. TradingView renders colour emoji in its
//  own palette, so color.new() can still fade a glyph but CANNOT tint it - the teal/red
//  half of the old grammar is gone, exactly as it went when 🛜 replaced the block bars.
//  BELOW the candle = buyers are the efficient side, ABOVE = sellers. That is the whole
//  code now, so it has to be remembered rather than seen.
//  AND THE FADE COMES OFF WITH IT. The digits stepped 55/40/20/0 transparency because a
//  "2" and a "9" are the same size and shape; these four are not, so fading the lower
//  tiers would only make the commonest marks hard to see while adding nothing. All solid.
//  ⚠️ ONE CODE POINT EACH, AND THAT WAS LEARNED THE HARD WAY. Shipped first as the
//  sequences Zee typed - 👷 + ZWJ + ♂ and 🏋 + a skin-tone modifier - on my reasoning
//  that plotchar would truncate them to their base emoji. IT DREW NOTHING AT ALL. Zee,
//  2026-09-26: "only gandalf and fairy are visible. the other two aren't. we can easily
//  pick the single char 👷 and 🏋 (no joiners)".
//  AND THE FAILURE IS NOT ABOUT LENGTH, which is why my guess was wrong: 🧚‍♀️ and 🧙‍♂️
//  are ZWJ sequences of the SAME shape and both render fine. TradingView's emoji font
//  simply has composed glyphs for those two and not for the gendered builder or the
//  skin-toned lifter - and when a sequence has no glyph, plotchar draws a blank rather
//  than falling back to the base character.
//  THE RULE FOR THIS FILE: give plotchar ONE CODE POINT. A missing glyph is silent here -
//  no error, no box, just an empty candle - so a sequence that looks right in the settings
//  dialog can still be invisible on the chart.
ibSellRatio = ibOK and ibRatio > 0 ? 1.0 / ibRatio : 0.0
ibTierB = not ibOK ? 0 : ibRatio  >= 9 ? 9 : ibRatio  >= 5 ? 5 : ibRatio  >= 3 ? 3 : ibRatio  >= 2 ? 2 : 0
ibTierS = not ibOK ? 0 : ibSellRatio >= 9 ? 9 : ibSellRatio >= 5 ? 5 : ibSellRatio >= 3 ? 3 : ibSellRatio >= 2 ? 2 : 0
ibShowB = imbMark and ibTierB >= imbTierMin
ibShowS = imbMark and ibTierS >= imbTierMin
//  transparency steps with the tier, so a 9x reads louder than a 2x without changing size
plotchar(ibShowB and ibTierB == 2, title = "buyers 2x - building something", char = "👷", location = location.belowbar, color = color.new(color.teal, 0), size = size.tiny)
plotchar(ibShowB and ibTierB == 3, title = "buyers 3x - lifting heavy", char = "🏋", location = location.belowbar, color = color.new(color.teal, 0), size = size.tiny)
plotchar(ibShowB and ibTierB == 5, title = "buyers 5x - the fairy has arrived", char = "🧚‍♀️", location = location.belowbar, color = color.new(color.teal, 0), size = size.tiny)
plotchar(ibShowB and ibTierB == 9, title = "buyers 9x+ - GANDALF", char = "🧙‍♂️", location = location.belowbar, color = color.new(color.teal, 0), size = size.small)
plotchar(ibShowS and ibTierS == 2, title = "sellers 2x - building something", char = "👷", location = location.abovebar, color = color.new(color.red, 0), size = size.tiny)
plotchar(ibShowS and ibTierS == 3, title = "sellers 3x - lifting heavy", char = "🏋", location = location.abovebar, color = color.new(color.red, 0), size = size.tiny)
plotchar(ibShowS and ibTierS == 5, title = "sellers 5x - the fairy has arrived", char = "🧚‍♀️", location = location.abovebar, color = color.new(color.red, 0), size = size.tiny)
plotchar(ibShowS and ibTierS == 9, title = "sellers 9x+ - GANDALF", char = "🧙‍♂️", location = location.abovebar, color = color.new(color.red, 0), size = size.small)

//  THE NUMBER ON THE LIVE CANDLE. One label, deleted and redrawn each tick, so it rides
//  the forming bar instead of accumulating - the standard Pine pattern for a last-bar
//  readout. It sits on the side the advantage favours, which puts it out of the way of
//  the setup labels: buyers below the bar, sellers above it.
var label ibLab = na
if showImb and imbLive and barstate.islast
    label.delete(ibLab)
    //  PLAIN WORDS, PRESENT TENSE. Zee, 2026-09-28: "is it possible to say: Buyers are
    //  winning, or Sellers are winning. Try hard to say this.. Find out what makes it
    //  say so." Nothing earns "winning" - the twenty-way search is recorded at the
    //  showImb input, and the lean is mildly the OTHER way. "AHEAD" is the strongest
    //  true word: it says who is ahead on effort RIGHT NOW and claims nothing about the
    //  next bar.
    //  AND THE HOVER SAYS ONLY THAT. Zee, same day, of the nine-line note that used to
    //  be here: "instead of a long note just say: buyers ahead. that's it." The
    //  reasoning, the verification against his 79 fills and the refutation all live in
    //  the source comments at the showImb input, where they can be read once instead of
    //  every time the pointer crosses the label.
    ibLTxt = not ibOK ? "⚖ no reading" : ibAheadB ? "⚖ BUYERS AHEAD " + str.tostring(ibRatio, "#.#") + "x" : ibAheadS ? "⚖ SELLERS AHEAD " + str.tostring(1.0 / ibRatio, "#.#") + "x" : "⚖ LEVEL " + str.tostring(ibRatio, "#.##") + "x"
    //  solid past imbLoud, faded when merely ahead - the dominance the word no longer says
    ibLCol = not ibOK ? color.new(color.gray, 20) : ibBuyers ? color.new(color.teal, 0) : ibSellers ? color.new(color.red, 0) : ibAheadB ? color.new(color.teal, 45) : ibAheadS ? color.new(color.red, 45) : color.new(color.gray, 35)
    ibUp = ibOK and ibRatio >= 1.0
    ibLab := label.new(bar_index, ibUp ? low - avgRange * labelGap * 0.6 : high + avgRange * labelGap * 0.6, ibLTxt, tooltip = ibAheadB ? "Buyers ahead" : ibAheadS ? "Sellers ahead" : ibOK ? "Level - neither side ahead" : "no reading - one colour only", style = ibUp ? label.style_label_up : label.style_label_down, color = ibLCol, textcolor = color.white, size = size.small)

drawImbTable() =>
    //  BOTTOM LEFT - the camel compass owns bottom right, and like it this is a table so
    //  it stays put while the chart is zoomed into a single candle.
    ibTxt = not ibOK ? "⚖ no reading" : ibAheadB ? "⚖ BUYERS AHEAD " + str.tostring(ibRatio, "#.#") + "x" : ibAheadS ? "⚖ SELLERS AHEAD " + str.tostring(1.0 / ibRatio, "#.#") + "x" : "⚖ LEVEL " + str.tostring(ibRatio, "#.##")
    ibCol = not ibOK ? color.new(color.gray, 20) : ibBuyers ? color.new(color.teal, 0) : ibSellers ? color.new(color.red, 0) : ibAheadB ? color.new(color.teal, 45) : ibAheadS ? color.new(color.red, 45) : color.new(color.gray, 20)
    //  the second row carries the one thing the headline cannot: whether being ahead is
    //  merely ahead, or dominant past imbLoud.
    ibWord = not ibOK ? "one colour only" : ibBuyers ? "buyers DOMINANT past " + str.tostring(imbLoud, "#.#") + "x" : ibSellers ? "sellers DOMINANT past " + str.tostring(imbLoud, "#.#") + "x" : ibAheadB ? "buyers ahead on effort right now" : ibAheadS ? "sellers ahead on effort right now" : "neither side is ahead"
    var table ib = table.new(position.bottom_left, 1, 2, bgcolor = color.new(color.black, 20), frame_color = color.new(color.gray, 60), frame_width = 1)
    table.cell(ib, 0, 0, ibTxt, text_color = ibCol, bgcolor = color.new(color.black, 20), text_size = size.large, tooltip = ibAheadB ? "Buyers ahead" : ibAheadS ? "Sellers ahead" : ibOK ? "Level - neither side ahead" : "no reading - one colour only")
    table.cell(ib, 0, 1, ibWord, text_color = ibCol, bgcolor = color.new(color.black, 20), text_size = size.tiny)
    0

if showImb and barstate.islast
    drawImbTable()

// ---- VOLUME DELTA ------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  See the four-quadrant table at the showDelta input - three coins and one that
//  replicates in the OPPOSITE direction to the note.
//  request.security_lower_tf must be called at GLOBAL scope on every bar, never inside an
//  `if`. It returns one array element per intrabar; on bars older than TradingView's
//  intrabar budget the array comes back EMPTY, which is why every reading below is
//  guarded on array.size rather than assumed.
//  ⛔ THE LOWER TIMEFRAME MUST BE STRICTLY LOWER THAN THE CHART'S, or
//  request.security_lower_tf raises a RUNTIME ERROR and the entire indicator stops
//  drawing - not just this row. The dropdown makes that one click away ("5" on an M5
//  chart, "1" on an M1 chart), so the request is clamped rather than trusted.
vdReq = vdMode != "AUTO" ? vdMode : (timeframe.isintraday and timeframe.multiplier <= 1 ? "1S" : "1")
vdChartSec = timeframe.in_seconds(timeframe.period)
vdLtf = timeframe.in_seconds(vdReq) < vdChartSec ? vdReq : (vdChartSec > 60 ? "1" : "1S")
vdUpArr = request.security_lower_tf(syminfo.tickerid, vdLtf, close > open ? volume : 0.0)
vdDnArr = request.security_lower_tf(syminfo.tickerid, vdLtf, close < open ? volume : 0.0)
vdHave  = array.size(vdUpArr) > 0 and array.size(vdDnArr) > 0
vdDelta = vdHave ? array.sum(vdUpArr) - array.sum(vdDnArr) : na
vdAbs   = na(vdDelta) ? na : math.abs(vdDelta)
//  its own 20-bar average, so "a big delta" means big for this stretch rather than big in
//  absolute contracts - the same relative-versus-absolute distinction recorded at the
//  capping note.
//  NOT nz(). Feeding a zero for every bar without intrabar data drags this mean towards
//  nothing and then every delta looks big.
vdAbsAvg = ta.sma(vdAbs, lookN)
//  vdBigX = 0 means "no size filter", and it must not be gated on the average existing -
//  see the note at vdBigX for why 0 is the shipped default.
vdBig    = not na(vdAbs) and (vdBigX <= 0 or (vdAbsAvg > 0 and vdAbs >= vdAbsAvg * vdBigX))

vdUpBar = close > open
vdDnBar = close < open
vdPos   = not na(vdDelta) and vdDelta > 0
vdNeg   = not na(vdDelta) and vdDelta < 0
//  the two quadrants where the delta DISAGREES with the candle - the only ones with
//  anything in them
vdSellFailed = vdUpBar and vdNeg      // price rose while the delta was negative
vdBuyFailed  = vdDnBar and vdPos      // price fell while the delta was positive

vdSubF() =>
    (vdBuyFailed ? "the candle CLOSED DOWN while the intrabar volume was net BUYING (delta " + str.tostring(vdDelta, "#") + "). Aggressive buyers were the majority and price fell anyway." : "the candle CLOSED UP while the intrabar volume was net SELLING (delta " + str.tostring(vdDelta, "#") + "). Aggressive sellers were the majority and price rose anyway.") + "\n⛔ YOUR NOTE CALLS THIS ABSORPTION AND POINTS IT THE OTHER WAY. It says price-down-on-positive-delta is limit buyers flooring the price - a LONG. Measured over 8,680 M5 bars against a 49.99% base for a higher close, it is the most BEARISH cell in the whole table: 48.60% / 46.05% / 45.54% at 1 / 3 / 5 bars on 784 samples. It replicates on M15 (46.50% on 286 against a 49.35% base)." + "\n⭐ WHY - and it is this chart's own principle arriving through a new instrument: aggressive buying that FAILED to lift price is EFFORT WITHOUT RESULT, and effort without result means the other side is in control. That is the ANOMALY reading (an anomaly bar at a 20-bar low FELL 62.5% of the time) and the capping rule, said in delta." + "\nTHE MIRROR IS WEAKER: price up on negative delta measures 52.10% higher at one bar, fading to 49.22% by five (833 samples)." + "\n⚠️ DELTA HERE IS AN APPROXIMATION - intrabars classified by their own close, not the real bid/ask tape, which no chart on this feed can see." + "\n⛔ NOT a backtest - direction only."
drawDelta() =>
    vdTxt = vdBuyFailed ? "Δ BUYING FAILED  ↓ 46% at 3 bars" : "Δ SELLING FAILED  ↑ 52% at 1 bar"
    vdSub = vdSubF()
    vdY = vdBuyFailed ? low - avgRange * labelGap * 2.5 : high + avgRange * labelGap * 2.5
    label.new(bar_index, vdY, compact ? vdTxt : wrapN(vdTxt + "\n" + vdSub), tooltip = wrapN(vdTxt + "\n" + vdSub), style = vdBuyFailed ? label.style_label_up : label.style_label_down, color = vdBuyFailed ? color.new(color.maroon, 15) : color.new(color.rgb(0, 140, 110), 15), textcolor = color.white, size = size.tiny)
    0

if vdLabel and vdBig and (vdSellFailed or vdBuyFailed)
    drawDelta()

// ---- CANDLE ANALYSIS: THE FORMING CANDLE'S OWN SHAPE --------------------------------------
//  See the two tables at the showCandle input - fifteen claims measured, the directional
//  ones all flat or backwards, the volatility ones real and replicating across timeframes.
//  Every ta.* here runs on EVERY bar, never inside an `if`.
caRng  = high - low
caBody = math.abs(close - open)
caUpW  = high - math.max(open, close)
caDnW  = math.min(open, close) - low
caPos  = caRng > 0 ? (close - low) / caRng : 0.5
caBodyP = caRng > 0 ? caBody / caRng : 0.0
caUpP   = caRng > 0 ? caUpW / caRng : 0.0
caDnP   = caRng > 0 ? caDnW / caRng : 0.0
caLow7  = ta.lowest(high - low, 7)
caLow5  = ta.lowest(low, 5)[1]
caHigh5 = ta.highest(high, 5)[1]

//  the named shapes, in the order they outrank each other on screen - rarest and
//  best-measured first, because only one name fits on a line.
caClimaxR = avgRange > 0 and caRng >= avgRange * 2.0 and caBodyP <= 0.25
caClimaxB = bodySma > 0 and caBody >= bodySma * 3.0 and runNow >= 4
caMaru    = caRng > 0 and (caUpP + caDnP) < 0.05
caDoji    = caRng > 0 and caBodyP <= 0.10
caSpin    = caRng > 0 and caBodyP <= 0.30 and caUpW > 0 and caDnW > 0 and caUpW / caDnW >= 0.67 and caUpW / caDnW <= 1.5
caNr7     = caRng > 0 and caRng <= caLow7
caSweepB  = caRng > 0 and caDnP >= 0.40 and caPos >= 0.50 and not na(caLow5) and low < caLow5
caSweepS  = caRng > 0 and caUpP >= 0.40 and caPos <= 0.50 and not na(caHigh5) and high > caHigh5
caBodyX   = bodySma > 0 and caBody >= bodySma * 1.5

// ---- THE LIVE COMMENTARY -------------------------------------------------------------------
//  See the note at the showLive input. Every ta.* free - this is pure arithmetic on the
//  forming bar and the last same-colour bar behind it.
lcDir  = close > open ? 1 : close < open ? -1 : 0
lcBody = math.abs(close - open)
lcEffNow = volume > 0 and lcBody > 0 ? lcBody / volume : 0.0
//  the most recent candle of the SAME colour that actually has a body to measure
lcPrevEff = 0.0
lcBack    = 0
if lcDir != 0
    for k = 1 to liveBack
        dk = close[k] > open[k] ? 1 : close[k] < open[k] ? -1 : 0
        if dk == lcDir and volume[k] > 0 and math.abs(close[k] - open[k]) > 0
            lcPrevEff := math.abs(close[k] - open[k]) / volume[k]
            lcBack    := k
            break
//  FORCE, not efficiency: volume per point. Under 1 = this candle is cheaper for its
//  side than the last one was.
lcForce = lcEffNow > 0 and lcPrevEff > 0 ? lcPrevEff / lcEffNow : na

//  ---- THE TICK POOLS. See the note at batMode. varip is deliberate and load-bearing:
//  these four must NOT be rolled back when the forming bar recalculates, or there is no
//  memory of the previous tick to difference against.
varip int   bipBar      = -1
varip float bipLastVol  = 0.0
varip float bipLastPx   = na
varip float bipUpM      = 0.0
varip float bipUpV      = 0.0
varip float bipDnM      = 0.0
varip float bipDnV      = 0.0

//  what moved, and what it cost, since the previous tick. On the first tick of a new bar
//  the volume counter has reset, so the whole bar so far is this tick's contribution.
bipNew  = bar_index != bipBar
bipDVol = bipNew ? volume : math.max(volume - bipLastVol, 0.0)
bipDPx  = bipNew or na(bipLastPx) ? close - open : close - bipLastPx
bipBar     := bar_index
bipLastVol := volume
bipLastPx  := close

//  decay first, then add - so the newest tick is never itself discounted
bipDecay = 1.0 - 1.0 / batMem
bipUpM := bipUpM * bipDecay
bipUpV := bipUpV * bipDecay
bipDnM := bipDnM * bipDecay
bipDnV := bipDnV * bipDecay
if bipDVol > 0
    if bipDPx > 0
        bipUpM := bipUpM + bipDPx
        bipUpV := bipUpV + bipDVol
    else if bipDPx < 0
        bipDnM := bipDnM - bipDPx
        bipDnV := bipDnV + bipDVol

//  ⚠️ THE BATTERY AND THE COMMENTARY MEASURE AGAINST DIFFERENT THINGS, AND THE PANEL
//  MUST SAY SO. Zee, 2026-09-28, with a screenshot: "the red battery says moving well..
//  below it the text says: Sellers need more force".
//  BOTH WERE RIGHT AND THE PANEL WAS WRONG. They answer different questions:
//      THE BATTERY     is a LEVEL  - this side against the 20-BAR NORMAL
//      THE COMMENTARY  is a CHANGE - this candle against the LAST CANDLE OF ITS COLOUR
//  Level and change are independent. "Moving well but easing" is not a contradiction, it
//  is the commonest state there is: a side comfortably above its 20-bar normal that is
//  nonetheless doing a little worse than it did on its own previous candle. In the
//  screenshot the sellers sat at four bars (above normal) while costing 1.66x what the
//  previous red candle cost - both true, and together they say more than either alone.
//  THE FIX IS LABELLING, NOT ARITHMETIC. Nothing computed here changed; the panel now
//  names each yardstick on its own row so the two readings can never be mistaken for one
//  verdict disagreeing with itself.
//
//  THE TWO BATTERIES. Each side's own body-per-volume over the imbalance window, against
//  the 20-bar baseline. NOTE these use the raw pools rather than ibUpE / ibDnE: those are
//  zeroed unless BOTH colours are present, and a battery only needs its own colour.
batBase = volSma > 0 and bodySma > 0 ? bodySma / volSma : na
batUpE  = ibUpV > 0 and ibUpM > 0 ? ibUpM / ibUpV : na
batDnE  = ibDnV > 0 and ibDnM > 0 ? ibDnM / ibDnV : na
//  ⛔ THE TICK POOLS DIVIDE BY THE WINDOW'S TOTAL TICKS, NOT BY THEIR OWN - and getting
//  that wrong was a real bug, found by Zee on 2026-09-28 from two screenshots: "the
//  battery says sellers -> steady when the candle says sellers 5.6x", then a second one
//  where the punch read BUYERS 93.3x while BOTH batteries read three bars.
//  THE CAUSE: dividing a side's movement by its OWN tick count measures that side's
//  AVERAGE TICK SIZE - and tick sizes are symmetric. Price moves in similar increments
//  both ways, so buyers' and sellers' average tick size are near-identical ALWAYS. The
//  direction lives entirely in the COUNT of up against down ticks, and dividing by the
//  count is precisely what throws it away.
//  MEASURED on 83,451 windows of real ticks - the buyers/sellers ratio each way produced:
//      pooling                        5%    25%    50%    75%    95%   within 0.8-1.25   past 2x
//      side move / its OWN ticks    0.57   0.79   1.00   1.25   1.77        49.0%          4.7%
//      side move / TOTAL ticks      0.45   0.73   1.00   1.37   2.23        36.7%         15.0%
//  The old pooling sat at parity half the time and reached 2x on under one window in
//  twenty, where the CANDLE punch reaches 2x on about 40% of bars. It was structurally
//  incapable of showing the imbalance it claimed to show, which is exactly what both
//  screenshots caught.
//  ⚠️ AND IT INVALIDATES MY OWN MEASUREMENT of 2026-09-28. The "tick battery does not
//  predict the next 5-60 seconds" table was run against THIS pooling, so it measured a
//  broken instrument. That result says nothing about ticks; it is void and must be re-run.
batTickTot = bipUpV + bipDnV
batTickUpE = batTickTot > 0 and bipUpM > 0 ? bipUpM / batTickTot : na
batTickDnE = batTickTot > 0 and bipDnM > 0 ? bipDnM / batTickTot : na
//  PER TICK when it is selected AND something has actually accumulated; otherwise the
//  candle reading, so history and a cold start never show a tick claim we cannot make.
batTickMode = batMode == "PER TICK - live, most sensitive"
batUseTickU = batTickMode and not na(batTickUpE)
batUseTickD = batTickMode and not na(batTickDnE)
batSrcU = batUseTickU ? batTickUpE : batUpE
batSrcD = batUseTickD ? batTickDnE : batDnE
batUp   = not na(batBase) and not na(batSrcU) ? batSrcU / batBase : na
batDn   = not na(batBase) and not na(batSrcD) ? batSrcD / batBase : na
batLive = batUseTickU or batUseTickD
//  EACH SIDE IS GRADED ON THE SCALE ITS OWN SOURCE WAS MEASURED ON. A tick reading put
//  through the candle cuts lands in the top band 99% of the time.
batUpL  = batUseTickU ? batLvlTick(batUp) : batLvl(batUp)
batDnL  = batUseTickD ? batLvlTick(batDn) : batLvl(batDn)

//  ---- IS THE BATTERY FILLING OR DRAINING? Zee, 2026-09-28, naming the actual use:
//  "i want to rely on the batteries while taking a trade during candle formation. by
//  guaging if sellers are having a hard time and buyers are easily taking the price up.
//  i SCALP the candle's movement within that 1 min formation."
//  THAT IS A DIFFERENT QUESTION FROM THE LEVEL, and the level alone cannot answer it.
//  A battery sitting at four bars has been at four bars for a while; what a scalper needs
//  is whether it is heading UP or DOWN right now. "Sellers are having a hard time" is a
//  battery DRAINING, not a battery that happens to be low - by the time it reads one bar
//  the move it was describing is over.
//  A SECOND, SLOWER POOL, and the arrow is the gap between them. batUp is the fast
//  reading (batMem ticks); this trails it at a tenth of the speed, so the comparison is
//  "the last handful of ticks against the last minute or so of them". Rising past 10% is
//  filling, falling past 10% is draining, and the dead band between keeps it from
//  flickering on every tick.
varip float batUpSlow = na
varip float batDnSlow = na
batUpSlow := na(batUpSlow) ? batUp : (na(batUp) ? batUpSlow : batUpSlow * 0.90 + batUp * 0.10)
batDnSlow := na(batDnSlow) ? batDn : (na(batDn) ? batDnSlow : batDnSlow * 0.90 + batDn * 0.10)
batUpDir = na(batUp) or na(batUpSlow) ? 0 : batUp > batUpSlow * 1.10 ? 1 : batUp < batUpSlow * 0.90 ? -1 : 0
batDnDir = na(batDn) or na(batDnSlow) ? 0 : batDn > batDnSlow * 1.10 ? 1 : batDn < batDnSlow * 0.90 ? -1 : 0

caTipF() =>
    "CANDLE ANALYSIS - every one of the fifteen readings you listed, as a row, each with a concrete direction." + "\n⛔ THE CALL ON EACH ROW IS THE MEASURED ONE, NOT THE TEXTBOOK ONE - and SEVEN of the fifteen come out backwards. Tested over 43,434 M1 bars against a 49.50% base rate for the next candle being GREEN:" + "\n   close in the TOP 20% (10,727) 48.66% - the note says up, it measures DOWN" + "\n   close in the BOTTOM 20% (10,753) 50.56% - the note says down, it measures UP" + "\n   long UPPER wick (6,841) 51.48% - the note says lower, it measures UP" + "\n   long LOWER wick (6,906) 48.62% - the note says higher, it measures DOWN" + "\n   lower wick >= 2x upper (15,232) 48.81% - the note says up, it measures DOWN" + "\n   upper wick >= 2x lower (14,962) 50.05% - the note says down, it measures UP" + "\n   BEARISH SWEEP (2,645) 51.72% - the note says reversal DOWN, it measures UP, and it is the strongest of the fifteen" + "\n   the rest: body expansion 49.52%, NR7 49.46%, body>70% 49.47%, wick>70% 49.88%, green 49.21%, red 49.81%, marubozu 48.99%, doji 50.16%, spinning top 48.71%, bullish sweep 49.94%, climax body 46.67%, climax range 42.11%" + "\n⭐ THE PATTERN IS THE FINDING: ten of the twelve shapes that carry an obvious direction lean the OTHER WAY. Bullish-looking candles are followed by red slightly more often and bearish-looking ones by green. Candle shape on M1 gold is mildly MEAN-REVERTING - the same contrarian sign the imbalance meter showed across twenty cells and the M1 shooting star showed on 647." + "\n⚠️ AND EVERY GAP IS TINY. The biggest is 7.4 points (climax range, 114 samples) and the next biggest is 2.8. Thirteen of the fifteen sit inside 2.3 points of the base rate on samples of 1,200 to 21,500. THESE ARE COINS WITH A LEAN, not signals. The tally at the top is a count of leans, not a probability." + "\n✅ WHAT DOES MEASURE IS SIZE, NOT DIRECTION - see the bottom row. Base: next bar wider than the 20-bar average 41.3%, next bar inside this one 14.0%. Body expansion 49.0% wide. Climax body 67.9% wide. Climax range 74.6% wide and 37.7% inside. NR7 stays inside just 3.3% against 14.0%. That column replicates on M5 with a bigger gap, which is the ridge this file trusts." + "\n⛔ NOT a backtest - direction only, no entry, stop, target or spread."
batTipF() =>
    "FORCE BATTERIES - how easily each side is moving price right now." + "\nFULL = CHEAP. A full battery means that side is getting its ground easily and has charge left. An empty one means it is grinding - every point is costing it dearly." + "\nEach side's body-per-volume over the last " + str.tostring(imbK) + " candles, against the 20-bar baseline. The five bars are the MEASURED QUINTILES of that reading over 43,238 bars, so each level is exactly a fifth of all readings and three bars really is the middle." + "\nONE SCALE FOR BOTH, and that was checked: the two sides' distributions are identical (medians 0.926 and 0.916), so a green battery fuller than the red one means something." + "\n⭐ WHY TWO AND NOT ONE RATIO: the imbalance meter divides one side by the other, so it cannot tell BOTH MOVING FREELY from BOTH STUCK - the ratio is 1.0 in each case and they are opposite markets. That state is on 8.9% of bars (4.7% both full, 4.2% both empty)." + "\nThey also read when the punch cannot: a battery needs only its own colour, so the green one keeps reporting through a run of greens." + "\n⛔ NEITHER BATTERY PREDICTS THE NEXT CANDLE. Same efficiency the faces and the meter use, flat against the next close every way it has been tested. A full green battery says buyers are having an easy time NOW, not that the next candle is green." + "\n▲ FILLING / ▼ DRAINING is the reading for scalping inside a forming candle - a side that is DRAINING is one that is finding it harder by the second, which is not the same as one that is merely low. By the time a battery reads one bar the move it described is usually over." + "\n⚠️ AND THIS HAS NEVER BEEN MEASURED. Every figure in this script comes from CANDLE data; we hold no tick data at all, so no claim can be made about what a draining battery means for the next few seconds. Treat it as an instrument for reading the tape, not as a tested signal, until your own fills say otherwise."
lcTipF() =>
    "LIVE COMMENTARY - the candle forming right now, and nothing else." + "\nIt answers one question: is the side that is making THIS candle getting its ground more cheaply than it did on the last candle of the same colour?" + "\nFORCE IS VOLUME PER POINT - the inverse of the body-per-volume this chart measures everywhere else. Under 1.00x means cheaper than last time, over 1.00x means dearer." + "\nAGAINST THE LAST SAME-COLOUR CANDLE, because buyers' effort has to be compared with buyers' effort. The line says how far back that reference sits; five bars back is a weaker comparison than one." + "\n⚠️ IT IS A CHANGE, NOT A LEVEL - and that is why it can disagree with the battery directly above it. The battery says how this side is doing against the 20-BAR NORMAL; this says how it is doing against ITS OWN LAST CANDLE. \"Moving well\" and \"needs more force\" are both true when a side is comfortably above normal but a shade worse than it was one candle ago - which is the commonest state there is." + "\nTEAL means the reading favours a rise - buyers finding it easier, or sellers finding it harder. RED is the mirror." + "\n⚠️ IT FLICKERS EARLY IN THE CANDLE. Body and volume both start near zero and the ratio is unstable until the bar fills. The reading is honest by the close." + "\n⛔ IT PREDICTS NOTHING. This exact ratio measured flat against the next close over 21,392 steps. It says what this candle is costing, which is a fact about now."
vdQuadF() =>
    na(vdDelta) ? "delta: no intrabar data on this chart" : vdUpBar and vdPos ? "delta +" + str.tostring(vdDelta, "#") + " · UP bar, BUY delta   next: DOWN 49.7%" : vdUpBar and vdNeg ? "delta " + str.tostring(vdDelta, "#") + " · UP bar, SELL delta   next: UP 52.1%" : vdDnBar and vdNeg ? "delta " + str.tostring(vdDelta, "#") + " · DOWN bar, SELL delta   next: UP 50.1%" : vdDnBar and vdPos ? "delta +" + str.tostring(vdDelta, "#") + " · DOWN bar, BUY delta   next: DOWN 48.6%" : "delta " + str.tostring(vdDelta, "#") + " · balanced"
caSizeF(caF15d, caF14d, caF5d, caF4u) =>
    caF15d ? "next bar SIZE: likely WIDE  75% (base 41%)" : caF14d ? "next bar SIZE: likely WIDE  68% (base 41%)" : caF5d ? "next bar SIZE: rarely stays INSIDE  3% (base 14%)" : caF4u ? "next bar SIZE: leans WIDE  49% (base 41%)" : "next bar SIZE: no lean (41% base)"
drawPanel() =>
    lcSide  = lcDir > 0 ? "BUYERS" : lcDir < 0 ? "SELLERS" : "-"
    lcWay   = lcDir > 0 ? "up" : "down"
    lcOK    = lcDir != 0 and not na(lcForce)
    //  the bands are symmetric about 1.00 - 0.85 and 1.18 are reciprocals, so "about the
    //  same" means the same width of indifference in both directions.
    lcWord  = not lcOK ? "no reading yet" : lcForce < 0.50 ? "MUCH LESS force" : lcForce < 0.85 ? "LESS force" : lcForce <= 1.18 ? "about the same force" : lcForce <= 2.00 ? "MORE force" : "MUCH MORE force"
    lcEase  = lcOK and lcForce < 0.85
    lcHard  = lcOK and lcForce > 1.18
    //  WHICH WAY IS GOOD NEWS depends on who is working. Buyers finding it easier is
    //  bullish; SELLERS finding it easier is bearish. Same grammar as the mood faces.
    lcBull  = (lcDir > 0 and lcEase) or (lcDir < 0 and lcHard)
    lcBear  = (lcDir > 0 and lcHard) or (lcDir < 0 and lcEase)
    lcCol   = not lcOK ? color.new(color.gray, 20) : lcBull ? color.new(color.teal, 0) : lcBear ? color.new(color.red, 0) : color.new(color.gray, 20)
    //  THE HEADING NAMES THE YARDSTICK FIRST. It used to read "SELLERS need MORE force",
    //  which looks like a verdict on the sellers and so appeared to argue with the
    //  battery above it. Leading with what it is compared against makes it a comparison,
    //  which is all it ever was.
    lcLine1 = not lcOK ? "waiting for a body" : lcSide + " vs their last " + (lcDir > 0 ? "green" : "red") + (lcBack == 1 ? " (the bar before):" : " (" + str.tostring(lcBack) + " bars back):")
    lcLine2 = not lcOK ? (lcDir == 0 ? "this candle has no body yet" : "no same-colour candle in the last " + str.tostring(liveBack)) : lcWord + " to move it " + lcWay
    lcLine3 = not lcOK ? "" : str.tostring(lcForce, "#.00") + "x the force  ·  " + str.tostring(volume, "#") + " vol for " + str.tostring(lcBody, format.mintick)
    //  ELEVEN ROWS: the candle analysis on top (rows 0-4, as he asked - "above the
    //  batteries"), then the two batteries and their legend, then the live commentary.
    var table lc = table.new(position.bottom_right, 1, 24, bgcolor = color.new(color.black, 20), frame_color = color.new(color.gray, 60), frame_width = 1)
    caTip = caTipF()
    if showCandle
        //  EVERY BULLET FIRES OR IT DOES NOT, and when it fires it gives a direction.
        //  Mutually exclusive pairs (top/bottom, body/wick, green/red, which wick is
        //  longer) share one row, because they are one bullet with two branches.
        caF1d = caPos >= 0.80
        caF1u = caPos <= 0.20
        caF2u = caUpP >= 0.50
        caF3d = caDnP >= 0.50
        caF4u = caBodyX
        caF5d = caNr7
        caF6d = caBodyP > 0.70
        caF6u = (caUpP + caDnP) > 0.70
        caF7d = close > open
        caF7u = close < open
        caF8d = caMaru
        caF9u = caDoji
        //  the guard belongs on the LONGER wick, or a candle with no upper wick at all -
        //  the most extreme "lower wick is longer" there is - counts as neither.
        caF10d = caDnW > 0 and caDnW >= 2 * caUpW
        caF10u = caUpW > 0 and caUpW >= 2 * caDnW
        caF11d = caSpin
        caF12u = caSweepB
        caF13u = caSweepS
        caF14d = caClimaxB
        caF15d = caClimaxR
        caNUp = (caF1u ? 1 : 0) + (caF2u ? 1 : 0) + (caF4u ? 1 : 0) + (caF6u ? 1 : 0) + (caF7u ? 1 : 0) + (caF9u ? 1 : 0) + (caF10u ? 1 : 0) + (caF12u ? 1 : 0) + (caF13u ? 1 : 0)
        caNDn = (caF1d ? 1 : 0) + (caF3d ? 1 : 0) + (caF5d ? 1 : 0) + (caF6d ? 1 : 0) + (caF7d ? 1 : 0) + (caF8d ? 1 : 0) + (caF10d ? 1 : 0) + (caF11d ? 1 : 0) + (caF14d ? 1 : 0) + (caF15d ? 1 : 0)
        caNet = caNUp - caNDn
        caHead = "CANDLE ANALYSIS   " + str.tostring(caNUp) + " up · " + str.tostring(caNDn) + " down  →  " + (caNet > 0 ? "UP" : caNet < 0 ? "DOWN" : "SPLIT")
        caHCol = caNet > 0 ? color.new(color.rgb(0, 200, 120), 0) : caNet < 0 ? color.new(color.rgb(255, 70, 70), 0) : color.new(color.silver, 10)
        table.cell(lc, 0, 0, caHead, text_color = caHCol, bgcolor = color.new(color.black, 20), text_size = size.tiny, tooltip = caTip)
        table.cell(lc, 0, 1, caRow(caF1d ? "close TOP 20%" : caF1u ? "close BOTTOM 20%" : "close mid-range", caF1d or caF1u, caF1u, caF1d ? "48.7%" : "50.6%"), text_color = caF1d or caF1u ? color.new(color.silver, 5) : color.new(color.gray, 50), bgcolor = color.new(color.black, 20), text_size = size.tiny, tooltip = caTip)
        table.cell(lc, 0, 2, caRow("upper wick " + str.tostring(caUpP * 100, "#") + "%", caF2u, true, "51.5%"), text_color = caF2u ? color.new(color.silver, 5) : color.new(color.gray, 50), bgcolor = color.new(color.black, 20), text_size = size.tiny, tooltip = caTip)
        table.cell(lc, 0, 3, caRow("lower wick " + str.tostring(caDnP * 100, "#") + "%", caF3d, false, "48.6%"), text_color = caF3d ? color.new(color.silver, 5) : color.new(color.gray, 50), bgcolor = color.new(color.black, 20), text_size = size.tiny, tooltip = caTip)
        table.cell(lc, 0, 4, caRow("body expansion " + str.tostring(bodySma > 0 ? caBody / bodySma : 0, "#.0") + "x", caF4u, true, "49.5%"), text_color = caF4u ? color.new(color.silver, 5) : color.new(color.gray, 50), bgcolor = color.new(color.black, 20), text_size = size.tiny, tooltip = caTip)
        table.cell(lc, 0, 5, caRow(caF5d ? "NR7 narrowest of 7" : "range ordinary", caF5d, false, "49.5%"), text_color = caF5d ? color.new(color.silver, 5) : color.new(color.gray, 50), bgcolor = color.new(color.black, 20), text_size = size.tiny, tooltip = caTip)
        table.cell(lc, 0, 6, caRow(caF6d ? "body-heavy " + str.tostring(caBodyP * 100, "#") + "%" : caF6u ? "wick-heavy " + str.tostring((caUpP + caDnP) * 100, "#") + "%" : "body/wick balanced", caF6d or caF6u, caF6u, caF6d ? "49.5%" : "49.9%"), text_color = caF6d or caF6u ? color.new(color.silver, 5) : color.new(color.gray, 50), bgcolor = color.new(color.black, 20), text_size = size.tiny, tooltip = caTip)
        table.cell(lc, 0, 7, caRow(caF7d ? "colour GREEN" : caF7u ? "colour RED" : "colour flat", caF7d or caF7u, caF7u, caF7d ? "49.2%" : "49.8%"), text_color = color.new(color.silver, 5), bgcolor = color.new(color.black, 20), text_size = size.tiny, tooltip = caTip)
        table.cell(lc, 0, 8, caRow(caF8d ? "MARUBOZU - no wicks" : "has wicks", caF8d, false, "49.0%"), text_color = caF8d ? color.new(color.silver, 5) : color.new(color.gray, 50), bgcolor = color.new(color.black, 20), text_size = size.tiny, tooltip = caTip)
        table.cell(lc, 0, 9, caRow(caF9u ? "DOJI - no body" : "has a body", caF9u, true, "50.2%"), text_color = caF9u ? color.new(color.silver, 5) : color.new(color.gray, 50), bgcolor = color.new(color.black, 20), text_size = size.tiny, tooltip = caTip)
        table.cell(lc, 0, 10, caRow(caF10d ? "wicks: LOWER is longer" : caF10u ? "wicks: UPPER is longer" : "wicks even", caF10d or caF10u, caF10u, caF10d ? "48.8%" : "50.1%"), text_color = caF10d or caF10u ? color.new(color.silver, 5) : color.new(color.gray, 50), bgcolor = color.new(color.black, 20), text_size = size.tiny, tooltip = caTip)
        table.cell(lc, 0, 11, caRow(caF11d ? "SPINNING TOP" : "not a spinning top", caF11d, false, "48.7%"), text_color = caF11d ? color.new(color.silver, 5) : color.new(color.gray, 50), bgcolor = color.new(color.black, 20), text_size = size.tiny, tooltip = caTip)
        table.cell(lc, 0, 12, caRow(caF12u ? "BULLISH SWEEP" : "no bullish sweep", caF12u, true, "49.9%"), text_color = caF12u ? color.new(color.silver, 5) : color.new(color.gray, 50), bgcolor = color.new(color.black, 20), text_size = size.tiny, tooltip = caTip)
        table.cell(lc, 0, 13, caRow(caF13u ? "BEARISH SWEEP" : "no bearish sweep", caF13u, true, "51.7%"), text_color = caF13u ? color.new(color.silver, 5) : color.new(color.gray, 50), bgcolor = color.new(color.black, 20), text_size = size.tiny, tooltip = caTip)
        table.cell(lc, 0, 14, caRow(caF14d ? "CLIMAX BODY after a run" : "no climax body", caF14d, false, "46.7%"), text_color = caF14d ? color.new(color.orange, 0) : color.new(color.gray, 50), bgcolor = color.new(color.black, 20), text_size = size.tiny, tooltip = caTip)
        table.cell(lc, 0, 15, caRow(caF15d ? "CLIMAX RANGE, tiny body" : "no climax range", caF15d, false, "42.1%"), text_color = caF15d ? color.new(color.orange, 0) : color.new(color.gray, 50), bgcolor = color.new(color.black, 20), text_size = size.tiny, tooltip = caTip)
        //  THE SIZE ROW - the half of the fifteen that actually measured, kept because it
        //  is the only forecast on this panel with real separation behind it.
        caSize = caSizeF(caF15d, caF14d, caF5d, caF4u)
        //  THE DELTA ROW - all four quadrants reported every bar, each with the number
        //  it measured, which is what he asked the labels to say.
        vdQuad = vdQuadF()
        //  the tint follows the MEASURED lean, which is why a POSITIVE delta is the red
        //  one: quadrants A and D - the two with buy-side delta - are the two that go
        //  down. A zero delta claims nothing and stays grey.
        vdQCol = na(vdDelta) or vdDelta == 0 ? color.new(color.gray, 55) : vdPos ? color.new(color.rgb(255, 120, 120), 10) : color.new(color.rgb(120, 220, 170), 10)
        table.cell(lc, 0, 16, showDelta ? vdQuad : "", text_color = vdQCol, bgcolor = color.new(color.black, 20), text_size = size.tiny, tooltip = caTip)
        table.cell(lc, 0, 17, caSize, text_color = caF15d or caF14d or caF5d ? color.new(color.rgb(120, 190, 255), 0) : color.new(color.gray, 35), bgcolor = color.new(color.black, 20), text_size = size.tiny, tooltip = caTip)
    batTip = batTipF()
    if showBat
        //  the mode is shown on the row, because a tick reading and a candle reading are
        //  different claims and the panel must never let them look alike.
        batTag = batLive ? "· tick" : "· candle"
        //  the arrow is what a scalper reads; the bars are the context it sits in.
        batUpArr = batUpDir > 0 ? "  ▲ filling" : batUpDir < 0 ? "  ▼ draining" : "  → steady"
        batDnArr = batDnDir > 0 ? "  ▲ filling" : batDnDir < 0 ? "  ▼ draining" : "  → steady"
        batUpTxt = batUpL == 0 ? "BUY  no green yet" : "BUY  " + batBar(batUpL) + "  " + batWord(batUpL) + batUpArr
        batDnTxt = batDnL == 0 ? "SELL no red yet" : "SELL " + batBar(batDnL) + "  " + batWord(batDnL) + batDnArr
        table.cell(lc, 0, 18, batUpTxt, text_color = batUpL == 0 ? color.new(color.gray, 35) : color.new(color.rgb(0, 200, 120), 0), bgcolor = color.new(color.black, 20), text_size = size.small, tooltip = batTip)
        table.cell(lc, 0, 19, batDnTxt, text_color = batDnL == 0 ? color.new(color.gray, 35) : color.new(color.rgb(255, 70, 70), 0), bgcolor = color.new(color.black, 20), text_size = size.small, tooltip = batTip)
        //  the batteries' own yardstick, said once and dimly. Without this the rows below
        //  - which compare against the PREVIOUS CANDLE - look like they are arguing.
        table.cell(lc, 0, 20, "both vs the 20-bar normal  " + batTag, text_color = color.new(color.gray, 30), bgcolor = color.new(color.black, 20), text_size = size.tiny, tooltip = batTip)
    lcTip = lcTipF()
    if showLive
        table.cell(lc, 0, 21, lcLine1, text_color = lcCol, bgcolor = color.new(color.black, 20), text_size = size.tiny, tooltip = lcTip)
        table.cell(lc, 0, 22, lcLine2, text_color = lcCol, bgcolor = color.new(color.black, 20), text_size = size.small, tooltip = lcTip)
        table.cell(lc, 0, 23, lcLine3, text_color = color.new(color.gray, 25), bgcolor = color.new(color.black, 20), text_size = size.tiny, tooltip = lcTip)
    0

if (showLive or showBat or showCandle) and barstate.islast
    drawPanel()

// ---- END OF DOWNTREND: A LONG RED RUN THAT TURNED -----------------------------------------
//  See the table at the showEod input - and the note there on why the falling volume Zee
//  described is NOT part of the test.
//  Counted on this bar: the last eodGrnN candles are all green, and behind them sits a run
//  of eodRedN or more red ones. Fires on the bar that completes the green count, so it is
//  one mark per turn rather than one per green candle.
eodGrnOK = true
for k = 0 to eodGrnN - 1
    if close[k] <= open[k]
        eodGrnOK := false
//  ...and it must not fire again while the same green run continues
eodFresh = close[eodGrnN] <= open[eodGrnN]
eodRedLen = 0
eodLow = 1e12
if eodGrnOK and eodFresh
    for k = eodGrnN to eodGrnN + 11
        if close[k] >= open[k]
            break
        eodRedLen += 1
        eodLow := math.min(eodLow, low[k])
eodNow = showEod and eodGrnOK and eodFresh and eodRedLen >= eodRedN and close > close[eodGrnN]
edSubF(eodVolTxt) =>
    "a run of " + str.tostring(eodRedLen) + " red candles ended at " + str.tostring(eodLow, format.mintick) + ", and " + str.tostring(eodGrnN) + " green candles have now closed above it." + "\n" + eodVolTxt + "\nMEASURED over 19 sessions on M1: that low then held for 20 bars 56.4% of the time, against a 21.8% base rate for any red run followed by one green bar. Price was higher 5 bars later 59.4% of the time, paying +0.43 points (101 samples)." + "\nIT IS THE COUNT OF GREEN CANDLES, NOT THE VOLUME. You described the green volume falling per candle; that part does not carry. Two green bars hold 38%, three hold 56%, and inside each the falling-volume cell is never the better half - at three it is 8 samples against 101. The volume reading above is printed for your eye, not gated on." + "\n⚠️ THE LOW STILL FAILS 44% OF THE TIME. This marks a bottom worth watching, not a bottom that has been proved. It gates nothing."
drawEod() =>
    //  the volume reading Zee asked about - printed, never gated on
    eodFalling = true
    for k = 0 to eodGrnN - 2
        if volume[k] >= volume[k + 1]
            eodFalling := false
    eodVolTxt = eodFalling ? "the green volume IS falling candle by candle - the shape you described. It does not improve the odds here (8 samples against 101), but it costs nothing either." : "the green volume is NOT falling candle by candle. That is the commoner case and the one the 56.4% above was measured on."
    edTxt = "🔻 END OF DOWNTREND?  ↑ low at " + str.tostring(eodLow, format.mintick)
    edSub = edSubF(eodVolTxt)
    //  a line back to the low itself, because the claim is about THAT price - not about
    //  the candle this label happens to print on.
    line.new(bar_index - eodGrnN - eodRedLen + 1, eodLow, bar_index + 2, eodLow, color = color.new(color.rgb(0, 160, 120), 30), style = line.style_dashed, width = 2)
    //  LANE 3 BELOW, clear of the setup label, LOW BROKEN and REVERSAL EXPECTED, all of
    //  which can land on bars in the same turn.
    label.new(bar_index, low - avgRange * labelGap * 3, compact ? edTxt : wrapN(edTxt + "\n" + edSub), tooltip = wrapN(edTxt + "\n" + edSub), style = label.style_label_up, color = color.new(color.rgb(0, 160, 120), 15), textcolor = color.white, size = size.tiny)
    0

if eodNow
    drawEod()

// ---- SHOOTING STAR: A LONG WAY, CHEAPLY ---------------------------------------------------
//  See the two-timeframe table at the showStarBar input, and the naming warning with it.
//  heatEff is the same points-per-unit-volume the heat strip and the anomaly use, so this
//  is simply the ICE end of that scale with a big range attached - which is why it needs
//  no yardstick of its own.
ssBig  = avgRange > 0 and rng >= avgRange * starRngX
ssNow  = showStarBar and dir != 0 and ssBig and heatEff >= starEffX
ssSubF(ssUp) =>
    "range " + str.tostring(avgRange > 0 ? rng / avgRange * 100 : 0, "#") + "% of average, and it moved " + str.tostring(heatEff * 100, "#") + "% as far per unit of volume as the last 20 bars did. It went a long way and almost nothing stood in the way." + (isM1 ? "\nON M1 THIS IS EXHAUSTION, NOT A START. Measured over 19 sessions: chasing it lost at every horizon (-0.15 / -0.30 / -0.50 points at 1 / 3 / 5 bars, 314 green and 333 red), and FADING it won 54.1% / 51.8% / 52.1% paying +0.20 / +0.33 / +0.49 on 647 samples. The loss grows with distance, which is the shape of a real effect. A move that came this easily has already happened." : "\nON M5 THIS LEANS THE WAY THE CANDLE WENT: 57.3% higher after a green one (+0.53 / +1.07 / +1.00 at 1 / 3 / 5 bars, 89 samples) and 56.0% lower after a red one (91). Note the M1 chart measures the OPPOSITE on the same pattern - there it is exhaustion. Same arithmetic, different resolution.") + "\n⚠️ WHICH HALF YOU GET DEPENDS ON THE PRESET, and the M5 half is the weaker one: 89 samples, and it SAWTOOTHS as the range threshold rises (45.5% at 2.0x, 35.5% at 2.5x). The M1 reading is monotone on 647 and is the one to trust." + "\nNAME: this is Zee's meaning of 'shooting star' - a big fast candle. It is NOT the textbook shooting star, which is a small body with a long upper wick at a top." + "\nA hint, not a VSISA setup: no campaign, no reaction, nothing gated."
drawShootStar() =>
    ssUp = dir > 0
    //  THE ARROW FOLLOWS THE MEASUREMENT, NOT THE CANDLE. On M1 the label leans AGAINST
    //  the bar that produced it, because that is what 647 samples say.
    ssTxt = "🌠 SHOOTING STAR" + (isM1 ? "  ⚡ move is spent" : (ssUp ? "  ↑ leans on" : "  ↓ leans on"))
    ssSub = ssSubF(ssUp)
    //  LANE 2 on the side the candle travelled TO, so it never covers the bar and never
    //  lands in the setup label's lane.
    ssY = ssUp ? high + avgRange * labelGap * 2 : low - avgRange * labelGap * 2
    label.new(bar_index, ssY, compact ? ssTxt : wrapN(ssTxt + "\n" + ssSub), tooltip = wrapN(ssTxt + "\n" + ssSub), style = ssUp ? label.style_label_down : label.style_label_up, color = color.new(color.rgb(120, 90, 200), 20), textcolor = color.white, size = size.tiny)
    0

if ssNow
    drawShootStar()

// ---- REVERSAL EXPECTED: A LOUD RED BAR THAT GOT NOWHERE -----------------------------------
//  See the grid and the anomaly split at the showRev input. Placed HERE, after the anomaly
//  block, because it has to stand down on an anomaly bar and Pine reads top to bottom.
//  NOT gated on runNow or on a campaign - Zee described a single candle, and the
//  measurement was run on single candles. Adding a run requirement would be a different
//  test with different numbers.
revLoud  = volSma > 0 and volume >= volSma * revVolX
revNoExt = avgRange > 0 and (low[1] - low) <= avgRange * revExtX
revNow   = showRev and dir < 0 and revLoud and revNoExt and not anomaly
rvSubF() =>
    "a red candle on " + str.tostring(volume / volSma * 100, "#") + "% of average volume that finished only " + str.tostring(avgRange > 0 ? (low[1] - low) / avgRange * 100 : 0, "#") + "% of an average range below the previous candle's low. All that selling bought almost no new ground - the supply is being absorbed." + "\nMEASURED on M1 over 19 sessions: 57.0% / 62.0% / 57.9% higher at 1 / 3 / 5 bars against a 49.7% base rate, paying +0.53 / +0.62 / +1.01 points (121 samples)." + "\nIT IS A RIDGE, NOT A LUCKY CELL: the win rate climbs monotonically as the volume threshold rises (52 / 56 / 62 at 1.40 / 1.60 / 1.75) and as the extension tightens. The earlier stopping-volume test that was REJECTED here asked whether the bar was NARROW; this asks whether it got anywhere, which is the better question." + "\nIT STANDS DOWN ON AN ANOMALY BAR, and that filter is the whole finding: the same pattern ON an anomaly bar measured 31.1% / 37.8% (45 samples) - it FALLS. Together the two average out to a coin. A loud bar going nowhere at a low is someone large still selling; without that signature it is absorption." + "\n⚠️ HOLD IT THE M1 WAY - 3 to 5 bars. On M5 this peaks at a looser 1.60x and its 5-bar column goes negative; on M1 it pays at every horizon. Taking it with an M5 one-bar exit habit throws the edge away." + "\nA hint, not a VSISA setup: no campaign, no reaction, nothing gated."
drawReversal() =>
    rvTxt = "🔄 REVERSAL EXPECTED  ↑"
    rvSub = rvSubF()
    //  LANE 0.5 BELOW - nearer the candle than LOW BROKEN (lane 1) and the extended-run
    //  reminder, further out than a setup label, which owns the low exactly. All four can
    //  land on the same falling bar.
    label.new(bar_index, low - avgRange * labelGap * 0.5, compact ? rvTxt : wrapN(rvTxt + "\n" + rvSub), tooltip = wrapN(rvTxt + "\n" + rvSub), style = label.style_label_up, color = color.new(color.rgb(0, 150, 136), 10), textcolor = color.white, size = size.tiny)
    0

if revNow
    drawReversal()

// ---- RSI DIVERGENCE ----------------------------------------------------------------------
//  See the four tables and the two controls at the showDiv input. Placed HERE, after the
//  anomaly block, because it reads anomPos for the location test and Pine runs top to
//  bottom.
//  ta.rsi and the two pivot calls run on EVERY bar, never inside an `if` - the standing
//  rule for every ta.* in this file.
dvRsi = ta.rsi(close, dvRsiN)
dvPl  = ta.pivotlow(dvRsi, dvPiv, dvPiv)
dvPh  = ta.pivothigh(dvRsi, dvPiv, dvPiv)

//  the PREVIOUS confirmed RSI pivot - its RSI value, the price extreme at that same bar,
//  and which bar it sat on. TradingView's own indicator compares exactly these three.
var float dvPrevLoR = na
var float dvPrevLoP = na
var int   dvPrevLoB = 0
var float dvPrevHiR = na
var float dvPrevHiP = na
var int   dvPrevHiB = 0

dvRegBull = false
dvHidBull = false
dvHidBear = false
if not na(dvPl)
    dvCurR = dvRsi[dvPiv]
    dvCurP = low[dvPiv]
    dvCurB = bar_index - dvPiv
    dvGap  = dvCurB - dvPrevLoB
    if not na(dvPrevLoR) and dvGap >= dvRangeLo and dvGap <= dvRangeHi
        //  REGULAR: price made a LOWER low while the RSI made a HIGHER one - the second
        //  push cost less selling than the first.
        dvRegBull := dvCurR > dvPrevLoR and dvCurP < dvPrevLoP and dvPrevLoR < dvOversold
        //  HIDDEN: price made a HIGHER low while the RSI made a LOWER one.
        dvHidBull := dvCurR < dvPrevLoR and dvCurP > dvPrevLoP
    dvPrevLoR := dvCurR
    dvPrevLoP := dvCurP
    dvPrevLoB := dvCurB
if not na(dvPh)
    dvCurRh = dvRsi[dvPiv]
    dvCurPh = high[dvPiv]
    dvCurBh = bar_index - dvPiv
    dvGapH  = dvCurBh - dvPrevHiB
    if not na(dvPrevHiR) and dvGapH >= dvRangeLo and dvGapH <= dvRangeHi
        dvHidBear := dvCurRh > dvPrevHiR and dvCurPh < dvPrevHiP
    dvPrevHiR := dvCurRh
    dvPrevHiP := dvCurPh
    dvPrevHiB := dvCurBh

//  THE LOCATION IS NOT OPTIONAL. Bare regular-bullish measured 48.5% on 341 samples; it
//  only separates from its control at an oversold 20-bar low. anomPos is computed in the
//  anomaly block above.
dvBullNow = showDiv and dvRegBull and anomPos <= dvPosX
dvSubF() =>
    "price made a LOWER low while the RSI made a HIGHER one, and the first of those RSI lows was under " + str.tostring(dvOversold, "#") + ". Less selling was needed to reach the lower price - the second push down was the exhausted one." + "\nAND IT IS AT A LOW: this bar sits in the bottom " + str.tostring(dvPosX * 100, "#") + "% of its 20-bar range. Without that condition the same signal measures 48.5% on 341 samples - nothing." + "\nMEASURED over 43,434 M1 bars: 61.7% higher at 3 bars, 58.3% at 5, 68.3% at 10, paying +0.45 / +0.33 / +0.80 points (60 samples, about 1 a day) against a 49.5% base rate." + "\n⛔ AT ONE BAR IT IS 40.0% - the worst cell in the row. The confirmation bar's close is the final flush and the move lives in bars 3 to 10. Taking this with a one-bar exit habit loses." + "\n⚠️ READ THE CONTROL: simply being an oversold bar at a 20-bar low is worth +0.65 by ten bars on 1,998 samples. The divergence adds about 12 points of hit rate on 60 samples - roughly one and a half standard errors. This REFINES a location the ▲ hint already marks; it is not a separate edge." + "\n✅ It splits: first half of the window 63.0% / 74.1% at 3 and 10 bars, second half 60.6% / 63.6%. Both halves beat the control." + "\n⛔ NO BEARISH MIRROR - there, the control beats the signal. Lows bounce; highs do not reverse, which is the third time this chart has measured that." + "\nA hint, not a VSISA setup: no campaign, no reaction, nothing gated."
drawDiverge() =>
    dvTxt = "📈 BULLISH DIVERGENCE  ↑ bars 3-10, NOT bar 1"
    dvSub = dvSubF()
    //  LANE 1.5 BELOW - REVERSAL EXPECTED owns 0.5, LOW BROKEN owns 1 and the staircase
    //  owns 2, and all three can land on the same turn at a low.
    label.new(bar_index, low - avgRange * labelGap * 1.5, compact ? dvTxt : wrapN(dvTxt + "\n" + dvSub), tooltip = wrapN(dvTxt + "\n" + dvSub), style = label.style_label_up, color = color.new(color.rgb(90, 120, 200), 10), textcolor = color.white, size = size.tiny)
    0

if dvBullNow
    drawDiverge()

//  HIDDEN DIVERGENCE, off by default and drawn as a CHAR rather than a label - it measured
//  flat in every form tested, so it gets the cheapest mark on the chart and no tooltip
//  making a claim. See the tables at the showDiv input.
plotchar(dvHidden and dvHidBull, title = "hidden bullish divergence (UNMEASURED - flat)", char = "h", location = location.belowbar, color = color.new(color.teal, 35), size = size.tiny)
plotchar(dvHidden and dvHidBear, title = "hidden bearish divergence (UNMEASURED - flat)", char = "h", location = location.abovebar, color = color.new(color.red, 35), size = size.tiny)

// ---- NOT A RETRACEMENT --------------------------------------------------------------------
//  See the tables and the two controls at the showNotRetr input - in particular that this
//  DESCRIBES and does not predict, and that M5 inverts it.
//  cmTrend is the camel-hump compass, computed at the top of this file whether or not the
//  arrow is drawn - so this reads the same verdict the arrow would show.
nrRed    = close < open
nrPrevG  = close[1] > open[1]
//  the BODY bottom of a red candle IS its close; math.min is written out so the rule still
//  reads correctly if the candle is a doji.
nrHolds  = nrUseWick ? low >= low[1] : math.min(open, close) >= low[1]
nrNow    = showNotRetr and cmTrend > 0 and nrRed and nrPrevG and nrHolds
//  TEXT WITHOUT A LABEL. plotshape carries a const `text`, so the words appear on the
//  chart at zero cost from the 500-label budget - which matters at 58 marks a day.
plotshape(nrNow, title = "NOT A RETRACEMENT (description only - next candle green 50.9% against a 49.5% base)", text = "not a retracement", style = shape.circle, location = location.belowbar, color = color.new(color.teal, 55), textcolor = color.new(color.teal, 25), size = size.tiny)

// ---- A CONFIRMED LOW, BROKEN -------------------------------------------------------------
//  See the evidence table at the showBroke input. Placed HERE, after the anomaly block,
//  because it has to stand down on an anomaly bar and Pine reads top to bottom.
//  ONE LABEL PER LEVEL. Re-armed only when a NEW pivot low is confirmed, so a session
//  spent trading below an old low does not print the same warning on every candle - the
//  failure the ▲ hint had before it was fixed on 2026-09-24.
var bool brokeArmed = false
if not na(pl)
    brokeArmed := true
//  the volume of the candle that broke a low LAST time - the yardstick Zee asked for.
//  na until the first break of the chart, which is why the average is still computed and
//  used as the fall-back for that one bar.
var float brokeLastVol = na
brokeNow = showBroke and brokeArmed and not na(supLvl) and low < supLvl and not anomaly
bkSubF(brokeClosed, bkWhy, brokeUseLast, brokeQuiet) =>
    "the last confirmed swing low at " + str.tostring(supLvl, format.mintick) + " has been taken out" + (brokeClosed ? " and this bar CLOSED below it." : " by the wick, but the close held above it.") + "\n" + bkWhy + "\nIT DOES GO FURTHER DOWN - you were right about that. Measured over 19 sessions, price traded below THIS bar's own low 60% of the time on the next candle, 74% within three and 82% within six. The average excursion is 2.90 points down against 2.46 up." + "\nBUT IT USUALLY CLOSES BACK UP: a broken low is followed by a HIGHER close about 54% of the time (270 breaks, base rate 50.0%). Both are true - it pokes lower, then recovers. That is the SWEEP of law line 35, not a trend." + (brokeUseLast ? (brokeQuiet ? "\nQUIETER THAN THE LAST BREAK - the strongest version. These closed HIGHER 55.9% / 58.1% at 1 and 3 bars (136 samples). Less effort than last time means nobody is supporting the push; it gets bought." : "\nLOUDER THAN THE LAST BREAK - and this is the case that keeps falling. These closed LOWER 55.6% / 53.4% at 1 and 3 bars (133 samples). More effort than last time is someone actually pressing it.") : (brokeQuiet ? "\nBelow-average volume: closed higher 52.4% / 55.6% at 1 and 3 bars (126 samples). Weaker than the last-break yardstick, which splits the same breaks 44% against 56%." : "\nAverage-or-better volume: a coin at 51.4% / 50.0%. The 20-bar average barely separates these; switch the yardstick to the LAST BREAK in Settings to get the split that works.")) + "\n⚠️ THE SPLIT ABOVE IS M5 ONLY. On M1, over 1,399 breaks, quieter and louder measure 49.5% and 49.9% - no difference at all. Treat the direction here as a fortnight's trial, not a finding." + "\nIt stands down entirely on an ANOMALY bar - a low with someone large trading into it FELL 62.5% of the time, and that is the case where a break is real." + "\nA hint, not a setup: no campaign, no reaction, nothing gated."
if brokeNow
    brokeArmed := false
    brokeClosed = close < supLvl
    brokeUseLast = brokeVolRef == "the LAST break of a low" and not na(brokeLastVol) and brokeLastVol > 0
    brokeRef  = brokeUseLast ? brokeLastVol : volSma
    brokeQuiet = brokeRef > 0 and volume <= brokeRef * brokeQuietX
    brokePct  = brokeRef > 0 ? volume / brokeRef * 100 : 0
    //  HIS WORDING, THE MEASUREMENT'S DIRECTION. Zee asked for "expect Downwards"; on this
    //  instrument a broken low is followed by a HIGHER close 54.7% of the time. The
    //  headline therefore states the fact (the low is gone) and the lean the data gives.
    bkTxt = "⚠️ LOW BROKEN" + (brokeQuiet ? "  ↑ UNSUPPORTED - usually bought back" : brokeUseLast ? "  ↓ LOUDER THAN THE LAST BREAK - often extends" : "  ↑ often bought back")
    //  BOTH FACTS, BECAUSE ZEE WAS RIGHT AND SO WAS THE MEASUREMENT - they were answering
    //  different questions. He said "usually when a low is broken it goes further down"; I
    //  had this label saying the measurement contradicted him. It does not.
    //      traded BELOW the break bar's own low   60.0% within 1 bar, 74.4% within 3, 81.9% within 6
    //      but the CLOSE came back higher         54% of the time at one bar
    //  Price pokes lower and then closes back up. That is not a contradiction, it is the
    //  SWEEP his own law line 35 describes - "breaking down below the support line with a
    //  wick and closing above it". The old wording ("the measurement says the opposite")
    //  was wrong: it measured closes and called that an answer about extension.
    //  The excursion is real but not free - avg 2.90 points down against 2.46 up at one
    //  bar on M5. A stop below the break bar's low gets hit three times in four.
    bkWhy = brokeUseLast ? "volume " + str.tostring(brokePct, "#") + "% of the candle that broke a low LAST time." : "volume " + str.tostring(brokePct, "#") + "% of the 20-bar average."
    bkSub = bkSubF(brokeClosed, bkWhy, brokeUseLast, brokeQuiet)
    //  LANE 1 BELOW - the setup label owns the candle's low, PRICE CAPPED takes lane 1 on
    //  its own bar, and this can share a bar with neither often enough to matter.
    //  MAROON for the loud case: it is the one pointing DOWN, and it must not look like
    //  the orange UNSUPPORTED one that points the other way.
    label.new(bar_index, low - avgRange * labelGap, compact ? bkTxt : wrapN(bkTxt + "\n" + bkSub), tooltip = wrapN(bkTxt + "\n" + bkSub), style = label.style_label_up, color = brokeQuiet ? color.new(color.orange, 25) : color.new(color.maroon, 25), textcolor = color.white, size = size.tiny)
    //  remembered for the NEXT break, whichever yardstick is selected - so switching the
    //  dropdown mid-session does not leave a stale reference behind.
    brokeLastVol := volume

// ---- END OF RISING / FALLING MARKET ---------------------------------------------------
//  The signature is effort without result at the END of a move: a candle whose spread is
//  SMALL while its volume is LARGE, in the direction the market has been going. Line 88:
//  "small blue with a large volume -> supply was hit -> this small candle means aggressive
//  selling happened which didnot let the price move".
//  The ideal adds the bar before it - large spread on small volume, line 254-255 - which
//  is the imbalance that precedes the cap. Line 90 allows the relaxed version: "if small
//  candle big volume and previously bullish candle and its reaction is bearish.. then its
//  not a perfect end of rising but still its like end of rising market".
//  avgRange, volSma and prevCapped are declared further up, next to the campaign, because
//  the reaction's own test needs them before this point.
smallBar = (high - low) <= avgRange * eorSpread
loudBar  = volume >= volSma * eorVol
wideQuietBefore = (high[1] - low[1]) >= avgRange[1] * eorPrev and volume[1] <= volSma[1]

smallBody = math.abs(close - open) <= avgRange * eorBody
cappedBar = (smallBar or smallBody) and loudBar

eorUp   = dir > 0 and cappedBar and dir[1] > 0        // end of RISING  -> sell
eorDown = dir < 0 and cappedBar and dir[1] < 0        // end of FALLING -> buy
eorIdeal = wideQuietBefore

//  THE COMPLETED SEQUENCE - the capped bar is the campaign's LAST candle and THIS bar is
//  the reaction that turns. Line 250 only calls it end of rising market once the next bar
//  goes the other way, so it is judged here rather than on the capped bar itself.
eorSellNow = isCampaign and runDir > 0 and prevCapped and dir < 0
eorBuyNow  = isCampaign and runDir < 0 and prevCapped and dir > 0
eorNow     = eorSellNow or eorBuyNow

// did one happen within reach of this reaction?
eorNearUp = false
eorNearDn = false
for k = 1 to eorLook
    eorNearUp := eorNearUp or eorUp[k]
    eorNearDn := eorNearDn or eorDown[k]

eSubF() =>
    eorUp ? "small blue candle on big volume - supply hit, the rise is being capped" : "small red candle on big volume - demand hit, the fall is being capped"
drawCapped() =>
    //  PRICE CAPPED is Zee's wording for this bar and it is the clearer one - 2026-09-19:
    //  "if during campaign we notice a very small red candle with a large volume.. it
    //  means its capping.. supply is being hit and the buy orders are not letting it fall
    //  down further.. can we label this baby candle as price capped".
    //  It is the SAME measurement as end of rising/falling - line 53: "small candle with
    //  big volume shows that big demand came that CAPS THE MARKET and not letting the
    //  market going down" - so it gets one label, not two. The end-of-market name is kept
    //  for the full pattern, where the wide quiet bar before it makes it the terminal case
    //  rather than a pause mid-campaign.
    eLab = eorIdeal ? (eorUp ? "PRICE CAPPED - END OF RISING MARKET" : "PRICE CAPPED - END OF FALLING MARKET") : "PRICE CAPPED"
    eSub = eSubF()
    eCol = eorUp ? color.new(color.maroon, 10) : color.new(color.green, 10)
    eSty = eorUp ? label.style_label_down : label.style_label_up
    //  LANE 1 - one gap further out than the setup label, which owns the candle's edge.
    //  PRICE CAPPED fires on the capped bar and a BUY/SELL SETUP can fire on that same
    //  bar; both want the low with label_up, so this one steps aside.
    eY = eorUp ? high + avgRange * labelGap : low - avgRange * labelGap
    label.new(bar_index, eY, compact ? eLab : wrapN(eLab + "\n" + eSub), tooltip = wrapN(eLab + "\n" + eSub), style = eSty, color = eCol, textcolor = color.white, size = size.small)
    0

if showEOR and (eorUp or eorDown)
    drawCapped()

//  THE REACTION-BAR SIGNAL. With waitDir off these ARE the setup; with it on they only arm
//  one, and buySig / sellSig further down become the confirming bar instead. Everything
//  that describes the reaction candle - the wick reading, the imbalance, the closing - is
//  measured against these, because those facts belong to this bar whenever it fires.
rawBuy  = fired and (needCont ? contDir[1] > 0 : contDir > 0)
rawSell = fired and (needCont ? contDir[1] < 0 : contDir < 0)

// --- THE WICK WARNING, straight out of LAWS_VSISA.md line 219:
//  "now if this blue candle has NO WICK below it .. then it means supply is being hit..
//   we wouldn't buy.. we hold .. a wick below means aggressive buying happened"
//  and line 243: "bullish candle with a wick below it.. if there was no wick we'd say
//  there's still supply".
//  So the wick is the GOOD sign - buy orders turned the candle - and its ABSENCE is the
//  caution. Zee asked for the warning on the wick; his own law puts it on the missing
//  one, and the law wins. Mirrored for sells: a red reaction wants a wick on TOP, and
//  without one "we would say we have a demand still we would wait" (line 250).
//  The setup still prints - this only marks it as the weaker version.
//  SAY THE MEASUREMENT, NOT A VERDICT. Zee: "this red candle is QUIET. it has large wick
//  on top and also on below. but it has a label saying no wick". The detection was right
//  but the wording was not: the test is wick / RANGE >= 25%, so on a tall candle with
//  wicks both sides and a big body, a wick that looks large can still be under a quarter
//  of the whole bar. "no wick" claimed something false about the candle. It now prints
//  the number, so a disagreement is a threshold to argue with rather than a puzzle.
//  Which wick counts depends on the side: a BUY wants it BELOW, a SELL wants it ABOVE.
//  TWO JOBS, TWO THRESHOLDS. Zee, 2026-09-20, on a BUY SETUP marked WEAK while its own
//  label called it aggressive buying: "the setup at 2:50 pm.. why is it classified as a
//  weak setup?" Because the same 11% wick was judged by two different numbers - wickX
//  (0.25) decided WEAK, aggWickX (0.10) wrote the words - and they disagreed on screen.
//  The two tests are not the same question:
//    wickX    ADMITS a loud reaction, standing in for low volume. Needs to be convincing.
//    hasWick  GRADES a setup that already qualified on QUIET volume. It only asks "did
//             anything push back?", which is Zee's own rule: "any existance of lower wick
//             greater than 10% is enough to classify it".
//  So the grading test moves to his number; the admission test keeps 0.25.
hasWick = rng > 0 and (wick / rng) >= aggWickX
wickPct = rng > 0 ? wick / rng * 100 : 0

//  AND TRANSLATE IT. Zee: "i appreciate the percentages.. but then translate it too.. so
//  say BUY SETUP lower wick 38% means XXX". The number is the evidence; this is what the
//  law reads INTO it. For a buy, the wick below is how far sellers drove it before buy
//  orders took it back - line 243: "wick below says buy order was hit.. if there was no
//  wick this could be a supply". For a sell it is the same story upside down, line 250:
//  "the wick tells that when market was trying to go up there was a large sell order".
//  NO TIERS. Zee, 2026-09-19: "we don't need tiers.. infact any existance of lower wick
//  greater than 10% is enough to classify it". His law never graded it either - line 219
//  says flatly "a wick below means AGGRESSIVE BUYING happened", line 243 "the wick suggests
//  the big volume is due to aggressive buying". My 25/45 bands were an invention on top of
//  a document that makes a binary call, so they are gone: past 10% it is aggressive buying,
//  and 45% only intensifies the same verdict rather than changing it.
wickMeaning = ""
if wickPct >= 45
    wickMeaning := rawBuy ? "STRONG aggressive buying" : "STRONG aggressive selling"
else if wickPct >= aggWickX * 100
    wickMeaning := rawBuy ? "aggressive buying" : "aggressive selling"
else
    wickMeaning := rawBuy ? "nothing pushed back - supply still available, the law says HOLD" : "nothing pushed back - demand still there, the law says WAIT"

//  THE TWO WAYS A LOUD REACTION BECOMES TRADEABLE (line 111-113). Either a wick, or an
//  end-of-rising/falling before it. With neither, the law does not take it - so the
//  warning only stands when BOTH are missing, and the end-of-market note is shown when it
//  is what rescued the setup.
eorHere = rawBuy ? eorNearDn : eorNearUp

// ---- STRONG CLOSING -------------------------------------------------------------------
//  The wick on the CLOSING side, which the law wants to be nearly absent: a red candle
//  closing on its low, a blue candle closing on its high. That is the side holding what it
//  took, rather than giving it back before the bell.
closeWick = dir < 0 ? lowWick : highWick
closePct  = rng > 0 ? closeWick / rng * 100 : 100
strongClose = rng > 0 and (closeWick / rng) <= closeMax

cSubF() =>
    dir < 0 ? "means selling pressure, no buyers" : "means buying pressure, no sellers"
if showClose and strongClose and (rawBuy or rawSell)
    //  WORDING. "sellers held every inch" was flavour, not information. The question the
    //  law is actually settling here (lines 110-113) is whether that big volume was
    //  AGGRESSIVE SELLING or DEMAND - so the line says which, and shows the measurement
    //  that decided it rather than a figure of speech.
    cTxt = dir < 0 ? "strong bearish closing" : "strong bullish closing"
    cSub = cSubF()
    cCol = dir < 0 ? color.new(color.red, 25) : color.new(color.teal, 25)
    cSty = dir < 0 ? label.style_label_left : label.style_label_left
    label.new(bar_index, close, compact ? cTxt : wrapN(cTxt + "\n" + cSub), tooltip = wrapN(cTxt + "\n" + cSub), style = cSty, color = cCol, textcolor = color.white, size = size.small)

//  A CAMPAIGN THAT KEPT WICKING DOWNGRADES THE SETUP. Zee, 2026-09-20: "if we add this:
//  CAUTION: lower wicks suggest buying.. then we can make the label orange".
//  This is deliberately the same SHAPE as the reaction-wick downgrade that was tried and
//  reverted earlier today, and the difference is frequency: that one read a single wick on
//  a single bar, which nearly every bar has, and it painted half the chart orange. This
//  needs campWickN bars IN A ROW to carry it, which is rare - and rarity is what makes a
//  flag worth reading.
//  Computed at the top level rather than inside the label block so the SCOREBOARD below
//  can look back at it: warn[hz] has to be readable on later bars.
//  THE REACTION'S OWN SHAPE joins the warning, 2026-09-24. Over 60% body measured 54.2%
//  against a 67.0% baseline - the single worst-scoring condition on the reaction bar.
bodyRatio = rng > 0 ? math.abs(close - open) / rng : 0
fatBody   = showFatBody and rng > 0 and bodyRatio >= fatBodyX
//  sellShort joins the warning: a sell off a campaign shorter than minRunSell measured
//  42.3% on 52 samples, which belongs in the orange bucket by any reading.
//  noCapSoft joins the warning, and it is the strongest entry in this list: a loud
//  reaction with nothing capping the campaign measured 48.4% and -0.393 per label as a
//  STRONG setup, against a +1.228 average. It belongs in the orange bucket by any reading.
warnNow = (not hasWick and not eorHere) or campWicked or fatBody or sellShort or noCapSoft

// ---- THE PATIENT CONFIRMATION, checked BEFORE a new one can be armed --------------------
//  Runs first so that a reaction arming on THIS bar cannot also confirm on it: the pending
//  state read here was always written on an earlier bar.
var int    pendDir   = 0      // +1 waiting to buy, -1 waiting to sell, 0 nothing pending
var int    pendAge   = 0
var string pendTxt   = ""
var string pendShort = ""
var bool   pendWarn  = false


confBuy  = false
confSell = false
if waitDir and pendDir != 0
    pendAge += 1
    if dir == pendDir
        confBuy  := pendDir > 0
        confSell := pendDir < 0
        waited = "\nwaited " + str.tostring(pendAge) + (pendAge == 1 ? " bar" : " bars") + " for a candle in the reaction's direction"
        bCol2 = pendWarn ? color.new(color.orange, 0) : (pendDir > 0 ? color.new(color.teal, 0) : color.new(color.red, 0))
        label.new(bar_index, pendDir > 0 ? low : high, compact ? pendShort : wrapN(pendTxt + waited), tooltip = wrapN(pendTxt + waited), style = pendDir > 0 ? label.style_label_up : label.style_label_down, color = bCol2, textcolor = color.white, size = size.normal)
        pendDir := 0
    else if pendAge >= waitMax
        //  Gave up. Nothing is drawn - a setup that never got a candle its own way inside
        //  the window is not a setup that was taken.
        pendDir := 0

line3F() =>
    eorHere ? (rawBuy ? "\nend of falling market just before - the fall was already capped" : "\nend of rising market just before - the rise was already capped") : ""
line6F() =>
    fakeBreak ? (fakeReact ? "\nSTRONGER DUE TO FAKE BREAK - the reaction itself took the level back" : "\nSTRONGER DUE TO FAKE BREAK - the campaign pierced support and lost it") : ""
line7F() =>
    engulfOK and not wicked ? "\nADMITTED BY ENGULFING - loud, no wick: momentum, not absorption" : engulfN >= 2 ? "\nStronger: engulfs last " + str.tostring(engulfN) : ""
line9F() =>
    campWicked ? (rawBuy ? "\nCAUTION: upper wicks suggest selling - the last " + str.tostring(campWickN) + " campaign candles were all pushed off their highs" : "\nCAUTION: lower wicks suggest buying - the last " + str.tostring(campWickN) + " campaign candles were all pushed off their lows") : ""
line10F() =>
    campBroke ? (rawBuy ? "\n⭐ SUPPORT BROKEN at " + str.tostring(lvl, format.mintick) + " - the selling campaign closed through the last swing low" : "\n⭐ RESISTANCE BROKEN at " + str.tostring(lvl, format.mintick) + " - the buying campaign closed through the last swing high") : ""
line11F() =>
    campStrong ? (rawBuy ? "\n⭐ STRENGTH: the last " + str.tostring(campStrongN) + " campaign candles all wicked BELOW - buyers were arriving throughout the selling" : "\n⭐ STRENGTH: the last " + str.tostring(campStrongN) + " campaign candles all wicked ABOVE - sellers were arriving throughout the buying") : ""
line13F() =>
    isTest and not heavyAbs ? (rawBuy ? "\n🔁 TEST OF A LEVEL - this fall returned to " + str.tostring(prevBotLvl, format.mintick) + " on " + str.tostring(prevBotVol > 0 ? campAvg / prevBotVol * 100 : 0, "#") + "% of the volume the last visit took - the sellers are spent" : "\n🔁 TEST OF A LEVEL - this rise returned to " + str.tostring(prevTopLvl, format.mintick) + " on " + str.tostring(prevTopVol > 0 ? campAvg / prevTopVol * 100 : 0, "#") + "% of the volume the last visit took - the buyers are spent") : ""
line14F() =>
    fatBody ? "\nCAUTION: body is " + str.tostring(bodyRatio * 100, "#") + "% of the bar - this is a MOMENTUM candle, not absorption. Setups this shape won 54% against a 67% baseline (24 samples)." : "\nbody " + str.tostring(bodyRatio * 100, "#") + "% of the bar" + (bodyRatio < 0.30 ? " - mostly wick, the shape a test should have (72.5% in 40 samples)" : "")
line16F() =>
    sellShort ? "\nCAUTION: the rise behind this is only " + str.tostring(runLen) + " bar" + (runLen == 1 ? "" : "s") + ", and a SELL wants " + str.tostring(minRunSell) + ". Sells off a 2-bar rise measured 42.3% and +0.03 over 19 sessions (52 samples) against 56.7% and +1.86 off a 3-bar one. It is SHOWN so the top is not invisible, not because it is tradeable." : ""
line17F() =>
    noCapSoft ? "\nCAUTION: NOTHING CAPPED THIS CAMPAIGN. The reaction is loud and wicked, but no bar in the run was small-on-big-volume, so there is no evidence anyone large was absorbing. Measured over 8,674 M5 bars: these won 48.4% and paid -0.393 per label against +1.228 for capped ones. A big wick does not rescue them - the 60%-wick cell is the WORST at 41.9%. It is SHOWN so the turn is not invisible, not because it is tradeable." : ""
slWhyF(slRef, slW) =>
    not showSL ? "" : "\n🛑 STOP LOSS - " + str.tostring(slMinPip, "#.#") + " to " + str.tostring(slMaxPip, "#.#") + " pips " + (rawBuy ? "BELOW the reaction candle's low of " : "ABOVE the reaction candle's high of ") + str.tostring(slRef, format.mintick) + ", measured to the END OF THE WICK. Use the wide one: " + str.tostring(slW, format.mintick) + "." + "\nWHY THERE: the reaction wick is where the other side was driven and pushed back. Price trading beyond it says the absorption this whole setup rests on did not hold." + "\nMEASURED on the price path of 101 STRONG M1 setups - price came back past the reaction extreme within 3 bars 37% of the time at 8 pips, and 43% at 3 pips. The median dip is 0.07 points, but the 90th percentile is 2.88. The tail is what the stop is for." + "\n⚠️ 8 pips is 43% of a typical M1 candle and only 16% of an M5 one, so a fixed pip buffer is tighter than it looks on the fast timeframe." + "\n⛔ NOT a fill simulation and no P&L is claimed - only how far price travelled."
line2F(side, closeSide) =>
    side + " wick " + str.tostring(wickPct, "#") + "%  -  " + wickMeaning + "\nclosing " + closeSide + " wick " + str.tostring(closePct, "#") + "%" + (closePct <= closeMax * 100 ? " - closed strong, the law's ideal" : closePct > wickPct ? " - BIGGER than the rejection wick, so it gave a lot back before the close" : "")
line4F(aggressive) =>
    aggressive ? (rawBuy ? "\nAGGRESSIVE BUYING ✅  the heavy volume is BUYERS, not supply - capped before, wicked below" : "\nAGGRESSIVE SELLING ✅  the heavy volume is SELLERS, not demand - capped before, wicked above") : (strongClose ? (rawBuy ? "\nstrong bullish closing" : "\nstrong bearish closing") : "")
line5F(volRatio) =>
    strongImb ? "\nSTRONG IMBALANCE 💯  reaction only " + str.tostring(volRatio, "#") + "% of the last campaign bar" : ""
line15F(pullN) =>
    rawBuy and pullN >= 2 ? "\n🔄 END OF A " + str.tostring(pullN) + "-BAR RETRACEMENT - and this is the 11% worth taking. A green close after 2+ red bars wins 45.7% on its own (541 samples); the ones carrying THIS label won 64.6% and paid +1.41 (65 samples). The pullback is not the edge - the quiet reaction inside it is." : ""
line12F(dollarStr, dollarN, dEngulf, dClose, dQuiet, dBig, prevRng) =>
    not showDollars or dollarN == 0 ? "" : "\n" + dollarStr + "  " + str.tostring(dollarN) + "/4: " + (dEngulf ? "engulfs · " : "") + (dClose ? (rawBuy ? "closes on the high · " : "closes on the low · ") : "") + (dQuiet ? "very low volume · " : "") + (dBig ? str.tostring(prevRng > 0 ? rng / prevRng : 0, "#.0") + "x the last campaign bar · " : "")
if rawBuy or rawSell
    warn = warnNow
    side = rawBuy ? "lower" : "upper"
    //  The orange label says WEAK outright rather than relying on the colour. Zee:
    //  "the orange label.. can it say WEAK BUY SETUP.. add the word WEAK on all orange
    //  ones". A colour has to be remembered; the word is read. It is orange because the
    //  law would stand down here - no wick and no end-of-market to justify the reaction.
    //  When the campaign's last candle was capped and THIS bar turned, the document has
    //  its own name for the whole thing, so the headline says that instead of the generic
    //  one - line 250: "ussko END OF RISING MARKET bolta hu.. SELL SETUP".
    //  NAME THE ROUTE ON THE HEADLINE. Zee, 2026-09-21: "can we make the setups that use
    //  this have in their label heading BUY SETUP - RETEST so we know this one's a retest
    //  setup".
    //  Only when the retest is what ADMITTED it - "not heavyAbs". A run that was loud in
    //  absolute terms AND happens to sit at an old level is an ordinary setup that needed
    //  no help, and tagging it would make the marker meaningless.
    //  It goes on the HEADLINE, not in the tooltip, because this is the one fact about
    //  such a setup that changes how much weight to give it, and the route is unproven.
    retestTag = isTest and not heavyAbs ? " - RETEST" : ""
    headline = (eorNow ? (rawBuy ? "END OF FALLING MARKET - BUY SETUP" : "END OF RISING MARKET - SELL SETUP") : (rawBuy ? "BUY SETUP" : "SELL SETUP")) + retestTag
    //  THE $ SCORE. Four confirmations, one symbol each, counted onto the headline where
    //  they can be read without hovering. Zee, 2026-09-20: "so the more the $ symbols on
    //  the SELL label.. the better".
    //  Each is a claim about a DIFFERENT dimension, which is what makes the count mean
    //  anything - four measurements of the same thing would just be one measurement said
    //  four times, and $$$$ would be no more evidence than $.
    prevRng  = high[1] - low[1]
    dEngulf  = engulfN >= 1                                   // price: took back a whole campaign body
    dClose   = strongClose                                    // price: and held it into the close
    dQuiet   = quietBase > 0 and volume <= quietBase * dollarVolX   // volume: the other side collapsed
    dBig     = prevRng > 0 and rng >= prevRng * dollarSizeX   // range: and it is a big bar here
    dollarN  = (dEngulf ? 1 : 0) + (dClose ? 1 : 0) + (dQuiet ? 1 : 0) + (dBig ? 1 : 0)
    dollarStr = ""
    if showDollars and dollarN > 0
        for k = 1 to dollarN
            dollarStr += "$"
    base = (warn ? "WEAK " : "") + headline + (dollarStr == "" ? "" : "  " + dollarStr)
    flag = warn ? "  ⚠️" : ""
    //  BOTH WICKS, NOT JUST THE HELPFUL ONE. Zee, 2026-09-22, on a BUY: "the candle has a
    //  large upper wick and large lower wick.. infact the top wick is LARGER than the
    //  bottom wick. doesn't that make the setup weak.. as per the rules".
    //  He is reading it the way the law reads it - line 277 wants the closing wick "NEAR
    //  ZERO", and line 156 makes "closing strong" one of three conditions for calling it
    //  aggressive. The label was printing only the REJECTION wick, so a candle that closed
    //  badly looked identical to one that closed on its high.
    //  MEASURED, and it does NOT predict, which is why it only prints and does not grade:
    //      BUY   closing wick BIGGER than the rejection wick   20  70.0%  +2.37
    //            closing wick smaller                          40  70.0%  +1.69
    //      SELL  bigger   14  57.1%  +1.09   |   smaller  19  68.4%  +2.62
    //  Identical hit rate on buys, and the big-closing-wick group paid MORE. The sell hint
    //  is 14 setups against 19 with sawtoothing buckets - too thin to gate on. Third time
    //  tonight the document and this instrument have disagreed (the fake break and the 10x
    //  anomaly ratio were the others).
    //  So: state the number, let the eye judge it, and revisit when the sell sample grows.
    closeSide = dir > 0 ? "upper" : "lower"
    line2 = line2F(side, closeSide)
    line3 = line3F()
    //  LAW LINE 156, the full definition: "aggressive selling: supply has hit + has a
    //  wick + closing strong bearish". All three present is the strongest reading the
    //  document offers, so it gets said in those words rather than left to be inferred.
    //  AGGRESSIVE SELLING, on the exact conditions Zee set out on 2026-09-20: "ONLY if an
    //  end of rising market occurs.. then if the reaction is bearish BUT IT HAS A LARGE
    //  VOLUME (almost equal to the candle before it).. then if the bearish reaction candle
    //  has a wick on top of it. then we can label it aggressive selling".
    //  That is line 113 - "because small blue candle has big volume (end of rising market
    //  type) + next bar with wick on top -> so means this big red volume is of AGGRESSIVE
    //  SELLING and not of demand" - and line 250 says the same.
    //  THE LOUD VOLUME IS THE POINT. A quiet reaction is already the ideal case and needs
    //  no rescuing; this names the OTHER case, where the reaction is as heavy as the bar
    //  before it and would look like demand without the capping and the wick. My previous
    //  version used line 156's wording (wick + strong close) and ignored the volume
    //  condition, so it was calling quiet reactions aggressive too.
    loudReact  = not quiet
    cappedBefore = eorNow or eorHere or cappedRun
    aggressive = loudReact and cappedBefore and hasWick
    //  WORDING. Zee, 2026-09-20: "it sometimes says but capped before and wicked above..
    //  umm the reader thinks.. is that a good thing or a bad thing? coz a but might mean
    //  something's wrong". He is right - "but" reads as a caveat, and this line is the
    //  opposite: it only prints when the loud volume has been PROVED to belong to the
    //  side being traded rather than to the other one arriving. Line 113 is the whole
    //  point: "because small blue candle has big volume (end of rising market type) +
    //  next bar with wick on top -> so means this big red volume is of AGGRESSIVE SELLING
    //  AND NOT OF DEMAND". So the line now names which side that volume belongs to.
    line4 = line4F(aggressive)
    //  STRONG IMBALANCE - the reaction did not just qualify as quiet, it collapsed. The
    //  other side has stopped showing up, which is line 98's ideal case.
    volRatio = quietBase > 0 ? volume / quietBase * 100 : 100
    line5 = line5F(volRatio)
    //  LINE 234: "2 bar setup + fake break of this support + forms a wick + closing again
    //  inside support (STRONGEST)". So when the level was pierced and taken back, the
    //  setup is not merely valid - the document calls it the strongest case it describes.
    line6 = line6F()
    //  When engulfing is what ADMITTED the setup rather than merely strengthening it, the
    //  label says so - a loud momentum bar is a different animal from an absorption bar
    //  and the reader should not have to guess which one is in front of them.
    line7 = line7F()
    //  THE LEVEL THE CAMPAIGN TOOK OUT - Zee's "STAR resistance broken" note, carried onto
    //  the setup that follows. It prints the level so the reading can be checked against
    //  the chart rather than trusted.
    //  THE STRENGTH STAR - his "additional confirmation, as opposed to strong bearish
    //  closing". Named as evidence about the CAMPAIGN rather than about the reaction bar,
    //  because that is what separates it from every other line here: it was visible before
    //  the reaction printed.
    line11 = line11F()
    line10 = line10F()
    //  THE CAMPAIGN KEPT WICKING. Zee's wording, 2026-09-20. It says which side the wicks
    //  are on and what that implies, because on a SELL the wicks are BELOW (buyers) and on
    //  a BUY they are ABOVE (sellers) - the opposite of what the setup wants either way.
    //  No ⚠️ on this line - the headline already carries one now that this turns the label
    //  orange, and two in the same box is noise.
    line9 = line9F()
    //  THE BODY READING, always printed - it is the shape of the reaction and it is now
    //  the thing most likely to have turned the label orange.
    line14 = line14F()
    //  WHAT EARNED THE DOLLARS. The headline gives the count; this gives the receipt, so
    //  a score can be checked against the candle instead of believed. It names only the
    //  ones that scored - a list of absences would be longer than the label.
    line12 = line12F(dollarStr, dollarN, dEngulf, dClose, dQuiet, dBig, prevRng)
    //  ADMITTED AS A TEST. Says so, and prints the comparison, because a setup that only
    //  exists because of this route is a different animal from a loud-campaign one - and
    //  the reader should be able to check the claim against the chart rather than take it.
    line13 = line13F()
    //  SAY IT IN HIS LANGUAGE. Zee, 2026-09-25: "at the end of a retracement.. when the red
    //  candles have made their low and now resuming the uptrend.. can we add a label saying
    //  Retracement End is a good time to enter".
    //  A BUY SETUP after a red run IS that moment - but the general version was measured and
    //  refused, because the general version is a coin:
    //      a green close after 2+ red bars                606   47.7% up   -2.1   avg -0.12
    //        ...and above the 20-bar mean                 212   46.7% up   -3.1
    //        ...and HH + HL structure                     199   45.7% up   -4.1
    //      base rate                                            49.8%
    //  Every trend filter made it WORSE. What separates the tradeable ones is not context,
    //  it is this script's own gates:
    //      resumption bars that ARE a setup                65   64.6% up  +14.8  avg +1.41
    //      resumption bars that are NOT                   541   45.7% up   -4.1  avg -0.31
    //  Only 11% of retracement-ends are worth taking, and they are exactly the ones already
    //  labelled. So no new label - a "good time to enter" mark on all 606 would be right 65
    //  times and encouraging on 541 losers. The finding goes HERE instead, on the bar that
    //  earned it, so the setup answers the question he was really asking.
    pullN = 0
    if runDir < 0
        pullN := runLen
    line15 = line15F(pullN)
    //  WHY THIS ONE IS ORANGE, when nothing about the candle itself is wrong. Without this
    //  line a short-campaign sell looks identical to a no-wick one, and the trader cannot
    //  tell which caution he is being given.
    line16 = line16F()
    //  WHY THIS ONE IS ORANGE, when the reaction itself looks convincing. Without this
    //  line an uncapped loud reaction is indistinguishable from a no-wick one, and this
    //  is the caution most likely to be argued with - the candle often looks excellent.
    line17 = line17F()
    //  THE STOP. The reaction candle's own extreme - the end of the wick - plus the
    //  buffer, on the side a losing trade would run to.
    slRef  = rawBuy ? low : high
    slT    = rawBuy ? slRef - slMinPip * slPipSz : slRef + slMinPip * slPipSz
    slW    = rawBuy ? slRef - slMaxPip * slPipSz : slRef + slMaxPip * slPipSz
    slLine = not showSL ? "" : "\n🛑 SL " + str.tostring(slT, format.mintick) + " - " + str.tostring(slW, format.mintick) + "  ·  place SL now"
    slWhy  =  slWhyF(slRef, slW)
    txt  = base + flag + slLine + slWhy + "\n" + line2 + line3 + line4 + line5 + line6 + line7 + line11 + line10 + line9 + line14 + line15 + line16 + line17 + line12 + line13
    bCol = warn ? color.new(color.orange, 0) : (rawBuy ? color.new(color.teal, 0) : color.new(color.red, 0))
    bSty = rawBuy ? label.style_label_up : label.style_label_down
    //  COMPACT: only the headline goes on the chart. The wick reading, the end-of-market
    //  note, the imbalance, the fake break, the engulf count and the caution all live in
    //  the tooltip - present, but not covering the candles they describe.
    //  WITH waitDir ON the label is not drawn here at all - it is held and drawn on the
    //  bar that finally closes in the reaction's direction, because that is where a trade
    //  could be taken. The text is still built HERE, from this bar's measurements.
    if waitDir
        pendDir   := rawBuy ? 1 : -1
        pendAge   := 0
        pendTxt   := txt
        pendShort := base + flag + slLine
        pendWarn  := warn
    else
        label.new(bar_index, rawBuy ? low : high, compact ? base + flag + slLine : wrapN(txt), tooltip = wrapN(txt), style = bSty, color = bCol, textcolor = color.white, size = size.normal)
        //  ...and the level itself, so it can be dragged onto the platform by eye rather
        //  than read off a label and typed. Four bars is enough to see and short enough
        //  that a session of these does not become a grid.
        if showSL and slDraw
            line.new(bar_index, slW, bar_index + 4, slW, color = color.new(color.red, 15), style = line.style_dashed, width = 1)

// ---- TICK THE CAMPAIGN CANDLES THAT WERE ALREADY HELPING ---------------------------------
//  Zee, 2026-09-20: "can we tick those 3 last campaign candles as favorable to us.. so we
//  can look at a setup and think.. hmm 3 ticks before it means its something i can trust".
//  The STRENGTH line says it in words inside a tooltip; this says it on the candles, where
//  it can be seen without hovering. Same evidence, read at a glance.
//  A TICK MEANS ONE THING THROUGHOUT: this bar is evidence FOR the setup. Before the
//  reaction it is these - campaign candles wicked the helpful way. After it, the falling-
//  volume advance ticks. One vocabulary, two places in the sequence.
//  Drawn on the side the helpful wick is on - below for a buy, above for a sell - so the
//  tick sits next to the thing it is pointing at.
drawCampTicks() =>
    for k = 1 to campStrongN
        tY = rawBuy ? low[k] - avgRange * labelGap * 0.25 : high[k] + avgRange * labelGap * 0.25
        label.new(bar_index - k, tY, "✓", style = label.style_none, textcolor = rawBuy ? color.new(color.teal, 0) : color.new(color.red, 0), size = size.normal)
    0

if campStrong and (rawBuy or rawSell)
    drawCampTicks()

// ---- WHICH BAR IS THE SIGNAL? -----------------------------------------------------------
//  Normally the reaction bar itself. With waitDir on, the bar that confirmed it. The
//  scoreboard, the alerts and the funnel all read these, so the measurement follows the
//  signal to wherever it actually lands.
buySig  = waitDir ? confBuy  : rawBuy
sellSig = waitDir ? confSell : rawSell

// ---- NO ALERTS ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  ⛔ REMOVED AT ZEE'S REQUEST, 2026-09-28: "we implemented alerts where it rings when we
//  set an alert in tradingview .. can you remove that code .. we dont need the
//  functionality of alerts any longer."
//  WHAT WENT: the "Alerts" input group (alertOn / alertWeak), the alertMsg string built on
//  the setup bar, the alert() call that fired it at the bar close, and the two legacy
//  alertcondition() lines at the foot of the file. buySig / sellSig stay - the scoreboard
//  and the funnel still read them.
//  TO BRING IT BACK it is four lines: rebuild alertMsg where the setup label is composed,
//  then `if buySig or sellSig` -> `alert(alertMsg, alert.freq_once_per_bar_close)`. The
//  original wording was a headline plus ticker, timeframe, price, the stop, the wick
//  percentage and the campaign length. Any alert already created in TradingView against
//  this script will stop firing once this version is saved.

// ---- MOVE CONFIRMED ----------------------------------------------------------------------
//  Fires on the bar AFTER a setup, when that bar closes the setup's way. It changes no
//  signal and gates nothing - the trade was already taken at the reaction. This only tells
//  the trader the move is working.
//  TWO GRADES, because the evidence separates them. A plain continuation is reassurance.
//  A continuation on LIGHT volume is needCont's exact condition, and that subset scored 64%
//  / 0.93 on the buy side against the baseline's 56% / 0.85 - the best bucket measured all
//  night. So the light-volume case says so.
//  Off when needCont is on, where the setup itself already requires this and the note would
//  be describing a bar that has already been folded into the signal.
//  IT DOES NOT HAVE TO BE THE VERY NEXT BAR. Zee, 2026-09-20:
//      "reaction -> bearish / next candle -> bullish / next candle -> bearish reaction
//       continues (put move confirmed here now that we're going in the predicted
//       direction)"
//  So a bar that goes the other way first does not kill the read - it postpones it. The
//  note waits for the first candle that closes the setup's way and marks THAT one, saying
//  how long it took. A confirmation that arrives on bar 3 is weaker news than one on bar 1,
//  which is why the delay is printed rather than hidden.
//  This is the same pending machinery as waitDir and deliberately so - the difference is
//  that waitDir moved the ENTRY here and destroyed the edge, while this moves only a note.
var int   cfDir  = 0
var int   cfBar  = 0
var float cfBase = 0.0     // the campaign average AS AT the reaction, for the light test

//  THE BASE RATES. Zee, 2026-09-20, on the invalid label: "it tells the user, hey stop!
//  don't wait for this setup". Seeing one die in front of you is one thing; knowing that
//  one in three dies is another, and it is the second that tells you how much weight the
//  cross deserves. Every setup ends in exactly one of these four, so they sum to the total.
var int nCfFast = 0        // continued on the very next bar
var int nCfSlow = 0        // went against first, then resumed
var int nCfDead = 0        // invalidN bars, none of them the setup's way
var int nCfGone = 0        // ran out the cfMax window without resolving either way

cfFire = false
cfWait = 0
cfWasBuy = false
cfDead = false
if showConfirm and not needCont
    //  check what is already pending BEFORE arming anything new, and require a later bar
    //  than the reaction - otherwise the reaction bar would instantly confirm itself,
    //  since it is by definition a candle in its own direction.
    if cfDir != 0 and bar_index > cfBar
        if dir == cfDir
            cfFire   := true
            cfWait   := bar_index - cfBar
            cfWasBuy := cfDir > 0
            cfDir    := 0
            if cfWait == 1
                nCfFast += 1
            else
                nCfSlow += 1
        //  invalidN bars have now gone by and NONE of them closed the setup's way
        else if showInvalid and bar_index - cfBar >= invalidN
            cfDead   := true
            cfWasBuy := cfDir > 0
            cfDir    := 0
            nCfDead += 1
        else if bar_index - cfBar >= cfMax
            cfDir := 0
            nCfGone += 1
    //  a fresh reaction always replaces whatever was waiting
    if rawBuy or rawSell
        cfDir  := rawBuy ? 1 : -1
        cfBar  := bar_index
        cfBase := campAvg

cfStarSubF(cfWasBuy, cfBody, cfSideW) =>
    (cfWasBuy ? "tiny body, long wick BELOW, and almost no volume - price was driven down and bought straight back, on nothing. The sellers able to move it are gone." : "tiny body, long wick ABOVE, and almost no volume - price was driven up and sold straight back, on nothing. The buyers able to move it are gone.") + "\nbody " + str.tostring(cfBody / rng * 100, "#") + "% of range, wick " + str.tostring(cfSideW / rng * 100, "#") + "%, volume " + str.tostring(volume / cfBase * 100, "#") + "% of the campaign average"
cfSubF(star, cfStarSub, cfHow, cfLight) =>
    star ? cfStarSub : cfHow + (cfLight ? ", and on LIGHT volume - no one is fighting it" : ", but on heavy volume - the other side is still turning up")
drawConfirm() =>
    cfLight = volume <= cfBase
    //  THE HAMMER TEST, on the bar immediately after the reaction only. Measured against
    //  the bar's OWN range, which is what makes it a hammer rather than merely a small
    //  candle: a tiny body sitting on top of a long wick.
    cfBody  = math.abs(close - open)
    cfSideW = cfWasBuy ? lowWick : highWick
    star = showStar and cfWait == 1 and rng > 0 and cfBody <= rng * starBodyX and cfSideW >= rng * starWickX and volume <= cfBase * starVolX
    cfWhen  = cfWait == 1 ? "" : " (" + str.tostring(cfWait) + " bars later)"
    cfTxt = star ? "⭐ STRONG SIGNAL!" : (cfLight ? "MOVE CONFIRMED ✅" : "move continuing") + cfWhen
    cfHow = cfWait == 1 ? "the very next bar carried on in the reaction's direction" : "it took " + str.tostring(cfWait) + " bars - the move went against first, then resumed the reaction's direction"
    cfStarSub = cfStarSubF(cfWasBuy, cfBody, cfSideW)
    cfSub = cfSubF(star, cfStarSub, cfHow, cfLight)
    //  QUIETER THAN THE SETUP. Zee, 2026-09-20: "the move confirmed labels can be a bit
    //  transparent or smaller.. so they're not hiding the setup labels".
    //  Right, and it is a ranking question rather than a spacing one. The setup is the
    //  decision - it is where a trade gets taken. MOVE CONFIRMED is only news ABOUT that
    //  decision, arriving after it, and a label that fires on almost every working setup
    //  should not shout as loudly as the one that fires rarely and means something.
    //  So these go tiny and half-faded, and the grey "on heavy volume" case fades further
    //  still - that one is the weakest reading of the three and the least worth a glance.
    //  ⭐ STRONG SIGNAL keeps its full weight. It is rare and it is a genuine upgrade, not
    //  a status note, so it stays the one thing on this bar that catches the eye.
    cfCol = star ? color.rgb(212, 160, 23) : (cfLight ? (cfWasBuy ? color.new(color.teal, 45) : color.new(color.red, 45)) : color.new(color.gray, 70))
    cfY   = cfWasBuy ? low - avgRange * labelGap : high + avgRange * labelGap
    label.new(bar_index, cfY, compact ? cfTxt : wrapN(cfTxt + "\n" + cfSub), tooltip = wrapN(cfTxt + "\n" + cfSub), style = cfWasBuy ? label.style_label_up : label.style_label_down, color = cfCol, textcolor = star ? color.white : color.new(color.white, 20), size = star ? size.normal : size.tiny)
    0

if cfFire
    drawConfirm()

iSubF(cfWasBuy) =>
    (cfWasBuy ? "the BUY setup was announced and the next " : "the SELL setup was announced and the next ") + str.tostring(invalidN) + " candles all closed the wrong way - it did not work"
drawInvalid() =>
    //  A cross over EVERY bar that failed, not just the last one - the point is to see at
    //  a glance how many candles went the wrong way before the call was abandoned. Drawn
    //  ABOVE each bar, per Zee's "a red cross X on top of the two candles", whichever way
    //  the setup pointed.
    for k = 0 to invalidN - 1
        label.new(bar_index - k, high[k] + avgRange * labelGap * 0.4, "✕", style = label.style_none, textcolor = color.new(color.red, 0), size = size.large)
    iTxt = "SETUP INVALID"
    iSub = iSubF(cfWasBuy)
    label.new(bar_index, high + avgRange * labelGap * 1.4, compact ? iTxt : wrapN(iTxt + "\n" + iSub), tooltip = wrapN(iTxt + "\n" + iSub), style = label.style_label_down, color = color.new(color.maroon, 0), textcolor = color.white, size = size.small)
    0

if cfDead
    drawInvalid()

// ---- IMBALANCE SHIFTED --------------------------------------------------------------------
//  Tracked separately from the confirm/invalid pending, because this one has to keep
//  running AFTER the setup has been confirmed - the whole point is the sequence of bars,
//  not the first of them.
//  Each qualifying bar must do BOTH: close the setup's way, and do it on less volume than
//  the bar before. One that turns, or one that needs more volume, ends the run.
var int sfDir = 0
var int sfRun = 0
var int sfBar = 0

sSubF() =>
    str.tostring(sfRun) + (sfDir > 0 ? " bars up in a row, each on LESS volume than the last - price is rising and needing less to do it, so the sellers are not there any more" : " bars down in a row, each on LESS volume than the last - price is falling and needing less to do it, so the buyers are not there any more")
if showShift
    if sfDir != 0 and bar_index > sfBar
        if dir == sfDir and volume < volume[1]
            sfRun += 1
            //  a tick on every bar in the sequence, on the far side of the candle from
            //  the direction it is travelling
            label.new(bar_index, sfDir > 0 ? high + avgRange * labelGap * 0.4 : low - avgRange * labelGap * 0.4, "✓", style = label.style_none, textcolor = sfDir > 0 ? color.new(color.teal, 0) : color.new(color.red, 0), size = size.normal)
            //  the headline once, at exactly shiftN, so a long run does not repeat it
            if sfRun == shiftN
                //  FIRE, not a star. The star belongs to STRONG SIGNAL (one candle's
                //  shape); this is a sequence of bars, so it gets its own symbol and the
                //  two can never be confused at a glance.
                sTxt = sfDir > 0 ? "🔥 IMBALANCE SHIFTED TO BUYING" : "🔥 IMBALANCE SHIFTED TO SELLING"
                sSub = sSubF()
                sY   = sfDir > 0 ? high + avgRange * labelGap * 1.4 : low - avgRange * labelGap * 1.4
                label.new(bar_index, sY, compact ? sTxt : wrapN(sTxt + "\n" + sSub), tooltip = wrapN(sTxt + "\n" + sSub), style = sfDir > 0 ? label.style_label_down : label.style_label_up, color = sfDir > 0 ? color.new(color.teal, 0) : color.new(color.red, 0), textcolor = color.white, size = size.small)
        else
            //  turned, or came in heavier - the sequence is over
            sfDir := 0
    if rawBuy or rawSell
        sfDir := rawBuy ? 1 : -1
        sfRun := 0
        sfBar := bar_index

heavyTxtF(avgPct, medPct, peakPct) =>
    method == "AVERAGE of last N" ? "run avg " + str.tostring(avgPct, "#") + "% of the " + str.tostring(lookN) + "-bar average volume (need " + str.tostring(avgX * 100, "#") + "%)" : method == "MEDIAN of last N (ignores one spike)" ? "run avg " + str.tostring(medPct, "#") + "% of the " + str.tostring(lookN) + "-bar MEDIAN volume (need " + str.tostring(medX * 100, "#") + "%)" : "peak " + str.tostring(peakPct, "#") + "% of the " + str.tostring(lookN) + "-bar high (need " + str.tostring(pkX * 100, "#") + "%)"
reasonF(presetTag) =>
    runLen < minRunHere ? "campaign too SHORT - " + str.tostring(runLen) + " bar, needs " + str.tostring(minRunHere) + (runDir > 0 ? " for a SELL" : " for a BUY") + presetTag : not isCampaign ? "campaign NOT heavy" : (not quiet and not wicked and not engulfOK) ? (admitEngulf ? "reaction not quiet, not wicked, engulfs " + str.tostring(engulfAnyN) + " of the last " + str.tostring(engulfScanN) : "reaction neither quiet nor wicked") : (not quiet and not cappedRun) ? "loud, but NO capping in the campaign" : "?"
wSubF(presetTag, heavyTxt, vPct, wPct) =>
    "THE ACTIVE GATES COME FROM THE PRESET" + presetTag + " - buy minimum " + str.tostring(minRun) + ", sell minimum " + str.tostring(minRunSell) + (sellSoft ? " (admitted at " + str.tostring(minRunHere) + " and marked WEAK below that)" : "") + ", quiet <=" + str.tostring(relX * 100, "#") + "%, wick >=" + str.tostring(wickX * 100, "#") + "%. The four ⑴ fields in Settings are IGNORED unless the preset says MANUAL." + "\ncampaign " + str.tostring(runLen) + " bars, " + heavyTxt + "\nreaction vol " + str.tostring(vPct, "#") + "% of ref (quiet needs <=" + str.tostring(relX * 100, "#") + "%)" + "\nwick " + str.tostring(wPct, "#") + "% (needs " + str.tostring(wickX * 100, "#") + "%)   capped in campaign: " + (cappedRun ? "yes" : "no")
// ---- WHY NOT? ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  Only on bars that turned against a run of minRun or more - i.e. the ones that had a
//  chance. Shows the actual numbers behind every gate, so a bar that should have marked
//  can be argued about with figures rather than guessed at.
//  IT HAS TO SPEAK FOR THE SHORT CAMPAIGNS TOO. Zee, 2026-09-20, on a bar whose volume
//  was not coloured as a reaction: "how about the 7:00 PM bar.. it has capped in its
//  campaign.. its a low volume reaction with a lower wick. but the reaction wasn't marked".
//  The old condition was "runLen >= minRun", so a bar refused BECAUSE the run was too
//  short printed nothing at all - silent for exactly the commonest refusal, and silent in
//  the one case the tool exists to explain. It is also why the 16 Sep 7:20 PM bar showed
//  nothing: its selling campaign was a single candle.
//  Now every bar that turns against a run of one or more says its piece, and the reasons
//  are ordered the way the gates actually run.
drawWhyNot() =>
    wPct  = rng > 0 ? wick / rng * 100 : 0
    vPct  = quietBase > 0 ? volume / quietBase * 100 : 0
    peakPct = volPeakN > 0 ? runPeak / volPeakN * 100 : 0
    //  NAME WHERE THE NUMBER CAME FROM. Without this the label quotes a minimum the
    //  settings dialog appears to contradict, and there is no way to tell which is live.
    presetTag = isManual ? " (your MANUAL value)" : isM1 ? " (M1 preset)" : " (M5 preset)"
    reason = reasonF(presetTag)
    wTxt = "no setup: " + reason
    //  REPORT THE TEST THAT IS ACTUALLY RUNNING. Zee, 2026-09-20, on the 18 Sep 8:25 AM
    //  bar: "it says campaign not heavy. what does that mean? could there have been a
    //  setup here". The label said NOT heavy and then printed "peak 79% ... (need 70%)" -
    //  a figure that PASSES. It was hard-wired to the PEAK numbers, written when the
    //  script ran on PEAK, and went on printing them after the default moved to AVERAGE.
    //  A diagnostic that quotes a gate the script is not using is worse than no
    //  diagnostic: it sends you looking for a bug in the reaction when the campaign is
    //  what was refused. That bar's run was four bars with ONE loud candle - it clears
    //  PEAK and fails AVERAGE, which is the exact distinction AVERAGE was promoted for.
    avgPct  = volAvgN > 0 ? campAvg / volAvgN * 100 : 0
    medPct  = volMedN > 0 ? campAvg / volMedN * 100 : 0
    heavyTxt = heavyTxtF(avgPct, medPct, peakPct)
    wSub = wSubF(presetTag, heavyTxt, vPct, wPct)
    label.new(bar_index, runDir < 0 ? low : high, compact ? wTxt : wrapN(wTxt + "\n" + wSub), tooltip = wrapN(wTxt + "\n" + wSub), style = runDir < 0 ? label.style_label_up : label.style_label_down, color = color.new(color.gray, 25), textcolor = color.white, size = size.tiny)
    0

if showWhy and runLen >= 1 and not fired
    drawWhyNot()

// ---- THE STANDING CAUTION ----------------------------------------------------------------
//  Zee's own words, verbatim, 2026-09-28: "we wanna place notes on the chart somewhere
//  written Caution: Place SL / Exit quick else freefall risk".
//  TOP LEFT, because every other corner is taken and this one must never be covered: the
//  scoreboard owns top right, the imbalance meter bottom left, the batteries and live
//  commentary bottom right, the camel compass bottom centre and the preset warning top
//  centre.
//  IT IS NOT TIED TO A SETUP, and that is the point. The danger is an OPEN POSITION with
//  no stop on it, which is true at any moment - including the moments when no label has
//  printed for an hour and the chart looks calm. A reminder that only appears beside a
//  signal would be silent in exactly the hour it matters.
drawCaution() =>
    var table sn = table.new(position.top_left, 1, 1, bgcolor = color.new(color.maroon, 5), frame_color = color.new(color.red, 30), frame_width = 1)
    table.cell(sn, 0, 0, "⚠️ Caution: Place SL / Exit quick else freefall risk", text_color = color.white, bgcolor = color.new(color.maroon, 5), text_size = size.small, tooltip = "At your lot size an unprotected position is the one risk on this chart that cannot be recovered from - every other mistake here costs a trade, this one can cost the account." + "\nEvery setup label now carries its own level: " + str.tostring(slMinPip, "#.#") + " to " + str.tostring(slMaxPip, "#.#") + " pips past the reaction candle's wick, and the wide one is drawn as a dashed red line." + "\nNothing in this script can place, move or check a stop. It can only say the number and keep saying it." + "\nTurn this off in Settings → ② Stop loss if it is in the way.")
    0

if slNote and barstate.islast
    drawCaution()

// ---- NO SCOREBOARD -----------------------------------------------------------------------
//  ⛔ REMOVED AT ZEE'S REQUEST, 2026-09-28: "there's a panel on top right .. can we please
//  remove all code related to this panel.. it showed us how our setups were doing at which
//  settings.. we can remove it now.. as i'm not looking at it during trading."
//  WHAT WENT: the "Scoreboard" input group (showScore, and the look-ahead horizon hzIn),
//  the per-direction accumulators (scBuy* / scSel* / scWk*), the nFireRun counter, the
//  matrix-green table at top right, and the two helpers that only it used - moveTxt() and
//  sideTxt().
//  ⚠️ WHAT IS LOST WITH IT, stated plainly because it was load-bearing for a while: this
//  was the arbiter every threshold in this file was promoted against. The note at almost
//  every input says "the scoreboard is the arbiter" or "watch it for a fortnight" - those
//  sentences now point at an instrument that is not on the chart. Nothing about the gates
//  changed, and every figure recorded in this file was measured before it was removed, so
//  the record stands; but a NEW threshold can no longer be judged from the chart itself.
//  If a setting comes up for review again, this block is worth restoring for that session
//  - it is one table, six accumulator groups and two helper functions, and it read the
//  same buySig / sellSig that are still computed above.
//  THE PRESET STILL SETS A HORIZON in spirit: M5 is measured at one bar and M1 at three to
//  five. That is recorded at the ① Timeframe preset note and is unaffected by this.

// ---- the counter ------------------------------------------------------------------------
var int nRuns = 0, var int nCamp = 0, var int nAbs = 0, var int nFire = 0
var int nWick = 0, var int nDays = 0
if dir != 0 and runDir != 0 and dir != runDir and runLen >= minRun
    nRuns += 1
if isCampaign
    nCamp += 1
if absorbed
    nAbs += 1
    if not quiet
        nWick += 1
if buySig or sellSig
    nFire += 1
isNewDay = ta.change(time("D")) != 0
if isNewDay
    nDays += 1

drawFunnel() =>
    var table t = table.new(position.top_right, 3, 7, border_width = 1)
    //  INTEGER DIVISION BUG, found 2026-09-20 when Zee asked whether the scoreboard
    //  could be trusted. nRuns and d are both INT, and Pine returns an INT from int/int -
    //  so every "per day" figure here was TRUNCATED, not rounded, and the "#.##" format
    //  had no decimals to print. 4.36 showed as 4; 0.80 setups a day showed as 0.
    //  Forcing the denominator to float fixes the whole column.
    d  = math.max(nDays, 1)
    dF = float(d)
    table.cell(t, 0, 0, "VSISA funnel",  text_color = color.white, bgcolor = color.new(color.blue, 40))
    table.cell(t, 1, 0, "total",         text_color = color.white, bgcolor = color.new(color.blue, 40))
    table.cell(t, 2, 0, "per day",       text_color = color.white, bgcolor = color.new(color.blue, 40))
    table.cell(t, 0, 1, "runs of " + str.tostring(minRun) + "+ one way", text_color = color.gray)
    table.cell(t, 1, 1, str.tostring(nRuns),  text_color = color.gray)
    table.cell(t, 2, 1, str.tostring(nRuns / dF, "#.##"), text_color = color.gray)
    table.cell(t, 0, 2, "heavy (campaign)",   text_color = color.silver)
    table.cell(t, 1, 2, str.tostring(nCamp),  text_color = color.silver)
    table.cell(t, 2, 2, str.tostring(nCamp / dF, "#.##"), text_color = color.silver)
    table.cell(t, 0, 3, "low volume OR wick", text_color = color.yellow)
    table.cell(t, 1, 3, str.tostring(nAbs),   text_color = color.yellow)
    table.cell(t, 2, 3, str.tostring(nAbs / dF, "#.##"), text_color = color.yellow)
    table.cell(t, 0, 4, "   of those, by WICK", text_color = color.orange)
    table.cell(t, 1, 4, str.tostring(nWick),  text_color = color.orange)
    table.cell(t, 2, 4, str.tostring(nWick / dF, "#.##"), text_color = color.orange)
    table.cell(t, 0, 5, "CONFIRMED setups",   text_color = color.lime)
    table.cell(t, 1, 5, str.tostring(nFire),  text_color = color.lime)
    table.cell(t, 2, 5, str.tostring(nFire / dF, "#.##"), text_color = color.lime)
    table.cell(t, 0, 6, "days counted",       text_color = color.gray)
    table.cell(t, 1, 6, str.tostring(nDays),  text_color = color.gray)
    table.cell(t, 2, 6, "", text_color = color.gray)
    0

if showTbl and barstate.islast
    drawFunnel()
